{"id":"infra","ord":"a0","title":"Infrastructure","desc":"Build system, CI, workspace plumbing. Norm inherited from the rewrite plan (git history has it): one PR per work item; every PR leaves the repo working and CI green.","changed":"2026-08-16T13:04:57-04:00"}
{"id":"scaffold","parent":"infra","ord":"a0","title":"Scaffold","done":"2026-08-09T13:06:54-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #56 (squash ee09573). flake + npins (kolu@4d8a668, nixpkgs), Bun, drishti-style workspace, bunfig.toml isolated linker, @kolu/* hydrate + check-kolu-deps scripts, justfile whose `check` recipe IS the CI DAG ([metadata(\"ci\")], odu-only — the planned GHA wrapper was dropped mid-flight at the human's direction), linux + darwin lanes, bun2nix from juspay/bun2nix#2. Effect wired in from day one: effect + @effect/platform-node at the kolu pin's exact version (4.0.0-beta.106) with single-copy overrides, and packages/core shipped a real @kolu/surface + Effect Schema cell exercised by bun test — that package is gone as of see-outline (PR #58), which split it into format/store/surface/server/web."}
{"id":"hm-module","parent":"infra","ord":"a1","title":"Home Manager module #racket-parity","done":"2026-08-10T09:55:14-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #64 (squash ea85270), authored by Grok, reviewed by Claude — the review empirically caught a clean-stop-lands-failed systemd bug (Effect exits 130 on interrupt; SuccessExitStatus now declares it clean) and a README snippet that did not evaluate. From the racket audit (2026-08-09): the racket flake shipped a home-manager module running serve as a systemd user service (Linux) / launchd agent (macOS) with dataDir/host/port options. Port it for `olai web`.","after":["see-outline"]}
{"id":"logging","parent":"infra","ord":"a2","title":"Proper logging #techdebt","done":"2026-08-10T14:55:28-04:00","desc":"Added 2026-08-10 after the chat-agent notice shipped as a bare console line ('no ACP agent configured … so chat is off'): log the way kolu does — Effect's structured logging throughout the server (levels, spans/annotations, one consistent format, quiet-by-default with a verbosity switch), replacing the ad-hoc options.log/console.log seam. Study kolu's logging setup at the pin and match its shape. Folded in from the sloppiness audit (2026-08-10), same seam: serve.ts's fault path calls process.exit(1) from a bare .catch, bypassing the scope finalizers the file itself documents as load-bearing (and that path is deliberately untested); listener.ts still renders a raw String(cause) one file from the Cause.pretty helper built to prevent it; OLAI_ALLOWED_ORIGINS is read inline with no named constant and no docs mention, unlike OLAI_DIST_DIR's exemplary pattern."}
{"id":"bun2nix-fork","parent":"infra","ord":"a4","title":"Drop the bun2nix fork #techdebt #upstream","done":"2026-08-10T12:13:40-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #67 (squash 232e774): upstream nix-community/bun2nix 2.1.2 turned out consumable without flake-parts; the fork input is gone, bun.nix equivalence proven by the bun-nix-fresh lane, the darwin fix gained, flake.nix header rewritten to tell the new truth. Filed 2026-08-10, a phase-1 watch item never itemized: the flake's ONE external input is juspay/bun2nix#2 (the rawflake shape), carried because upstream nix-community/bun2nix's nix layer was flake-parts-shaped and could not be consumed. Upstream has since shipped fixes the fork lags (2.1.2 carried a darwin nixpkgs fix at last check). Re-check upstream's shape; if consumable, re-point and delete the fork dependency — if not, PR upstream the rawflake shape and then re-point."}
{"id":"serve-surface-app","parent":"infra","ord":"a3","title":"Upstream: serveSurfaceApp #techdebt #upstream","done":"2026-08-10T16:49:24-04:00","desc":"SHIPPED 2026-08-10 as PR #78 — olai's half, and the debt is paid: pin past kolu #2137 AND #2144, listener.ts from 321 lines to one serveSurfaceApp call plus the two decisions the primitive leaves (whose port this is — the EADDRINUSE fallback — and what a listener event sounds like, SurfaceAppEvent mapped onto @olai/log: stale tab Info, refused cross-site origin Warn). Two things left rather than moved: the frame cap, now RPC_MAX_FRAME_BYTES inside the primitive with no option to move it, so #71 cannot recur; and the surface runtime's lifetime, which went the OTHER way, into serve.ts, which built it. listener.test.ts retargeted off the number onto the sink (real sockets, asserting on what the logger heard). Pin fallout, all bunfig-invariant rather than feature: @kolu/url-shape joins the hydrate list, ws moves to the root manifest and leaves @olai/server, check-kolu-deps.sh grew peerDependencies. architecture.md's 'Owed upstream' note now reads 'Paid upstream'. The park is what proved the fence: shutdown.test.ts caught the primitive's teardown hanging under bun (server.close's callback registered while a socket was open — node never sees it), that diagnosis went into kolu #2144, and the fence passes against the primitive now. DISPATCHED 2026-08-10 → Kolu terminal e500de76 (Claude/Opus); PARKED same day: the primitive's teardown hangs under bun (server.close registered while a socket is open — caught by olai's SIGINT fence), fix folded into the human's in-flight kolu listener-migration PR (kolu #2144); a re-pin past that merge unblocks. Kolu side SHIPPED 2026-08-10 as kolu PR #2137 (merged by the human): serveSurfaceApp in @kolu/surface-app owns origin gate → upgrade → stale-tab → heartbeat → serve, with the payload cap sourced from the framework constant (unspellable to undercut) and 15 e2e tests. What remains is OLAI'S PAYBACK, dispatchable now: bump the kolu pin past #2137 (just update-pins), delete packages/server/src/listener.ts's hand-rolled sequence in favor of one serveSurfaceApp call, shrink/retarget listener.test.ts (the #71 cap test may be subsumed by the primitive's own), update architecture.md's debt note, and done-mark this item. Original filing: Pay back the debt architecture.md has carried since phase 2: packages/server/src/listener.ts hand-sequences origin gate → upgrade → stale-tab check → heartbeat → serve, copied from kolu's own surface-app example. PR kolu a serveSurfaceApp primitive owning that sequence; at the next pin bump olai deletes its copy and calls one line. Kolu-side work, zero olai conflict. (Filed 2026-08-10 — the sloppiness audit flagged this as a prose-only 'for now' with no tracking id.)"}
{"id":"viewing","ord":"a1","title":"Viewing","doing":true,"desc":"Seeing your outlines in the browser, kept current with what is on disk."}
{"id":"see-outline","parent":"viewing","ord":"a0","title":"See your outline","done":"2026-08-09T15:36:34-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #58 (squash ac0cba3). Serve a directory, parse and validate the .jsonl files (format core: unique ids, dangling refs, cycles, stored derived state), render the tree read-only in the browser via a minimal surface view. Visible: your outline in a browser tab — or the validation error, with file:line.\nResolved 2026-08-09: entrypoint is `olai web <dir> [--port] [--host]` (nix-built binary; just serve and nix run wrap it; loopback default); UI is a sidebar of found outlines + one tree per route; mirrors render the target subtree inline, marked (cycle-safe); nodes render title with styled #tags, derived done/doing/open status, date badge, and desc as sanitized markdown; packages/store is born here in load-once form (revision-tagged snapshot, codec seam; watcher/probe arrive with live-updates — see docs/brainstorming/architecture.live-store.md); an invalid set renders an error view listing ALL errors with file:line, grouped by file, cross-file errors in their own section; the tree has client-local collapse/expand; e2e is Cucumber + Playwright in kolu's packages/tests shape, features written fresh for this item (nothing ported from the racket branch); styling is Tailwind v4 exactly as kolu does it (utilities inline; no class-validity scanner — accepted trade-off, decided 2026-08-09). Specs: docs/format.md, docs/architecture.md.","after":["scaffold"]}
{"id":"live","parent":"viewing","ord":"a1","title":"Live updates","done":"2026-08-09T16:54:33-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #59 (squash ec5aead). Probe-based staleness over the recursive directory, snapshot stream, error cell. Visible: edit a file or git pull, the page updates without reload; break a file, its outline degrades in place while the rest stays live; a cross-file break keeps last-good and shows the error banner.\nResolved 2026-08-09: error scope is hybrid — a file that fails to decode renders its error in place of that one outline (emanote-style) while other outlines stay live; cross-file validation failures (dangling refs, cycles) hold whole-set last-good with the banner (the codec passes decode failures into validate so the snapshot embeds per-outline failures; store stays generic); deletion is not special (a probe diff — dangling inbound refs are ordinary validation errors, no grace window); the commit()/optimistic-concurrency write gate is deferred to the chat item, where the ops layer is born; the periodic backstop probe stays (~60s) alongside the watcher and refresh(); stamps are mtime+size. This item also closes the see-outline test gap: packages/store gets real unit tests. Design: docs/brainstorming/architecture.live-store.md.","after":["see-outline"]}
{"id":"navigation","parent":"viewing","ord":"a2","title":"Zoom & navigation","done":"2026-08-09T17:18:29-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #60 (squash e036504), which also carried two server fixes approved mid-flight: a busy port now falls back to an OS-assigned one with both facts logged, and a listen failure reports as itself (ListenFailed) instead of the [object Object] runtime-fault message. Zoom into any node as its own page at /n/<id> — stable set-unique ids make permalinks free, surviving renames and moves across files — with breadcrumbs that always show the canonical parent chain (stateless, even when you arrived through a mirror) and a per-view done-visibility toggle (client-local cell, like collapse). Resolved 2026-08-09 (see brainstorming doc). Depends only on see-outline's tree — can run in parallel with live-updates. Brainstorming: docs/brainstorming/viewing-web.md.","after":["see-outline"]}
{"id":"search","parent":"viewing","ord":"a3","title":"Search","desc":"Finding things in the outlines. Grammar and filter-in-place shipped as #149/#168; semantic recall is parked on its own node under Someday/Maybe. Children are the open work, PR-shaped; a child carrying from=168 was deferred out of that PR (find them all with prop:from=168).","after":["navigation"],"changed":"2026-08-16T14:38:57-04:00"}
{"id":"md-docs","parent":"viewing","ord":"a4","title":"Documents #racket-parity","done":"2026-08-10T11:11:13-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #66 (squash ed3ef7f): every .md is a page at /doc/<path> with its own sidebar section; doc-carrying nodes preview one line and render the document inline when zoomed; document text rides the store snapshot (same codec, same revision — no second read path); /media/* pictures route with a both-ends traversal guard (e2e-tested against ..%2f); GFM footnotes with per-block id minting; rehype-highlight bundled, no CDN (e2e asserts nothing fetched off-server). Decisions recorded in docs/brainstorming/viewing-web.md §Documents. Render the served .md files as documents and link them from nodes' doc fields. Moved out of the old editor-growth bucket — it is viewing, not editing. Racket parity (2026-08-09 audit): relative images served safely (racket's /media/* with restricted extensions), highlighted code fences without a CDN, footnotes. Depends only on see-outline's reader; parallel-safe with everything else in this theme.","after":["see-outline"]}
{"id":"journal","parent":"viewing","ord":"a5","title":"Journal & calendar #racket-parity","done":"2026-08-10T08:48:42-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #62 (squash ccb77a1), including a scope addition: the shipped roadmap nodes carry date fields stamped with their PR merge instants (local -04:00), so this feature's calendar renders the project's own history. From the racket audit (2026-08-09): a month calendar in the sidebar (day-with-content dots, today ringed, day-being-read filled) plus a /today view. All derived from date fields at view time — no stored year/month hierarchy (rewrite decision 11), no `daily` op.\nResolved 2026-08-09: the calendar aggregates the WHOLE set's dated nodes (any outline — the journal-is-a-query decision realized; no Daily.jsonl filename magic); clicking a day (and /today) opens /d/<date>, a day view listing every node dated that day grouped by outline with ancestors for context; /today with nothing dated today renders an honest empty state (creation arrives with editing ops); the calendar shows the current month with prev/next paging.","after":["navigation"]}
{"id":"theming","parent":"viewing","ord":"a6","title":"Theming #racket-parity","done":"2026-08-10T13:06:09-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #69 (squash 430d572), authored by Claude/Opus: fifteen named palettes picked in the browser, chalk default, localStorage + data-theme, no server round-trip. From the racket audit (2026-08-09): named palettes selected client-side (racket shipped 15, chalk default with AA contrast), stored in localStorage, applied via data-theme — no server round-trip.","after":["see-outline"]}
{"id":"mobile-pwa","parent":"viewing","ord":"a7","title":"Mobile & PWA #racket-parity","done":"2026-08-10T11:00:12-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #65 (squash 4005580): the original racket icons byte-identical (git hash-object verified), a real manifest as server/src/manifest.ts via kolu's layer, one column below 48rem with the sidebar as a capped scrolling header, 44px touch targets spelled once (touch.ts), visualViewport published as --visible-h/--visible-bottom (lifting the connection dot; left as the chat sheet's seam), no service worker with a scenario pinning it, phone e2e at 390×844 plus a laptop scenario. The chat full-sheet remains owed to after #63. From the racket audit (2026-08-09): installable PWA (manifest + icons, standalone display; no offline mode — the app is live-only), touch target sizing, mobile viewport/keyboard handling (racket's visualViewport tracking), and the chat panel becoming a full sheet on phones. Verified 2026-08-10: today the server ships kolu's manifest with icons: [] — no logo, no touch icon, no favicon — while master-racket carries the complete ORIGINAL asset set (olai/web/static/: icon.svg, icon-192/512, maskable, apple-touch-icon, manifest.webmanifest). Port those original logo assets verbatim; the connection-health indicator shipped separately with PR #61.","after":["see-outline"]}
{"id":"outlines-collection","parent":"viewing","ord":"a8","title":"Outlines as a collection #techdebt","done":"2026-08-10T18:07:08-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #81 (squash e75f157). The `outlines` member was a STREAM carrying the whole corpus in every frame, with the fine-grained part delegated to a client-side `reconcile` keyed on a Solid default the payload never promised. It is now a COLLECTION keyed by root-relative file path — declared in the protocol as `keySchema` — served with the batched `deltas` verb: the whole keyed set on (re)subscribe, then one coalesced {upserts, removes} frame per probe tick. Wire cost is O(changed files), so a `git pull` that rewrites forty outlines is still one frame.\n\n@olai/store keeps the diff it already computed: a published Snapshot names `changed` and `removed`, so a consumer publishing per file does not re-derive it by comparing two whole snapshots. It spans the gap between two PUBLISHED revisions rather than one probe — a probe the codec refuses publishes nothing, so what it found is still owed to the next revision that validates, and a path edited-then-deleted is a remove rather than a change. Still no olai types in the package: it is path talk.\n\n@olai/surface declares OutlineEntry — a file's nodes, its `rev`, and its `broken`, which is the per-entity error scope as DATA: an unreadable file keeps its key and carries its errors — plus a `manifest` cell for the set-wide facts, whose `null` is the \"never loaded\" bit a collection cannot express (an empty snapshot means the directory holds no outlines; a first probe still running has to say something else).\n\nThe server cuts one revision into entries and maps the store's changed/removed onto the collection's verbs (server/src/outlines.ts), published by one owned fiber so a revision's entries and manifest are one write. The client's read side is one module (web/src/client/outlines.ts): the collection's keys in path order are the sidebar's list, and `derive` over every entry's nodes is the one derivation the whole app reads.\n\ndocs/brainstorming/outlines-as-collection.md moves from brainstorming to BUILT, with its open questions answered.\n\nOriginal filing: Re-model the `outlines` stream as a surface collection keyed by file path, with the opt-in deltas verb: snapshot on (re)subscribe, then one coalesced {upserts, removes} frame per probe tick — wire cost O(changed files) instead of O(corpus), keying declared in the protocol (keySchema) rather than inherited from a client-library default, per-entity brokenness as plain entry data. The store's probe already computes the change list; expose it as a per-publish summary so the patch computation stays encapsulated in packages/store. Timing open: bundle with the kolu writeWrappedValue fix (same client layer), or wait until corpus size makes full-snapshot frames hurt; also pushes the frame-cap horizon out.","after":["live"],"see":["live"]}
{"id":"edges-ui","parent":"viewing","ord":"a9","title":"Blocked dependencies & edges in the UI #racket-parity","done":"2026-08-11T10:42:38-04:00","desc":"Re-scoped 2026-08-10: the see half moved to the see-refs item (agents theme); this item keeps BLOCKED derivation and rendering. Filed 2026-08-10: the format validates after/blocks/see (acyclic, blocks normalized to after) but nothing RENDERS them — racket derived and showed blockedness. Derive blocked at view time and render it distinctly from the marks. THE RULE, corrected 2026-08-11 against this item's own filing text above (\"after-targets are not all done\" is `status !== \"done\"`, the trap docs/format.md writes the rule against): `a after b` holds `a` up while `b` is a TASK that is not done — doing or todo — so a target nobody marked never blocks. Racket exempted archived nodes from blocking but not from cycle checks, and that asymmetry is kept. No stored state — blockedness is derived from the edges and the marks together.","after":["navigation"],"see":["see-refs","checkbox"]}
{"id":"title-markdown","parent":"viewing","ord":"a10","title":"Inline markdown in titles #racket-parity","done":"2026-08-11T06:43:14-04:00","desc":"CORRECTION 2026-08-11: this done stamp was FALSE from seconds after the merge — commit a56cb65, mis-titled as this item's roadmap stamp, reverted the entire feature out of master (25 files; exact content-inverse of squash e1ab412, verified by the orchestrator's stamp audit). The third such incident from the same morning session (with dd34849/#87 and 12ea294/#83). Restoration tracked as restore-84; this stamp stands for the original merge instant of #84.\n\nFiled 2026-08-10: racket rendered titles as INLINE-ONLY markdown (bold, links, code — no block elements); the new olai renders titles verbatim with only #tags styled. Port the inline-only discipline through the existing sanitized pipeline.","after":["see-outline"],"see":["md-docs"]}
{"id":"checkbox","parent":"viewing","ord":"a14","title":"Status checkbox #racket-parity","done":"2026-08-10T15:33:37-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #75: status checkbox beside the bullet — ☑ done, ◧ doing, ☐ open — from the same derived status the title already tones with; open is a visible EMPTY box, not the absence of one; read-only (toggling is keyboard-editing). E2E asserts all three faces including empty on an open node. The racket original rendered node status as a CHECKBOX beside the bullet (checked / half / empty for done / doing / open); the rewrite showed only strikethrough/title tone. Port the three faces as a read-only affordance from the same derived status the styling already reads — open is a visible EMPTY box, not the absence of one. No click handler: toggling belongs to keyboard-editing.","after":["see-outline"]}
{"id":"notes-single","parent":"viewing","ord":"a16","title":"Notes: one state, same line #ui","done":"2026-08-10T17:08:58-04:00","desc":"DISPATCHED 2026-08-10 -> Kolu terminal fd21b099 (Grok; reviewer at green: Claude Opus). Human, on sight of the shipped 3-way toggle: delete it — no density states at all. ONE rendering, Workflowy-exact (screen recording 2026-08-10, frame-verified): the note renders UNDER the title on its own line — smaller, dim — CLAMPED TO ONE LINE with ellipsis; CLICKING it expands in place to the full desc plus date and see links, click-away or re-click collapses; no hover behavior, tap is the same click. (Two earlier shapes retracted same day: the 3-way toggle, then a same-line-hover design.) The zoomed page keeps rendering everything, always. Supersedes the full/first-line/hidden switch from desc-density (#74/#76); the per-place unfold cell goes away with it.","see":["desc-density"]}
{"id":"sidebar-tree","parent":"viewing","ord":"a11","title":"Sidebar file tree #racket-parity","done":"2026-08-11T06:33:55-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-10: racket's sidebar was a file TREE; the new sidebar is a flat list where nested outlines render as path strings (Daily/2026-08.jsonl). Group by directory once a served corpus has depth — the ported Dropbox data already does. Scope confirmed & widened by the human 2026-08-10 (screenshot of the deployed sidebar): tree navigation of FOLDERS covering BOTH lists — .jsonl outlines and .md documents — today every brainstorming/*.md renders as a wrapped path string. Collapsible directory nodes (collapse state client-local, like the outline tree's). Resolved 2026-08-10 (human): a SINGLE unified tree mixing .jsonl and .md — a folder shows everything it holds, as racket had — replacing the two flat sections.","after":["see-outline"]}
{"id":"panels","parent":"viewing","ord":"a12","title":"Panel rework: toggleable & resizable","done":"2026-08-11T18:19:57-04:00","desc":"SHIPPED as PR #104 (squash 407edb2; done carries the merge instant). Final head b42b62f: reviewer's 8 must-fixes addressed (phone drawer/scrim/sheet geometry, viewport clamp, transcript unmount, palette '>' failure surfaced, behavioral e2e) + sheet drag-to-snap + 8 cheap takes; two ledger-conflict rounds resolved (branch now carries zero roadmap edits of its own — the file changes only via merge commits taking master's side); 18 required odu checks green both platforms.\n\nDISPATCHED 2026-08-11 -> Kolu terminal c34ee216 (Grok 4.5 high, always-approve; worktree .worktrees/panels, branch panels; brief at .worktrees/panels-BRIEF.md). #stage review — PR #104 (https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/104) open: impl (9d373dd), drawer touch-target fix, hickey/lowy refactor (822589f); 527 unit + 173 e2e green locally; ten evidence shots verified by orchestrator (rail, pill, palette shell match the decisions; pill photographed only in empty state — reviewer to verify last-message + pulse). Review DONE (Claude Opus, split 620b1deb, 2026-08-11; drove the app desktop+phone with a scripted ACP agent, 21 shots): desktop half solid, phone half broken in ways the suite cannot see — 8 must-fixes (drawer resolves position:relative and renders displaced/clipped; scrim and sheet cover the header making its controls untappable; widths don't clamp against viewport; Minimized keeps a transcript subscription for the page's life; palette '>' swallows failure silently; stale README names; tests assert visibility not effect) + 10 nice-to-haves (notably: sheet grab handle must drag, not tap-cycle; hand-roll justified for resize, not for the sheet). MERGE PRE-APPROVED by the human 2026-08-11 — approval opens the gate, not skips the pipeline: squash lands once all 8 must-fixes are addressed, phone evidence verified by the orchestrator, and odu CI is green both platforms at the synced head. #stage addressing-review: Grok fixing, then master merge (past #103) + odu CI to green. Reviewer: Claude (cross-model default). RECONCILED with the shipped app-header (#101), human 2026-08-11: the HEADER KEEPS the connection dot and agent toggle (one home for app chrome); the minimized chat pill is only the chat's collapsed state — last agent message + pulse — bottom-right, no connection dot on it.\n\nFiled 2026-08-10 from the human's UI evaluation session; ONE item by decision (human, 2026-08-10), operable prototypes at https://claude.ai/code/artifact/e8b0f5ad-f293-4982-a4cc-470565ab320e (evaluation grounded in master@232e774 + live screenshots; the prototype's pop-out floating chat window was explored and DROPPED — human retracted it 2026-08-10, chat stays a docked panel). Every panel gets exactly TWO states — open, or minimized-with-signal; nothing closes to nowhere (resolved 2026-08-10, human — the prototype's third 'closed' state and the chat × are dropped) — and the reader owns the widths. Desktop, all resolved 2026-08-10 (human, on the Prototype 1 shell): sidebar collapses to a ~3rem ICON RAIL (expand, outlines/calendar/docs icons, live dot — app chrome never disappears) and drag-resizes; chat dock drag-resizes; minimized chat is a pill carrying the LAST agent message, pulsing while a turn runs (replacing the bare >_ agent pill, whose sidebar-footer/corner homes go away); the connection dot JOINS that pill — one bottom-right chrome strip answering 'is the app alive' (connection state + last agent message; interacts with no-lifecycle's indicator, which this subsumes on every screen); keyboard map reserved NOW so keyboard-editing cannot collide: ⌘K palette, ⌘\\ sidebar, ⌘J chat. A ⌘K palette is the summon surface: navigation, panel toggles, and a '>' prefix that sends to the agent — this ships the palette SHELL; the palette roadmap item keeps jump-to-node type-ahead and op actions. Mobile mirrors it translated: the capped close-on-any-tap burger sheet becomes a real slide-over drawer with scrim; chat becomes a bottom sheet with half/full snap points; its collapsed form is a strip above the thumb with the last message (this discharges mobile-pwa's owed 'chat full sheet'). All layout state client-local in localStorage like chatOpen — never sent. No open questions remain: dispatchable.","after":["chat"],"see":["no-lifecycle","palette"]}
{"id":"solid-utilization","parent":"viewing","ord":"a13","title":"Solid utilization: what the client hand-rolls that the framework does better #techdebt","done":"2026-08-10T14:47:41-04:00","desc":"SHIPPED 2026-08-10 (PR #72): all five seams, one PR, each with a test. (1) <ErrorBoundary> around the shell in main.tsx drawing errors/Fault.tsx — what threw, verbatim, and TWO ways out: a reload (the framework's reloadForUpdate) for a stale bundle, and `/` for a page that is poison, since a fault is usually deterministic for its route and Reload alone would loop. The fallback also LOGS it: a boundary swallows, so without that line the fault reaches no console at all and a browser test meets it as a timeout on a missing element. Documented as a FOURTH error surface beside the three that are about the files. (2) createSelector in Sidebar.tsx (one selector, both lists: what is active is a file) and calendar/Calendar.tsx (the open day; deliberately NOT today, which moves once a day and is meant to redraw the grid). Worth more than the comment claims: `page()` mints a new object every wire frame, so the old form re-ran every row's comparison on every frame from the server, not only on navigation. (3) client/scroll.ts owns every statement that moves the page — top on push, per-entry restore on popstate, and history.scrollRestoration = manual (the browser's restore runs into a page this client has not drawn). The router says only WHICH of the two a navigation gets, because a push and a pop are the one thing it knows that scroll.ts does not. Positions are keyed by an entry key in history.state, read at the moment they are written rather than copied, and recorded AS THE READER SCROLLS because popstate fires after the position it was made from is gone. Unthrottled on purpose: a trailing write lands after the navigation and would record the old page against the new entry. (4) preference.ts grows preferenceChanged + watchPreference — one storage listener feeding the existing theme and chatOpen signals, both started from main.tsx where this client starts everything document-lifetime, and a cleared storage (event.key null) counts as a change rather than as silence. @solid-primitives/storage's makePersisted was evaluated and NOT adopted: <html> is the theme's state and the shell's boot script writes it before any module exists, and this file's degradation contract (storage may throw, the preference stands) is a promise made here rather than trusted to a dependency. (5) clock.ts re-reads on visibilitychange beside the timer, and re-aims the timer from where the clock actually got to — in one place, so the rule the file argues for is written down once. TESTS: unit for thrownText, preferenceChanged and the clock's wake (a fake document + setSystemTime); e2e for the fault card, for scroll on zoom/back (verified to FAIL without the fix — the page stayed at 64px), and for a theme picked in a second tab of the same browser, left open on purpose. SETTLED_SELECTOR now knows three shapes of the app rather than two and world.open() quotes a fault card, so no scenario meets a client fault as a bare timeout again.\n\nFiled 2026-08-10 as the follow-up PR #70 asked for (the client stopped keeping a store above the framework's and adopted @solid-primitives/keyed; this audit hunts the same class — hand-rolled code where a Solid primitive is MORE CORRECT, not dependency swaps). Five verified gaps, each with evidence: (1) NO ErrorBoundary anywhere — the app has three error surfaces for DATA (errors/Page, Banner, Broken) and none for its own code, so a thrown render fault is a dead white tab; #70's RangeError crashed exactly this way. Wrap the shell in <ErrorBoundary> rendering an honest 'this page broke' card with the fault and a reload. (2) The sidebar marks its active entry O(n): Sidebar.tsx:120/149 computes current={props.active === file} per Link, re-diffing every row on each navigation — while Picker.tsx:31 (theming, #69) already established createSelector as the house pattern with the rationale written out. Apply it to both sidebar lists and the calendar's open day (calendar/Day.tsx:108); matters more once sidebar-tree lands and the corpus is the Dropbox port. (3) Navigation ignores scroll: no scrollTo/scrollRestoration outside the chat transcript — router.go() keeps the old scrollTop so zooming from deep in a long outline lands the new page mid-scroll, and back/forward restores nothing the SPA redraw doesn't accidentally keep. Scroll-to-top on push, per-entry restore on popstate; chat/Transcript.tsx's follow-discipline is the house proof this is done deliberately or not at all. (4) Preferences never sync across tabs: preference.ts has no storage-event listener, so a theme picked in one tab leaves a sibling tab stale until reload — against the promise clock.ts argues from ('the one stale thing on the screen'). Either @solid-primitives/storage's makePersisted or one listener in preference.ts feeding the existing signals. (5) clock.ts's midnight setTimeout does not survive suspend/background-throttle: a laptop asleep past midnight wakes showing yesterday's ring until the late timer fires — the file's own doc names this exact promise. Re-read on visibilitychange alongside the timer. Audited and REJECTED, so nobody re-litigates: a router library (routes.ts is five shapes and the hand-rolled pushState router is documented as deliberate), Portal for the fixed panels (no transformed ancestors exist), Suspense (errors-as-data is the design). One PR: the five are independent seams but each is small and they share the review.","after":["see-outline"]}
{"id":"desc-density","parent":"viewing","ord":"a15","title":"Notes fold to one line","done":"2026-08-10T15:31:46-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #74. Filed 2026-08-10 (human, from a screenshot: ~3 nodes per screen once every desc renders in full). Workflowy's answer, adopted: desc renders as ONE dim truncated plain-text line by default — no markdown blocks in the preview. The full desc renders in exactly two places: the node's own zoomed page (it is the page's subject there), and per-node in-place expansion — click the first line to toggle the body open and shut, client-local and keyed by PLACE like collapse. A per-view density switch beside the done toggle: full / first-line, first-line the default (the first line toggles the body open and shut), the same client-local cell pattern as doneHidden. Same visual grammar as md-docs' one-line doc preview. Pairs with title-markdown; independent and parallel-safe.","after":["see-outline"]}
{"id":"agents","ord":"a2","title":"Agents","doing":true,"desc":"Coding agents reading and editing outlines through olai."}
{"id":"chat","parent":"agents","ord":"a0","title":"Chat #racket-parity","done":"2026-08-10T12:12:41-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #63 (squash 66d64ff), the largest PR of the rewrite, through three review rounds plus the human's hands-on rounds: ops layer + store write gate + internal MCP + singleton Claude ACP session with racket session semantics; full-parity panel (in-place streaming, persistent folds, live tool progress, dual-source model, append-log transcript with break markers, slash completion + show-all); busy visibility in three places; mid-turn messages queue; packages/chat extracted from the composition root; the sigint-hang fix with a real-process regression test; the mobile burger (sidebar and chat one tap away on phones). One PR: the chat panel — an ACP session (@agentclientprotocol/sdk) projected into surface events; send, stream, cancel. The ops layer is born here (add, mark, move, archive; fractional-ord insertion; temp-file → re-validate → rename → commit) since chat's agent is the first writer; whole-file agent writes stay rejected. Visible: ask the agent in the web app to check something off and watch the outline update live; a refused write (derived state) shows its unfinished children in chat.\nDecided 2026-08-09: the former agent-editing and chat items collapse into this one; MCP tools for external coding agents are punted to their own item. Resolved 2026-08-09: Claude first via a claude-code ACP adapter; the server owns one singleton ACP session (olai is single-user); agent→ops channel is an internal MCP server passed via session/new; ops tool calls auto-approved (permission UI deferred); agent reads via internal MCP query tools only (likely incl. grep), no fs; transcripts persist as Claude Code's own session for the served directory so `claude --resume` works from a terminal; errors are never silently ignored — only a succeeding StaleWrite retry is invisible, every genuine failure renders in chat with structured detail. Resolved 2026-08-09 round two: racket chat semantics adopted wholesale (boot adopts the most-recent session for the cwd via session/list + session/load; picker; foldable tool frames; slash completion; model display; nix-pinned adapter) — full parity in the one PR; query tools are over parsed nodes with file:line, no raw grep. Racket parity to preserve (2026-08-09 audit): session picker (list/switch/new — racket adopted the most-recently-updated session on boot), slash-command completion in the chat input, foldable tool calls in the transcript; write ops auto-commit with racket's message convention (capture:/done:/doing:/move:/archive: TITLE) when the directory is a git work tree, with an opt-out; the archive op rebuilds the ancestor title-chain in Archive.jsonl and references keep resolving after the move. Brainstorming: docs/brainstorming/acp.md.","after":["live"]}
{"id":"mcp","parent":"agents","ord":"a1","title":"MCP for external agents","done":"2026-08-10T13:16:40-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #68 (squash 64455f7), authored by Claude/Opus, twice re-based over master moves (theming #69, set-fat #70) with conflicts resolved by the agent on instruction: the ops layer as a command an agent launches. Punted 2026-08-09: expose the ops layer as MCP tools so a coding agent outside olai (e.g. in a terminal) can edit outlines. Chat's internal agent→ops wiring is expected to produce most of this for free.","after":["chat"]}
{"id":"create-outline","parent":"agents","ord":"a2","title":"Create an outline","done":"2026-08-10T16:36:06-04:00","desc":"Agents cannot start a new outline file: add_node's plan refuses any file not already under the served directory, and no create op exists. Ship create_outline(file, seed?): a validated relative .jsonl path (traversal-guarded segment-by-segment like /media/*), refuse an already-existing file, optionally seed one first node (same shape add_node mints), ride the store's temp-file → validate → rename → commit write gate, with the capture:/create: git auto-commit convention when the directory is a work tree. Expose through both faces of the ops layer (MCP tools list and therefore chat). Unit tests on the plan refusals; e2e that a live tab shows the new outline without reload.","after":["mcp"],"see":["mcp"]}
{"id":"see-refs","parent":"agents","ord":"a3","title":"see references: rendered, and writable over MCP","done":"2026-08-10T16:26:44-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #79 (squash c04269c). Filed 2026-08-10 from the leg-pain conversation: `see` is the format's free cross-reference — validated, and today invisible and unwritable by agents. Two halves, one PR: (1) RENDER: a node's see refs draw as links to their targets' /n/<id> pages (target's title as the text), on the zoomed page and on rows carrying them — the see half of edges-ui, moved here; edges-ui keeps blocked-derivation. (2) OPS/MCP: a set_see-shaped op (add/remove see targets on an existing node) through the standard write gate, validation refusing unknown targets with the usual teaching error; MCP read tools (search_nodes, read_subtree) carry see in their output so an agent can traverse references. Backlinks (inbound rendering) remain unfiled — this is the forward direction only.","after":["mcp"]}
{"id":"bugs","ord":"zz","title":"Bugs","doing":true,"desc":"Known defects, each carrying its diagnosis; per HACKING.md every fix ships with a test that reproduces it."}
{"id":"live-dead","parent":"bugs","ord":"a0","title":"Live view doesn't work at all — and e2e doesn't catch it","done":"2026-08-09T20:34:19-04:00","desc":"FIXED via kolu PR #2133 (the store merge stops guessing keys) plus PR #61 (pin bump and the failing-shape e2e scenarios: mid-insert, mid-delete, reorder, poisoning — asserting the id multiset). Observed 2026-08-09 in real use: a write to the served directory never reaches the page without a manual reload, while the e2e liveness suite stays green.\nDIAGNOSED 2026-08-09 (Grok; DIAGNOSIS.md in the live-dead worktree). Root cause is UPSTREAM in kolu: @kolu/surface's solid writeWrappedValue merges each stream frame with Solid reconcile(next) under the DEFAULT key 'id' — but olai's outline nodes are Located records ({file,line,node:{id,…}}) with no top-level id, so every element keys as undefined and any membership/order change mis-merges the client store: missing nodes, duplicated nodes, scrambled lines, and a poisoned store that stays wrong for all later frames until reload. Server, watcher, probe, bind and WS frames are all healthy (verified leg by leg; the correct frame reaches the browser). A/B-verified: reconcile(next,{key:null}) or plain replacement fixes mid-insert/mid-delete/reorder.\nWhy e2e is green: every liveness scenario edits in exactly the shapes keyed-reconcile survives — whole-file rewrites at stable indices and end-of-file appends — never a mid-insert, mid-delete, or reorder, and never an assertion on the full id multiset.\nFix direction: upstream PR to kolu (writeWrappedValue must not assume key 'id' for arbitrary payloads — caller-supplied key or replace policy from the stream definition), plus e2e scenarios that fail today: mid-insert, mid-delete, reorder, mid-insert-then-append poisoning, asserting the id multiset rather than eventual title text.\nUpstream fix in flight 2026-08-09: folded into the kolu-side agent's scope (branch surface-typed-lifecycle), pointed at DIAGNOSIS.md. Olai still owes, once the kolu pin moves: the failing-today e2e scenarios and the pin bump.","after":["live"]}
{"id":"no-lifecycle","parent":"bugs","ord":"a1","title":"Stale server accepted silently — no connection indicator","done":"2026-08-09T20:34:19-04:00","desc":"Diagnosed 2026-08-09. Surface is designed to address exactly this class of bug and olai doesn't use that part: packages/web/src/client/wire.ts calls connectSurface (the no-lifecycle seam) and keeps only .client, discarding the wire status accessor. A tab wired to a replaced/dead server just sits there looking fine: on reconnect the new process closes it with the stale-tab code, the wire retires by design (stops re-dialing, reports status 'retired'), and nothing in the UI says so. Fix: make full use of the framework — derive createServerLifecycle (or consume the status connectSurface already returns) and render a GREEN CONNECTION INDICATOR in the UI (kolu's header dot is the house pattern), with the retired state auto-reloading or showing 'server restarted — reload'. Regression test (HACKING.md): restart the scenario-owned server under an open page and assert the page recovers; assert the indicator reflects connected/disconnected/retired. Pairs with the connection-health pill under mobile-pwa.","after":["live"]}
{"id":"no-lifecycle-fix","parent":"no-lifecycle","ord":"a0","title":"The fix in olai","done":"2026-08-09T20:34:19-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #61 (squash 46b3a70), reworked onto kolu #2133 before merge: connection indicator (corner dot, green only when live), retired-wire recovery ('server restarted — Reload' via reloadForUpdate), createServerLifecycle over the status accessor (whose projection maps retired to down), an identity.info surface member feeding the pid echo — which the agent proved was previously a dead gate — and same-port server-restart e2e scenarios. Decided 2026-08-09: this PR will also adopt the upstream kolu API from no-lifecycle-upstream once it exists, in the same PR."}
{"id":"no-lifecycle-upstream","parent":"no-lifecycle","ord":"a1","title":"Upstream: make the framework prevent this #upstream","done":"2026-08-09T20:31:33-04:00","desc":"Shipped as kolu PR #2133: the framework owns the stale-tab handshake end to end (system/identity carries processId, the socket feeds its own echo, rejectStaleProcess deleted), the retired state is a required option on connectSurface (type-level, as preferred), and the store merge stops inferring element keys from field names. The agent was briefed on the three shapes with type-level required, the self-feeding pid echo, and the projectStatus retired→down conflation. What to PR to @kolu/surface / surface-app so a future app cannot silently ship a lifeless wire. To be fleshed out from the PR that comes out of no-lifecycle-fix (decided 2026-08-09). Preference (human, 2026-08-09): type-level is always best — the seam should require a status/onRetired consumer at the type level instead of letting it be discarded. Other shapes on the table: connectSurface defaulting a retired-state handler (reloadForUpdate) unless explicitly overridden; the app shell (surfaceAppLayer) owning a built-in connection indicator. Context: docs/brainstorming/surface-utilization.md.","after":["no-lifecycle-fix"]}
{"id":"live-dead-residual","parent":"bugs","ord":"a4","title":"Live update still duplicates rows — one layer above the fixed merge","done":"2026-08-10T13:11:19-04:00","desc":"FIXED 2026-08-10, shipped as PR #70 (squash 298c30c): the client keeps no store of its own.\nDIAGNOSED 2026-08-10, and it is olai's, one layer above kolu's merge exactly as suspected — but a CONTRACT violation rather than a keying accident. App.tsx reconciled the day's groups (key `file`) and the outline's rows (key `key`) into stores of its own, and what it handed `reconcile` was BORROWED: `situate`/`rowsFor` mint fresh wrappers, but the records inside them are the served set's own Located objects (derive.ts, dates.ts), and `reconcile` unwraps proxies and mutates the previous objects transitively. Read two days in turn and the entries inside a group merge POSITIONALLY (a Situated carries no `file`), while the `shows` records either side of that merge do carry one and it matches — so instead of swapping a reference, reconcile copied one node's id/title/date/desc over another node's record, in the live store, which is the set every other page is read from. Those writes then invalidated the very memo they came from (`derived` reads every node), so the effect re-triggered itself until the stack ran out: `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded`, frames alternating runUpdates ⇄ completeUpdates. Observed in the browser on opening a second day; the Dropbox-port sighting above (a node drawn twice, a day missing from the calendar) is the same class. Reproduced headlessly against docs/roadmap.jsonl before any fix — the first navigation never converges.\nTHE FIX: no store above the framework's. The rows are a memo, a day's groups are read straight off the page model, and identity across frames — the only thing the stores were for — is a KEYING question, answered where the rows are drawn by `<Key>` (@solid-primitives/keyed) on `Row.key` and on `file/id` for a day entry. Nothing in the client writes to what the wire hands it, which is now the rule architecture.md states.\nE2E: `Two days, read in turn, each still says what it said` fails on the old client (day 06 lists `pack` twice) and passes on the new one. `An outline that arrives beside the open one leaves it whole` closes the new-files gap this entry named — it is green on the old client too, so that gap was coverage rather than a second bug.","after":["live"],"see":["live-dead","solid-utilization"]}
{"id":"sigint-hang","parent":"bugs","ord":"a3","title":"Ctrl+C doesn't stop `just run`","done":"2026-08-10T12:12:41-04:00","desc":"Done-marked 2026-08-10 (human-confirmed): the fix and its regression test shipped inside chat's PR #63 (squash 66d64ff) — this stamp carries that merge instant. Observed 2026-08-10 on the chat branch; a second wedged instance (a store-only smoke server from the previous night) suggests it predates chat. Investigation so far: SIGINT is delivered and caught (runMain's handler runs), the listener fully closes (no listening socket, no child processes, no TCP connections remain), yet the process never exits — wedged inside Effect scope teardown after all I/O is gone; repeated Ctrl+C is ignored because the handler already tripped. ROOT CAUSE (fixed in PR #63, with the orchestrator's earlier chat-introduced attribution corrected): NOT chat-specific — master hangs identically whenever a browser tab is connected (the chat agent reproduced it with a master build; the orchestrator's clean master test was an idle server with no tab, the wrong discriminator). server.close waits for every connection, and a tab holds two that never end on their own — the surface websocket and the page's keep-alive — so a server with a tab attached never finished its listener finalizer. Fix: the teardown now terminates websockets and destroys idle keep-alives before close. Regression test packages/server/src/shutdown.test.ts: real child process, real websocket, SIGINT, assert exit within a bound — fails against the old finalizer. Fix must include a regression test: start the real server, SIGINT it, assert exit within a bound.","after":["live"],"see":["serve-surface-app"]}
{"id":"frame-cap","parent":"bugs","ord":"a2","title":"WS frame cap undercuts the framework's #techdebt","done":"2026-08-10T14:09:23-04:00","desc":"FIXED 2026-08-10, shipped as PR #71 (squash 3822a29): the cap is sourced from the framework — `maxPayload: RPC_MAX_FRAME_BYTES + the ndjson delimiter` (the newline rides the wire but is outside what the decoder measures), so the two layers can no longer disagree whatever either of them is re-pinned to. One correction to the audit's account, measured while fixing it: under bun — what actually runs this server — `maxPayload` was never enforced at all. Bun's built-in `ws` ignores the option and applies its own 16 MiB ceiling, closing 1006 \"Received too big message\" (16777216 delivered, one byte more killed). So the 8 MiB cap was inert in production and the raw-1009 death was a node host's failure mode; the misconfiguration was real, its symptom was not reachable here. That is why the regression fence (`packages/server/src/listener.test.ts`) pins the NUMBER — it fails against the old 8 MiB and against any future drift — beside an end-to-end test that a 9 MiB frame is carried and answered. Also newly known: while bun's ceiling stands, the framework's handled INBOUND oversize path is unreachable here at all — a frame the decoder would refuse is already past bun's limit, and even one at exactly the cap arrives a delimiter byte over it and dies at 1006 rather than 1009. (Superseded 2026-08-10 by PR #78, which moved the cap inside serveSurfaceApp as an unspellable framework constant — see serve-surface-app.)\nFound by the surface-utilization audit (2026-08-09): listener.ts sets ws maxPayload to 8 MiB while @kolu/surface's frameLimit classifies oversize at 16 MiB — an 8–16 MiB frame (a big outline snapshot) dies at the raw ws layer with close 1009, taking the whole socket down, instead of the framework's handled oversize path. Fix: source the cap from the framework's constant, with a test. Audit: docs/brainstorming/surface-utilization.md.","after":["live"],"see":["serve-surface-app"]}
{"id":"editor","ord":"a3","title":"Editor","desc":"Editing outlines directly in the web UI. Everything goes through the same ops layer as the agent (born in chat); the growth items after keyboard-editing are mutually independent — parallel-safe, one PR each.","changed":"2026-08-17T21:27:03-04:00"}
{"id":"self-edit","parent":"editor","ord":"a0","title":"Keyboard editing","done":"2026-08-12T09:15:56-04:00","desc":"Shipped in PR #109 (squash a1d18f7, merged 2026-08-12T13:13:53Z, human-approved). The Workflowy editing loop through the ops layer: Enter (new sibling as draft — never writes a blank record), Tab/Shift+Tab indent/outdent, Alt+Shift+↑↓ move, Ctrl+Enter toggle done, ↑↓ row walking with visible caret, Escape; titles edit inline via input overlay, descs edit inline on click (post-review fix); drafts commit on blur/Enter/idle and survive a REFUSED write; every structural key is one op through the write gate — no optimistic echo. edit.apply intent seam: client sends indent/outdent/move intents, server resolves placement against the judged snapshot (mirror-anchor bugs fixed under adversarial review). One keyboard registry joined with #104's map; ⌘K/⌘\\/⌘J pinned by test. 18 new e2e + unit coverage; 858 unit / 252 e2e green on the merged tree. Human's post-test fixes folded in: sibling indent, Shift+Enter inline (no box), desc click-to-edit, visible caret. DELETE ABSENT by ruling; split/merge/undo/multi-select/drag-drop deferred. Final leg note: git rerere silently auto-resolved the roadmap during the master merge — caught and restored byte-for-byte; wariness recorded for future merges.","after":["chat"]}
{"id":"split-merge","parent":"editor","ord":"a1","title":"Split & merge","done":"2026-08-14T00:46:59-04:00","desc":"Enter mid-text splits a node in two; Backspace at line start merges into the previous sibling — compound ops (split_node/merge_node) over the ops layer, both faces calling the same op (deferred 2026-08-09 out of keyboard-editing).\n\nMERGED 2026-08-14 (night slate, SIMPLE — autonomous merge per the human's overnight authorization): PR #157, squashed. Grok review: 2 blocking (split-at-a-mirror wrote in the target's home file and lost the caret — now follows the 'Enter on a mirror makes a sibling of the PLACEMENT' rule; Backspace on an uncommitted typed row was swallowed — merge now commits the draft first), 4 nits, all addressed at 43c1bae with fail-then-pass proof. Undo design settled by the reviewer: split's inverse stays merge-as-inverse (remove + was-guarded title would break redo placement); the residual was-hole is documented in inverseOf's split arm — THE HUMAN MAY OVERRULE. Nine scope decisions documented on the PR (merge targets previous sibling, not Workflowy's previous visible row; Enter-at-head stays add; etc.). CI 9/9 required green; 1362 unit tests, 437 e2e scenarios; evidence page on the PR.","after":["self-edit"]}
{"id":"undo","parent":"editor","ord":"a2","title":"Undo","done":"2026-08-12T13:23:05-04:00","desc":"Shipped in PR #120 (squash b77f929, merged 2026-08-12T17:22:33Z, human-approved with the un-create framing ratified by that approval). Undo/redo as an intent-inverse stack: every web edit — structural AND, after the human's live finding, committed title/desc text — records its server-derived inverse at apply time; ⌘Z replays the inverse through the same edit.apply gate against the current snapshot, ⌘⇧Z symmetric; refusals verbatim over the page. Four review rounds (Grok), each closing a real defect with a regression test proven red: (1) redo pollution — a structural op landing mid-replay repopulated the cleared redo side; fixed by putting ALL stack mutations through one serial queue, making the window unobservable; (2) ledger purity; (3) the human's finding — 'drafts excluded' had over-excluded committed text ops; title/desc inverses added with a was guard (undo of a retyped row refuses naming the current text); (4) the was guard's TOCTOU across ops.run StaleWrite retries — re-checked on every plan attempt. Un-create: ⌘Z on a row this session created archives it (childless only, GONE aside, cannot be redone — unarchive is parity-unarchive); NO delete key, deferral stays open and docs say so (docs/editing.md, new under #123's split). Judgment calls ratified in review: undo of done re-derives the stamp (no op writes a chosen mark value — consistency); position restored as placement (parent + after neighbour), not byte-identical ord. Stack: session-local, depth 100, cleared on outline switch, dead in drafts/forms/palette. 920 unit + 279 e2e at merge; CI green both platforms at the SHIP head.","after":["self-edit"]}
{"id":"dragdrop-multiselect","parent":"editor","ord":"a3","title":"Drag-drop & multi-select","done":"2026-08-14T09:44:30-04:00","desc":"Drag a node to move it with its subtree; Workflowy's multi-select gestures with bulk complete/move/indent/put-away.\n\nMERGED 2026-08-14 (PR #159, the human approved, squashed at bbfeec70 after resolving against master's #161/#163/#164): a drop is a gap plus a depth; carried rows leave the candidate set where rows are measured (cross-file rows too — a mirror is not a parent, and reordering inside a mirror lands in its home file); pick-XOR-caret enforced in open/start; the Trash confirm keys on the pick's identity. Grok review was 3 blocking + 3 nits, all addressed with fail-then-pass proof. In the master merge it adopted #164's canonical readers/steps (its own copies deleted), took LAYER.row/LAYER.chrome for its lines, and recorded why the refusal bar does not ride saying.ts (a run-stopper follows UndoSaid's no-fade rule) while gaining the missing aria-live. Its own flake caveat was closed by #164: 478/478 under the load that used to drop scenarios. CI 9/9 required green at bbfeec70. Deferred (named on the PR, still open): drag-across marquee, touch drag, edge auto-scroll, a delete key (the human's ruling wanted), tone/role unification across five said surfaces.","after":["self-edit"]}
{"id":"input-widgets","parent":"editor","ord":"a4","title":"Input affordances","done":"2026-08-14T10:04:48-04:00","desc":"The Workflowy input widgets: `!` natural-language date list (set_date), `#`/`@` tag autocomplete, `((` mirror-creation search (add_mirror — parity nobody had filed: the surface could retire a placement it had no way to make).\n\nMERGED 2026-08-14 (PR #160, the human approved, squashed at 5af9f68 after resolving against master's #159/#161/#163/#164): built over the ops layer; the palette's list-cursor machinery grew a third keys.ts layer and absorbed two unmigrated copies; a latent blur-swallow bug fixed with a timing-free redraws.test.ts (proved fail-then-pass); feb 29 walks to the next leap year. In the master merge it adopted #164's readers/steps and taught `answering` the completion-list receipt (theListIsGone — one spelling of \"no list\" for three callers), took LAYER.row for the completion popup (with the argued reason the error line does NOT ride saying.ts — it mirrors a live failure, not a lingering said), and closed two parity entries in the brainstorming inventory. Grok review was 1 blocking + 5 nits, all addressed. CI 9/9 required green at 5af9f68; 497 e2e scenarios, 1491 unit tests on the merged tree. Deferred (named on the PR): `!` opens the list only, not the calendar; parity-see/parity-after want this widget family but neither verb is on the wire; unmirror's inverse still empty (reason in the arm).","after":["self-edit"]}
{"id":"palette","parent":"editor","ord":"a5","title":"Command palette #racket-parity","done":"2026-08-14T10:24:50-04:00","desc":"Ctrl+K palette over ops and navigation. History: panels shipped the ⌘K shell; jump-to-node type-ahead shipped with it (fact-checked 2026-08-13); this item delivered the remaining command-ACTIONS half.\n\nMERGED 2026-08-14 (PR #162, the human approved, squashed at 5b99551 after resolving against master's #159/#160/#161/#163/#164): the ••• menu's verbs as palette commands on the zoomed context (palette/ops.ts); quick capture with racket `olai add`-to-Inbox parity; the Inbox convention written in docs/format.md and on the MCP tool text. Grok review was 1 blocking (capture in-flight guard) + 3 nits, all addressed with fail-then-pass proof. The master merge kept both palette features intact (23/23 palette scenarios) on #160's third keys layer, with the argued exception recorded for the one write whose promise is that the reader doesn't move. CI 9/9 required green at 5b99551; 520 e2e scenarios, 1263 unit tests on the merged tree. Evidence artifact: https://claude.ai/code/artifact/524e3160-f738-4b64-b0e6-eb5300eb3bdf (behaviour current; its shot counts predate the final master merge). Deferred, named on the PR: op rows are the zoomed node's only; the residual confirm-shell duplication is a <p> and a flex row.","after":["navigation","panels"],"see":["search","panels"]}
{"id":"kolu-mcp","parent":"agents","ord":"a4","title":"Kolu integration: auto-configure its MCP in the ACP chat","done":"2026-08-10T21:32:52-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-10. If Kolu is running on olai's host, the chat agent's session automatically gets kolu's MCP server — a stdio entry (`command: kolu, args: [\"mcp\"]`, forwarding `PADI_SOCKET`, per juspay/kolu's own `.mcp.json`) added to `session/new`'s `mcpServers` alongside olai's internal tool server (`mcpServersOf`, packages/chat/src/agent.ts — today it only speaks the http shape; this teaches it stdio).\n\nResolved 2026-08-10:\n- **Detection targets padi, not kolu web.** The web server may live on another machine reaching this host as a remote (the motivating topology: kolu web on pureintent, this host runs only the padi daemon — https://kolu.dev/padi/). \"Kolu is running\" = the padi daemon is reachable: `$PADI_SOCKET` when olai was launched inside a kolu terminal, else padi's state-root-keyed default socket; plus `kolu` on PATH to spawn the stdio server.\n- **Per session/new**: detect fresh each time a chat session is created, not once at boot — padi starting after olai still gets picked up.\n- **Automatic, no knob**: no flag/env; a knob can come later if ever wanted.\n- **Kolu-specific**: no generic extra-MCP-servers mechanism until a second server shows up.\n- **Upstream option** (#upstream): the detection logic can live in Kolu itself — e.g. a cheap \"is padi reachable\" probe verb, or `kolu mcp` failing fast when no daemon — so olai's side reduces to spawn-and-fall-back rather than reimplementing socket discovery.\n\nHazard found 2026-08-10 while orchestrating, worth knowing before implementing: `kolu` on PATH is not necessarily the host's kolu. Inside a kolu-spawned terminal the padi daemon prepends its own baked agent-tools bundle, and that bundle's `kolu` was an older build reporting the same version string while missing most verbs — filed as juspay/kolu#2146, fixed by kolu#2147 (the injected binary now resolves to the same build as the host's). The lesson survives the fix: a `kolu` on PATH still SPAWNS when it is the wrong build, so detection should confirm the spawned server answers rather than assume the binary is current. The #upstream option above covers this too.\n\nRefs: https://kolu.dev/mcp/, https://github.com/juspay/kolu/blob/master/.mcp.json"}
{"id":"commit-button","parent":"agents","ord":"a5","title":"Commit changes on purpose, not on every write","done":"2026-08-11T06:57:37-04:00","desc":"CORRECTION 2026-08-11: this done stamp was FALSE from 23 seconds after the merge — commit 12ea294, mis-titled as this item's roadmap stamp, reverted the entire feature out of master (59 files, +390/−4415; exact content-inverse of squash c5bcbdf outside the roadmap, verified). Second of three such incidents from the same morning session (dd34849/#87, a56cb65/#84). Restoration tracked under mcp-commit-manual (option A: restore-83 PR first, then the MCP-face delta); this stamp stands for the original merge instant of #83.\n\nFiled 2026-08-10. Today every op commits itself (packages/ops/src/git.ts), so one train of thought becomes a dozen commits — a roadmap gardening session produced eleven, each with a full node title as its subject line. Replace that with a commit someone asks for.\n\nDesign: docs/brainstorming/git-commits.md.\n\nResolved 2026-08-10:\n- **The feature earns its place for ONE reason: an audit trail of what the tool wrote.** Chat auto-approves its ops and there is no web editor yet, so every write olai makes is one the human did not type. Not for history (descs carry their own dates), not for sync (olai never pushes), not for undo (the `undo` item plans real op inverses).\n- **Manual, not automatic.** Two doors to the same action: a Commit button in the UI, and a `commit` MCP tool the agent calls when it knows its work is done (and can say why). `--commit=off | manual | auto`, default `manual`; `--no-commit` keeps meaning `off`. Never `--amend`.\n- **Derive from git, store nothing**; **never show a text diff**; **writer intent via X-Olai-Writer trailer**; **messages prefixed `olai`**; **busy-repository check decides manual over automatic**; **one derivation serves the Changes view and per-node history**. (Full resolution detail in git history and docs/brainstorming/git-commits.md.)"}
{"id":"paste-image","parent":"agents","ord":"a6","title":"Paste an image into the chat","done":"2026-08-11T06:38:54-04:00","desc":"CORRECTION 2026-08-11: this done stamp was FALSE from 19 seconds after the merge — commit dd34849, mis-titled as this item's roadmap stamp, reverted the entire feature out of master (29 files, -1830 lines, tests included; CI stayed green because the tests left with it). Discovered when the human pasted an image and nothing happened. Restoration tracked as paste-restore (split from silent-errors 2026-08-11, human's call: separate quick PR, no review); the wider audit tracked as silent-errors; this stamp stands for the original merge instant of #87.\n\nFiled 2026-08-10. Paste (and drop, and pick on a phone) an image into the chat composer and have the agent see it. Today the whole chat path is `string` end to end and the composer has no paste handler at all.\n\n**What hardcodes text, layer by layer:** `packages/surface/src/chat.ts` (`send: { input: { text: Schema.String } }`), `packages/chat/src/chat.ts` (`begin(prompt: string)`, queue is `string[]`), `packages/chat/src/agent.ts:115` (`prompt: (text: string)`) and `:549`, where the ACP call is a hardcoded single block `[{ type: \"text\", text }]`. `ChatEntry` (packages/surface/src/chat.ts) has only `text`, so a transcript row has nowhere to name an attachment. `Composer.tsx` mentions none of paste/clipboard/File/Blob.\n\nResolved 2026-08-10:\n- **The agent receives a PATH, as text.** The prompt carries the tmp path; Claude Code reads the file itself. Kolu's model exactly, and it does not depend on `promptCapabilities.image` — which ACP 1.3 does define (`ContentBlock` has an `image` variant carrying base64 `data` + `mimeType`), and which olai could switch to later without redoing the transport. Inline base64 was rejected for now: the bytes would ride the prompt into the persisted Claude Code session.\n- **Bytes land in a TMP dir, not under the served root.** That keeps screenshots out of the store's probe and out of anything `commit-button` would commit. One dir per chat session, removed when the session ends or the server shuts down (the scope finalizer).\n- **Transport is CHUNKED over an existing surface procedure**, not a new HTTP route — a procedure inherits the origin gate and session `listener.ts` already enforces for the websocket, where an upload route would need its own.\n- **The transcript shows a filename chip.** The tab that pasted previews from the Blob it already has; other tabs and any reload show the chip. `/media/*` cannot help — it is guarded to the served root and the file is in tmp — and a second guarded route was judged not worth a thumbnail.\n\n**Copy kolu's derivation rather than re-deriving it** (packages/padi/src/upload.ts, and `scratch.write` in packages/padi/src/surface.ts, at the pin):\n- Two independent numbers. `MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES` = 50 MiB is a POLICY cap on the file; `UPLOAD_CHUNK_BYTES` = 3 MiB is what one frame carries. Before chunking they were the same number and a 26 MB drop closed the socket with 1009, taking every other subscription on that tab's multiplexed wire down with it.\n- 3 MiB is chosen, not rounded: it must be a MULTIPLE OF 3 so base64's 3-bytes-to-4-chars grouping divides exactly and every chunk boundary lands on a 4-character group, which is what lets each chunk decode independently (4 MiB fails this — 1048576 is not divisible by 3). 3 MiB raw is exactly 4 MiB base64; plus a <64 KiB envelope that is 4.06 MiB against the 16 MiB budget, ~3.9x headroom. Base64's alphabet contains nothing JSON escapes, so there is no third expansion inside the JSON string — the one expansion it is tempting to forget, and it is genuinely absent.\n- First chunk creates the file (no `appendTo`); each later chunk passes back the path it was returned and appends.\n- Three details worth taking with it: ONE policy module consumed by both the client's pre-flight gate and the server's authoritative gate, so the two cannot drift on the rejection threshold; `appendTo` carries no authority, because the server re-derives the scratch dir and refuses any path outside it (\"a continuation token that happens to be readable\"); the dropped filename is sanitized to a safe basename and collision-suffixed.\n\nAlso: a phone has no Ctrl+V, so a file input belongs beside the paste handler (mobile-pwa shipped the viewport work this sits on). Drag-and-drop onto the panel is the same machinery once paste works."}
{"id":"not-every-node-a-task","parent":"viewing","ord":"aA","title":"A bullet is not a task","done":"2026-08-11T06:29:08-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-10. Today every node is a task: status is derived as done → doing → **otherwise open** (docs/format.md:62), so `open` is what a node gets for carrying no mark, and there is no way to say \"this is only a bullet\". `checkbox` (PR #75) made it visible on every row on purpose — \"open is a visible EMPTY box, not the absence of one\".\n\nWorkflowy's model, which we are adopting: a bullet is just a bullet, completion is a flag you apply to some of them, and an unticked bullet is plain text rather than an unfinished task.\n\n**No decision ever defended the current model.** Nothing in docs/ argues that every node should have a status. The rule arrived in ac0cba3 (PR #58, the first rendering PR) as part of the derived-status rule, and racket did the same before it (`checkbox` is tagged #racket-parity). Two things hid it: the only outline olai has ever served is its own roadmap, which really is all tasks, so \"everything is open\" never looked wrong; and format.md:62 argues carefully about WHERE status lives (\"a stored value that currently agrees with the computed one is still a second copy\") without ever asking WHETHER every node needs one.\n\nWhy it matters now: serving the ported Dropbox notes (see sidebar-tree) renders every paragraph as an unfinished to-do, and `search`'s planned `is:open` operator is useless if it matches the whole corpus.\n\nThe defect stated precisely: `open` answers two different questions with one value — \"a task nobody has started\" and \"not a task at all\". The same shape the manifest cell exists to fix, where an empty collection could not distinguish \"never loaded\" from \"empty\".\n\nThree shapes, cheapest first (the human chose the third; the first is a legitimate first step if the screen should be honest before the format moves):\n1. **View-only**: draw the checkbox only where something in the subtree is actually marked. No format change; the model still says everything is open.\n2. **Opt in**: a `#todo` tag or field makes a node a task. Clear, but adds a concept and migrates the roadmap.\n3. **Drop `open` as the residual**: a node is either marked (`done`/`doing`) or has no status at all. Closest to Workflowy, and the honest fix.\n\nKnown cost of (3), not a free lunch: the parent rule needs a second case — \"all children done → done\" becomes \"count only the children that are tasks\" — and `blocked` has to say what \"not done\" means for a bullet that was never a task (probably: a plain bullet never blocks). `edges-ui` has NOT shipped, which is lucky: blocked-derivation can be designed against the new model rather than migrated to it. Also touches the query tools and the done-visibility toggle, which both read `open`."}
{"id":"todo-mark","parent":"viewing","ord":"aB","title":"`todo` — the third mark","done":"2026-08-11T07:45:28-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11, straight out of using what #86 shipped. `not-every-node-a-task` removed `open` because it was the RESIDUAL — the value a node got for carrying nothing, which made every bullet a task. That was the defect and removing it was right. But it also removed the CONCEPT of an unstarted task, and those are two different changes.\n\n`done` and `doing` are explicit stored marks. A third explicit mark, `todo`, obeys the new model exactly: you have to put it there, so a bullet stays a bullet unless marked. What the model forbids is a DEFAULT, not a third face. Nothing about the Workflowy reading argues against it.\n\nThe gap showed the moment the human tried to say \"this is work I have not started\": there was nothing to say it with. The orchestrator reached for a `#todo` hashtag, which is decoration — no checkbox, no derivation, styled like `#racket-parity`. The human called it hacky and is right.\n\nShip:\n- `Status = \"done\" | \"doing\" | \"todo\"` (packages/format/src/derive.ts:32), stored like the other two, never derived as a residual. At most one of the three on a node, same rule `done`/`doing` already have.\n- The empty checkbox comes back, for `todo` ONLY — `checkbox` (PR #75) had three faces and #86 cut it to two; this restores the third without restoring the default. A node carrying no mark still draws no box.\n- Parents count a `todo` child as an unfinished task, so a theme of unstarted work derives `doing` rather than nothing. Decide and write down what a parent whose task children are ALL `todo` derives — `todo` is the natural answer, but it must be stated, not inferred.\n- `blocked`, still unshipped (`edges-ui`), inherits it: docs/format.md:49 currently says `b` blocks `a` \"while `b` is `doing`\" — with three marks that becomes `doing` or `todo`, and the trap it warns against (`status !== \"done\"`, which reads every plain bullet as an obstacle) stands unchanged.\n- Migration, authorised by the human for this PR only: the nine `#todo` TAGS the orchestrator put in docs/roadmap.jsonl (search, edges-ui, panels, self-edit, split-merge, undo, dragdrop-multiselect, input-widgets, palette) become the real mark, and the tags come out of those titles.\n\ndocs/format.md's Status section is the text that has to change, and it should argue the three-mark shape as carefully as it currently argues the two — the next reader learns the model from there, and `edges-ui` will be built from it."}
{"id":"orchestrator-in-chat","parent":"agents","ord":"a8","title":"Orchestrate from the chat, not a terminal #human","todo":true,"desc":"Filed 2026-08-11. RATIFIED 2026-08-15 as the direction: orchestration's home IS olai, operated from the chat. THE SHAPE (docs/brainstorming/orchestrator.md, with the worked DAG example and the two gates the day taught): agents (who — docs/agents.olai, shipped), workflows (how — the template DAG), lanes (what's live — the template cloned, marks flipping). RULINGS: template in ONE shared outline; cross-repo progress recorded there; babysitting transport is kolu MCP tool calls; the waker is olai itself as ACP client over kolu's subscribable urgency/terminals cells; titles say WHAT the PR does; chores get done, not tracked; the DEFERRAL GATE — a PR whose report carries deferrals merges only on the human's word, each deferral a node first; the charter's canonical home is THE GIST, never a repo copy.\n\nWHAT THE DAY BUILT UNDER THIS PROGRAM: set_doing refuses on blocked (PR #181); properties landed whole (PR #179 — custom, set_prop, prop: search, drawer, stamps), so workflow-template is UNBLOCKED and is the program's next authoring step; then lane-cloning, then olai-subscribes; hits-carry-custom filed as the board's one-query PR. The chat itself gained the model header (PR #176), token usage (PR #178) and wrapping diffs (PR #185) en route.\n\nVerified working — spawning: lifecycle_create over kolu's MCP cuts worktrees since kolu #2167. The roadmap edits natively from chat through the ops layer with writer attribution."}
{"id":"oic-worktree","parent":"orchestrator-in-chat","ord":"a0","title":"MCP `lifecycle_create` cannot cut a worktree #upstream","done":"2026-08-13T21:53:22-04:00","desc":"A missing option on an existing kolu MCP verb — completing the socket so one tool call replaces spawning a terminal to type `kolu create --worktree` (debate item 9, \"fix not vault\"). **Shipped as kolu #2167 (merged 2026-08-14, head d983f5d0)**: `lifecycle_create` takes repo/worktree/run; the worktree→create→sendInput composition carries a typed partial-failure payload (stopped-partway survivor reports pinned by wire tests that fail against the pre-fix pass-through); a bare create still behaves exactly as before (pinned). The author named the honest residue in the PR rather than burying it: this composition now exists in four disagreeing copies across kolu's faces, and the real fix is a padi-side lifecycle.createInWorktree — architecture, deliberately out of a fixes batch. Directly enables olai-chat orchestration (see orchestrator-home)."}
{"id":"oic-doc","parent":"orchestrator-in-chat","ord":"a3","title":"The charter stays at the gist — the repo copy was made and unmade","done":"2026-08-15T11:24:38-04:00","desc":"Was \"the orchestrator doc lives at a URL\". RESOLVED 2026-08-15 the opposite way it briefly went: a repo copy at docs/orchestrator.md was made and then DELETED on the human's word — the gist (gist.github.com/srid/af6b4bcccf649fb923e4e207a7b93c51) IS the canonical charter, hand-maintained, never mirrored. The chat-agent-can't-read-it concern this node was filed for dissolves differently: the chat has web reach questions of its own, and the charter's future in-olai home is the workflows outline (the DAG), not a prose copy."}
{"id":"hide-done-scope","parent":"viewing","ord":"aC","title":"Done-hidden hides things that are not done #human","done":"2026-08-11T09:12:52-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11, from the first real use of the #88 model. Two intertwined problems, hit on the same node (`orchestrator-in-chat`).\n\n**1. \"Done-hidden\" does not mean \"done nodes are hidden\".**\n\n`withoutDone` (packages/format/src/derive.ts:381) drops a `done` row **with its whole subtree**. A parent that *derives* done — because its task children are all done — takes its unmarked-bullet descendants down with it, even though nobody ever marked them done and they may be the very findings you wanted to read. Live case: `agents` derives done, so the done toggle hides `orchestrator-in-chat` and the four gap-findings under it.\n\nThe human's position, verbatim: *\"done-hidden should mean done nodes are hidden.\"*\n\n**2. A parent whose children are all bullets cannot be marked at all.**\n\nThe intended shape was: `orchestrator-in-chat` is `todo` (it IS the work), its four children are findings — plain bullets, correctly unmarked. The ops layer refuses it: `docs/format.md:49` forbids any stored mark on a node with counted children, as the price of no-stored-derived-state.\n\nThe two compose into an absurdity: the node tracking the chat-orchestrator work is unmarkable (2), so `agents` derives done, so the done toggle hides that work entirely (1). The escape hatch from (1) — mark the work `todo` so the parent reads doing — is exactly what (2) forbids, so #88's fix was defeated by the model on its first day of real use.\n\n**Resolved 2026-08-11 — the root cause is derivation itself, and the fix is to delete it.**\n\nDeriving a parent's status from its children treats outline containment (notes under an item) as task decomposition (subtasks). It also violates #86's own creed — \"a node is a task because someone said so, never by default\": derivation makes every parent-of-tasks a task by structure, nobody having said so, which is the `open` defect promoted one level up. The no-stored-derived-state rule exists only to defend derivation from contradiction; without derivation it has nothing to defend and dissolves rather than needing an exception. Neither candidate patch (vacuous-derivation exception, per-node hiding) was taken: each keeps the conflation and adds cases to it.\n\nThe design (the Workflowy model; org-mode likewise stores status per headline and keeps rollup display-only):\n\n- **Marks are plain stored facts on any node**, children or not. Status is never derived. `read this book` can be ticked with three notes hanging off it.\n- **Delete the machinery**: the `statuses` walk as status, `fromChildren`/`InTheWay`, both refusal sites (validator load error, ops `derived` refusal), and the format.md:83 rule. Net deletion.\n- **Done-hidden means exactly**: nodes whose stored mark is `done` are hidden, subtree included — a done mark on a parent is a human's claim about the whole branch, so the sweep is now justified rather than inferred.\n- **Rollup survives as display only**: a parent with task children may show derived progress (e.g. `3/5`), never a status — it feeds neither hiding nor blocking. Write-time nudges are policy, not invariants: when the last unfinished task child goes done, suggest marking the parent done; `set_done` over unfinished task children may warn. Never a load invariant — a merged file always loads.\n- **`blocked` (edges-ui) reads stored marks**: `doing` or `todo` blocks, a markless node never does. To block on a branch, mark the branch.\n- **Merge safety improves**: marks merge line-wise with no cross-line invariant a clean merge can break. (Today: mark a leaf in branch A, add a task child under it in branch B — clean textual merge, unloadable set.)\n- **Migration**: stored marks on the roadmap parents that should read as something (e.g. `bugs`/`infra` done, `viewing`/`agents` doing), through the format's own writer, like #88's migration."}
{"id":"mcp-fail-visible","parent":"bugs","ord":"a5","title":"A failed MCP server is invisible in the UI","done":"2026-08-13T13:51:08-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11, from the kolu-not-detected incident. An MCP server that fails to attach to a chat session must be VISIBLE.\n\n- The human's position: MCP must NOT fail silently. **Shipped as PR #140 (squash 1c56f90), 2026-08-13.** A strip under the chat panel header — master's three-surface vocabulary, one alarm dot, nothing on a healthy session — states the fact in the server's own words: refused the identity read / closed without answering / did not answer within 5s / could not be started. One probe answers both halves (serverOf / missingFrom); a `servers` event lands on the existing chat cell before every session opens; `MissingServer.where` names the probed binary. \"Never installed\" (no kolu on PATH, no PADI_SOCKET) is deliberately not a failure — but PADI_SOCKET set with kolu absent from the process's PATH is, with its own sentence (the review's crux residual, closed in the delta).\n- The `/simplify` pass found and fixed a live crash one file over: a bad OLAI_ACP_AGENT dumped an uncaught ENOENT stack, then blamed `initialize`; the exec-failure axis moved to pipes.ts and both subprocess callers race `unstartable`.\n- Pipeline: Grok review — zero MUST-FIX; all six NICE-TO-HAVEs taken in the delta (the deadline pin cost 150ms not 5s; the @phone scenario asserts the strip; the NTH#1 test caught the suite reading this machine's ambient PADI_SOCKET). DELTA-OK; CI 10/10 green on kolu-ci-1 at 16b8be5. The ci-2/ci-4 ~4m42s e2e walls struck again on two SHAs and were judged host-side by the reviewer — failed logs preserved this time.","see":["kolu-detect-primary"]}
{"id":"kolu-detect-primary","parent":"agents","ord":"a9","title":"Kolu detection must find the primary padi — delegate detection to kolu #upstream","done":"2026-08-11T09:58:21-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11, from the same incident. Multiple padi daemons on one host are EXPECTED and normal (dev/e2e state roots beside the main one). But olai's probe spawned `kolu mcp` with no `PADI_SOCKET` and `kolu mcp` refuses to choose among daemons — so detection answered \"no kolu\" on a healthy host. The human's position: selection belongs in kolu; olai deliberately refuses to reimplement discovery. Upstream path resolved 2026-08-11 with the human's personal approval; done-stamped then.\n\n- **The leftover — probe-as-evidence — SHIPPED 2026-08-14, both sides** (debate item 9, filed against this paid item per grok's disposition, no second node): kolu #2168 (merged) ships the zero-dependency leaf `@kolu/detect` — `detect()`/`probe()` answering tagged `Detected`/`ProbeFailure`, reading the live-daemon identity resource, racing exec failure, editorializing nothing (notOnPath deliberately reasonless — the judgement is the consumer's; no env reads, no throws; the review's one MUST-FIX, a PADI_SOCKET inherit leak where probe and returned spawn recipe could disagree, fixed red-first). olai #150 (squash be9dc95c, merged on the human's approval) shrinks packages/chat/src/kolu.ts onto the socket with the hard constraint held end to end: kolu.test.ts byte-for-byte unmodified through authoring, review fix, and repin — mcp-fail-visible's sentences survive verbatim, proven by the untouched pins and the a_failed_mcp_server e2e. Pinned to kolu master 39b97df6 (contains #2166+#2168; the next ordinary bump picks up #2167).","see":["mcp-fail-visible","kolu-mcp"]}
{"id":"surface-mcp","parent":"agents","ord":"aA","title":"One surface for browser and agents: project the surface into MCP #upstream","done":"2026-08-14T23:32:06-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11: adopt kolu's surface-MCP so the browser and agents share ONE surface. Largely landed via PR #94 (both MCP faces).\n\nAUDIT MERGED 2026-08-14 (PR #167, the human approved, squashed at 1fd9457 after resolving the #168 seam — the resolution exercised the PR's own property on #168's fields: SearchRequest gained file/under/operator-prose, SearchAnswer gained refusals, filter.ts's Refusal promoted to Schema, the duplicate SearchRefusal deleted; dropping refusals now fails ops+web+server together where before one side compiled on): docs/brainstorming/surface-mcp-positions.md holds the audit with receipts against master — two positions already true (subscribable resources; deps-check by construction), two closed (verbs-drift unrepresentable via format/searching.ts; refusal contract pinned across all four OpFailure kinds on both faces), the bridge priced (418 MB / 2099 fds, method written) and gated upstream.\n\nTHE HUMAN'S RULING (2026-08-14, night slate): reads-on-the-floor implemented tonight; watcher-fd-cost investigated tonight, fixed if the fix is contained; per-face-expose and mcp-bridge MOVED OUT to Agents (kolu ask filed + kolu PR drafted overnight, merge is the human's). This parent closes when its two remaining children do."}
{"id":"surface-mcp-viewing","parent":"surface-mcp","ord":"a0","title":"Viewing over surface-mcp: agents subscribe the browser's cells","done":"2026-08-11T14:38:08-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11, split out of the parent as the slice adoptable before web editing. Scope: adopt `@kolu/surface-mcp` (`serveSurfaceAsMcp`) for olai's READ side — the surface's cells exposed as subscribable MCP resources (default-deny `expose` list), so an agent watches the same live rows the browser draws; the existing query tools ride as bespoke tools unchanged. Design: docs/brainstorming/surface-mcp-viewing.md.\n\nShipped 2026-08-11 as PR #94 (squash), the whole chain in one day: kolu#2155 upstream (ToolFailure/structuredContent/instructions/title), pin bump, then the gated commit — ops/mcp.ts (227 lines) and mcp/stdio.ts (83) deleted, both faces flipped onto serveSurfaceAsMcp, documents exposed as a resource on #95's shape. Four SDK gaps closed en route (stdin-end drain, bespoke half-duplex HTTP transport where SSE hung e2e, Schema.Struct({}) anyOf wrapping that broke every no-arg call, plus a same-id waiter collision). Behaviour changes recorded and fenced: unknown tool → isError, destructiveHint derived from mutates, additionalProperties drift. Acknowledged residual: a handler that never replies leaves the drain counter stuck — pre-existing class, stated on the PR. Write tools stay bespoke until the Editor-theme design settles the parent.","after":["edges-ui"]}
{"id":"md-render","parent":"viewing","ord":"aD","title":"Markdown rendering, perfected","done":"2026-08-11T13:08:50-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11 on the human's ask. Documents (.md) and notes render through the one remark/rehype pipeline (gfm, sanitize, highlight — bundled); the rendering has never had a polish pass. Method, set by the human: the agent screenshots real rendered markdown, the orchestrator gives concrete visual feedback, iterate to done — same evidence loop the orchestrator protocol now requires anyway.\n\n**Now** (2026-08-11):\n- Kolu terminal: `7cc6c6da` (Claude Opus, YOLO), worktree `.worktrees/md-render`, branch `md-render`; reviewer split `f26ebf9d` (Grok)\n- Stage: #review — APPROVED by srid; resolving DIRTY vs master, merges on green\n- PR: https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/92"}
{"id":"run-reload","parent":"infra","ord":"a5","title":"`just run` auto-reloads on source change","done":"2026-08-11T10:45:01-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11 on the human's ask. `just serve` watches; `just run` deliberately does not (its comment says so). Wanted: auto-reload on source change for `just run`, with the recipe comment updated to the new truth and the sigint behaviour unregressed. Review waived by the human; evidence is a transcript in the PR description."}
{"id":"load-perf","parent":"bugs","ord":"a6","title":"Roadmap initial load feels very slow","done":"2026-08-12T08:24:51-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11 by the human. Symptom: initial load of the roadmap in the web UI is subjectively very slow. Dispatched as measurement + root-cause analysis FIRST — phase breakdown (server load/validate/derive, wire payload, client bundle/render) with hard numbers and a named dominant term; the fix is a separate dispatch after the human reads the RCA.\n\nP0 shipped 2026-08-11: PR #93 (squash f079b7b) — build-time .br/.gz siblings for /assets/*, negotiated by @kolu/surface-app's existing static layer; 977 KB → ~253 KB brotli on the wire, browser-verified. The RCA and measurement harness were dropped from the PR by the human's own hand (they live in the PR history at 624ffba if ever wanted). P2 shipped separately as snapshot-scale (PR #95). OPEN: P1 — code-split markdown/highlight off the critical path (the remaining long-task cost) — undecided, awaiting the human."}
{"id":"snapshot-scale","parent":"bugs","ord":"a7","title":"First snapshot carries every document body — must scale to 1000s of .md files","done":"2026-08-11T11:50:45-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11, escalated by the human from load-perf P2: the manifest cell in the FIRST websocket snapshot inlines the full text of every .md document in the served set (~124 KB of the ~212 KB snapshot for docs/, measured in LOAD-PERF-RCA.md). The human's requirement, verbatim intent: olai must support directories with THOUSANDS of .md files — a wire that ships every body up front is O(corpus) on first paint and therefore not acceptable, regardless of perf feel today.\n\nShape: documents become a collection on the surface — the first snapshot carries only what the sidebar draws (path, title); a body travels when a document is opened (lazy fetch or per-document subscription). This is a WIRE change only: the server still loads and validates the whole set (format.md's rule — doc references must be checkable — is untouched).\n\nInteractions: dovetails with surface-mcp-viewing — kolu surface-mcp projects collections as templated resources, so the documents-as-collection shape serves browser and agents alike; the viewing-slice design (terminal d5df9f63, in flight at filing) must anticipate this shape rather than exposing today's body-carrying manifest cell. At 1000s of files the server-side whole-set revalidation cost also deserves its own measurement before it surprises anyone — noted here, not scoped here.","see":["load-perf","surface-mcp-viewing"]}
{"id":"batch-capture","parent":"agents","ord":"aD","title":"Batch capture: a subtree in one MCP call","done":"2026-08-11T19:17:10-04:00","desc":"SHIPPED as PR #106 (squash 839ab6a; done carries the merge instant). Final head 258382f: add_node grows recursive children AND — human's ratification reversing the author's decline — create_outline seeds a whole tree, same capture shape, refused seed leaves NO file. One call/plan/validation/atomic write/commit; marks ride through the one stampOf seam (#103 day rules hold); captured names every minted node; MCP hosts reject $ref so nesting unrolls three levels with the planner refusing deeper BY NAME (silent leaf-loss guarded). Schema cost measured: +1523B after prose-dedupe mitigation; the further 600B description-strip DECLINED, human endorsed — an agent-facing schema teaches its own field shapes. Review (Grok): approve, zero must-fixes, probes independent. 577 unit + 183 e2e; 24/24 checks green both platforms.\n\nDISPATCHED 2026-08-11 -> Kolu terminal d94c7864 (Claude Opus, YOLO; worktree .worktrees/batch-capture; brief at .worktrees/batch-capture-BRIEF.md). #stage review — PR #106 (https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/106) open: impl + hickey/lowy + /simplify pushed; 553 unit + 172 e2e green locally; evidence (one call, 15 nodes, 4 levels, 19ms, one commit, ref-free schema) in .worktrees/batch-capture-out/. Notable: MCP hosts reject $ref so the recursive schema is UNROLLED to three levels, planner refuses deeper by name (a schema-dropped child must never silently lose leaves); nested-schema prose dedupe shrank the tool table 13239→11085 bytes; one declined altitude finding flagged for review. REVIEW DONE (Grok, split 02207bef, 2026-08-11): APPROVE, zero must-fixes — atomicity, schema unroll, stampOf seam, captured, placement, neighbor round-trip all verified under independent MCP probes; declined altitude finding endorsed. Four nice-to-haves. #stage extending: 3 of 4 nice-to-haves taken (neighbor test hardened to byte-identity of untouched lines), CI was fully green at 60b9921 (20 nodes both platforms, 574 unit) — then the human RATIFIED THE OTHER WAY on #4 (2026-08-11): create_outline seeds a whole TREE in this PR, same recursive capture shape, refused seed refuses the create (no empty outline); wire-cost doubling to be mitigated or measured. Opus building; re-CI; then FIELDED to the human at green — no pre-approval, he decides the merge. Reviewer: Grok (cross-model). SHAPE decided by the human 2026-08-11: add_node grows an optional recursive children field (title + optional desc/date/id/mark per child) — no dedicated add_subtree tool, no generic batch envelope. One plan, one validation, one atomic write, one commit.\n\nFiled 2026-08-11 from the human watching a chat agent capture a house outline: every child is its own add_node call, so a subtree is N sequential tool calls (screenshot: ~13), each riding the FULL write gate — whole-set revalidation, temp file, rename — and each costing the agent a round trip. Structural, not incidental: the MCP surface (packages/ops/src/tools.ts) has no batch op at all.\n\nThe fix is also an atomicity fix: the write gate is already all-files-or-none, so ONE op planning N records validates once and lands atomically — today a failure mid-sequence leaves a half-captured subtree behind.\n\nShape to decide at dispatch: (a) add_node grows an optional recursive children field (each child: title + optional note/date/id/mark, ids minted as today); or (b) a dedicated add_subtree tool taking a nested tree. Either keeps one plan → one validation → one write → one commit. Explicitly OUT unless separately wanted: a generic batch envelope mixing arbitrary op kinds in one call — cross-op transactionality is a bigger design than capture needs.\n\nAlso worth a look at dispatch: whether set_desc/set_date on a just-added node can ride the same plan (capture-with-note is the common agent gesture).","see":["mcp"]}
{"id":"precompress-upstream","parent":"upstream-dist-socket","ord":"a0","title":"Upstream: buildSurfaceClient should emit precompressed siblings #upstream","done":"2026-08-12T19:49:20-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11 from PR #93's review (this debt had no tracked home; the convention is an #upstream node, not a PR-body note). One half of the parent's ONE dist socket.\n\n- **The fact:** @kolu/surface-app's freshStaticLayer already NEGOTIATES precompressed assets (.br then .zst then .gz under the immutable prefix; identity when missing; shell never compressed; compressed media refused) — but buildSurfaceClient does not EMIT the sibling files, so every consumer re-derives the post-step; olai's is packages/web/src/precompress.ts (PR #93).\n- **The ask:** a precompress option (or always-on) on buildSurfaceClient, single-sourcing what its own static layer serves.\n- **When it lands:** olai deletes precompress.ts.","see":["load-perf"]}
{"id":"doc-toc","parent":"viewing","ord":"aE","title":"Documents need a table of contents","done":"2026-08-11T14:31:32-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11 by the human while reading his real brainstorming/*.md corpus through the perfected renderer: a long document (architecture notes run hundreds of lines) has no way to survey or jump — \"we need ToC.\"\n\nShape settled at dispatch: heading anchors first (rehype-slug + autolinked headings, sanitize schema admitting the anchor ids deliberately), then the ToC derived from the heading tree at view time — nothing stored. In-page collapsible won over a floating rail; notes never get one (a note is a tree row, not a page).\n\nShipped 2026-08-11 as PR #100 (squash cc899c5): platform `<details>` contents, heading self-anchors, sanitise.ts asserting against the live default schema, six e2e scenarios asserted against the page and mutation-tested by the reviewer, single-pipeline-run pinned by identity, ToC keyed on file. Known trade, disclosed hard: the id namespace hashes the whole body, so ANY edit remints every heading fragment — stable permalinks are a different, future change.","see":["md-render"]}
{"id":"keysbus-upstream","parent":"infra","ord":"a7","title":"Upstream: keysBus broadcasts the full key set per new key #upstream","done":"2026-08-13T19:42:25-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11 from PR #95 (snapshot-scale); a fix, not a receptacle (both debates). **Shipped as kolu #2166 (merged 2026-08-13)**: a same-tick bulk add of M keys now costs ONE keys frame and ONE readAll (was O(M·N) full-set rebroadcast per key), with same-tick membership bursts coalesced; pinned by tests that fail against pre-fix server.ts (expected 100 frames to be 1), and the six pre-existing membership-contract cases untouched. Grok-reviewed clean; complete two-platform durable CI pass. olai benefits on its next kolu pin bump (riding olai#150's repin).","see":["snapshot-scale","surface-mcp-viewing"]}
{"id":"smv-kolu-pr","parent":"surface-mcp-viewing","ord":"a0","title":"kolu: ToolFailure + instructions + title land in surface-mcp #upstream","done":"2026-08-11T13:13:36-04:00","desc":"The upstream gate: structuredContent on error results (ToolFailure), server-level instructions passthrough, title on BespokeTool, plus ToolFailure adopted in kolu mcp's own sendInput — design approved by srid 2026-08-11, in kolu's gauntlet on branch surface-mcp-structured (terminal 7be1fc95). Done when srid merges the kolu PR. PR: https://github.com/juspay/kolu/pull/2155 (gauntlet in progress).\n\n**Now** (2026-08-11):\n- Kolu terminal: `7be1fc95` (Claude Opus, YOLO), kolu repo, branch `surface-mcp-structured`\n- Stage: #review — gauntlet reviews posted, evidence posted, CI settling; srid merges the kolu PR personally\n- PR: https://github.com/juspay/kolu/pull/2155"}
{"id":"smv-pin-bump","parent":"surface-mcp-viewing","ord":"a1","title":"Bump olai's kolu pin to a revision carrying it","done":"2026-08-11T14:38:08-04:00","desc":"olai consumes @kolu/* hydrated from the Nix-pinned kolu revision — an open PR is not consumable. update-pins to a kolu master SHA containing the surface-mcp changes; check-kolu-deps.sh and the root dependency list move with it, and whatever other upstream movement rides the bump comes along (normal). Optional shortcut, pre-ratified: once the kolu PR's API is final post-gauntlet, the PR #94 agent may prove the final commit against a temporary pin of that revision, landing only after the real bump.","after":["smv-kolu-pr"]}
{"id":"smv-gated-commit","parent":"surface-mcp-viewing","ord":"a2","title":"The gated final commit: dispatch deleted, faces flipped","done":"2026-08-11T14:38:08-04:00","desc":"bespokeFrom(TOOLS, ops) carrying name/title/description; olai mcp (stdio) and the /mcp route flipped onto serveSurfaceAsMcp; ops/src/mcp.ts (227 lines) and mcp/stdio.ts (83 lines) deleted. Then PR #94 leaves draft, CI green per odu, review round, and it is fielded for srid's approval.","after":["smv-pin-bump"]}
{"id":"mirror-status","parent":"bugs","ord":"a8","title":"A mirror does not reflect its target's mark","done":"2026-08-11T13:01:24-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11 by the human, seen on the live roadmap: a mirror of a node whose target carries doing draws a plain bullet — no checkbox — where the target itself draws the half-filled box. Screenshot-confirmed on the \"Markdown rendering, perfected\" pair.\n\nPointer for the fix: packages/format/src/derive.ts's Derived.status doc comment PROMISES mirror coverage (\"a mirror standing for whatever its target stores, since that is what it shows\") — so either the #90 rewrite (stored-only status) dropped the mirror-follows-target hop from the map builder while keeping the comment, or the row/checkbox path looks the status up under the mirror's own id instead of what it shows. Same question for the blocked index and progressOf: a mirror must ask everything of its TARGET. Fix per HACKING.md ships with a test that reproduces (mirror of a doing node shows doing; of a todo node shows todo incl. the empty box; of a done node hides under done-hidden with its subtree ONLY per the done rules; bullet target still shows nothing).\n\nDiagnosed 2026-08-11 (terminal a1f792c9, PR #96): NOT a mirror bug — mirrors reflect their target's mark correctly, and PR #96 (+90/−0, tests+docs) locks that promise at both layers so it cannot silently break: every mark through the rows, both halves of the blocked glyph on a mirror row, done-hidden on a mirror-of-done, and the format.md paragraph (a mirror asks everything of what it shows; a see inherits none of it).\n\nThe screenshot's real cause: Now entries are regular nodes carrying see (the Now convention dropped real mirrors 2026-08-10 at 849eb9b, because a mirror's four fields cannot hold the terminal/stage/PR prose), and a markless node draws a bullet — correct under stored-only status. OPEN, a roadmap-convention call for the human: should Now entries reflect their item's mark? Options: (a) back to real mirrors — marks reflect for free, but the prose must live elsewhere; (b) keep see-nodes, status stays a hashtag in the title (today); (c) mark the Now entries by hand — legal under stored-only, but a second copy to keep in sync.\n\nShipped 2026-08-11 as PR #96 (squash d4c7df2): the test-lock at both layers plus the format.md paragraph. The Now convention itself was fixed the same day: Now entries are real mirrors again, with the tracking prose on the items."}
{"id":"log-pretty","parent":"infra","ord":"a8","title":"Pretty logs on a TTY, logfmt everywhere else","done":"2026-08-11T14:31:06-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11 from the human's ask (\"improve logging for human-friendliness, retaining agent-friendly format\"). Today `@olai/log` emits Effect's logfmt through one logger (`Logger.consoleLogFmt` / `formatLogFmt`, packages/log/src/sinks.ts) on every destination — correct for machines, dense for a human at a terminal.\n\nThe design: split by DESTINATION, not format — when stderr is a TTY, a pretty sink (Effect ships `Logger.consolePretty`: local time, coloured level, message first, key=values dimmed after); when piped / under systemd / in tests, logfmt byte-identical to today — the `@olai/log` testlib decoder and any agent parsing depend on it, so the pretty sink may only exist where no machine reads. `OLAI_LOG=logfmt|pretty` forces either way. Zero change to `toStdout`/`toStderr` call sites: the TTY check lives inside the sink layer.\n\nShipped 2026-08-11 in two parts. PR #97 (squash 87cf89f) landed the split; its review completed post-merge — a process misfire, recorded on the PR itself — and PR #99 (squash, tip f4ccd3e) carried the findings: `prettyFor(stream)` reading colour at emit time, wiring tests for both sinks that mutation-testing proved discriminating (three rounds of \"helper tested, wiring not\" before the lock actually held), `NO_COLOR`, per-logger `LogToStderr` scoping, `OLAI_LOG` pinned at every harness spawn site, and the knob documented in docs/architecture.md."}
{"id":"mcp-mirror-op","parent":"agents","ord":"aE","title":"The ops layer cannot place a mirror","done":"2026-08-11T22:13:42-04:00","desc":"SHIPPED as PR #117 (squash 7866e08), AUTO-MERGED by the orchestrator 2026-08-12T02:13:25Z under the overnight pre-approval — the second fully autonomous merge. Gate record: Grok review (six findings, all taken — teaching referenced-retire refusals, Now enumerable via placed/mirrors reads, query tests, master sync), odu 20/20 both platforms at 4d0dd84, CLEAN, roadmap zero-diff, evidence transcript spot-checked by the orchestrator (containment-cycle refusal naming the loop; did-you-mean; placement-retire leaving the target byte-identical). add_mirror / remove_mirror / set_after ship on both faces; ops are LEDGER-COMPLETE — the orchestrator's scripted-edit era ends when its MCP server reconnects onto this build.\n\nDISPATCHED 2026-08-11 (overnight slate) -> Kolu terminal 1bb73fb1 (Claude Opus, YOLO; reviewer Grok, split 11d79c7f)."}
{"id":"chat-node-context","parent":"agents","ord":"a7","title":"Ask the agent about this node: selection becomes chat context","done":"2026-08-13T14:19:23-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11. **Shipped as PR #141 (squash 80e4d65), 2026-08-13.** Both directions: a row's ••• \"Ask agent\" arms the composer with the shown node (a mirror arms what it is a placement of) and opens the panel; on the wire the context is ids only, resolved server-side through the same Query.foundOf that answers read_node, so a context line and a tool call cannot disagree; the agent gets one line under the message — a handle it can act on via MCP, never a copy. Back the other way, transcripts point at rows: chips on the sent message, the node an olai write was about (Applied.id finally crosses the wire), and ids the agent writes in backticks become pressable exactly when the loaded set declares them — marked with the RESOLVED id, so a placement id in prose lands on the node it shows. Pressing focuses in place when the node is on the page (the address stays put — pinned in e2e) and navigates when it isn't. An armed node that has gone refuses the send in ops' own notFound words and the composer restores.\n\n- Pipeline: Grok review found two MUST-FIX (focus-in-place had no pin — always-navigate kept e2e green; placement ids marked with the wrong id), both fixed red-first; all six NICE-TO-HAVEs taken including the gone-node join scenario and the palette's > sending armed context. Reviewer endorsed keeping see/after links as links (\"an href is a promise about leaving\"). Then #140 landed in packages/chat first: master merged, conflicts resolved preserving both features (384/384 e2e together), two-layer delta-verify DELTA-OK. CI 10/10 at b4c72a2 on kolu-ci-1. The prototype artifact was unfetchable; interaction built from olai's own idioms instead."}
{"id":"app-header","parent":"viewing","ord":"aF","title":"A header bar: the app's chrome above every column","done":"2026-08-11T15:40:03-04:00","desc":"The header carries what is about the APP — wordmark, connection, agent toggle, theme; the sidebar carries what is about the DIRECTORY — calendar and the file tree. Collapses the corner-pills special case on the sidebar-less screens: the header is the one home for chrome on every page, error report included (the ErrorBoundary fault card is the noted sole exception). On the phone the burger joins the header's left edge. Settled slice of the layout rethink (take 2): https://claude.ai/code/artifact/cfe8c88b-9011-4838-86fb-7ddd412817af\n\nShipped 2026-08-11 as PR #101 (squash 875dec7). Mid-flight design ruling from the human: the agent pill is a permanent TOGGLE (aria-pressed while the drawer is open), and the drawer's × went — with permanent chrome, the toggle is the one closer. The review round was the day's hardest: two measured blockers invisible to CI (header clipping its pills at phone widths in three connection states; the grid's min-h-full resolving to 0 inside a min-h-0 flex item, ending the sidebar rule mid-screen), plus settle() weakened on a false premise, popover Escape/focus, ARIA — all fixed, re-measured dead in a real browser, and locked with geometry-per-connection-state, short-page height, error-report chrome, and picker-promise scenarios, the connecting one deterministic (holds the socket in CONNECTING or fails)."}
{"id":"wf-styling","parent":"viewing","ord":"aG","title":"Workflowy-exact styling: outline, sidebar, everything #ui","done":"2026-08-11T16:50:23-04:00","desc":"SHIPPED as PR #102 (squash c2dea45; the done mark carries the merge instant). Final head 8c11745: review's 5 must-fixes addressed + 8 nice-to-haves, 20 required odu checks green on x86_64-linux and aarch64-darwin. Six skipped nice-to-haves ratified by the human into wf-gutter-debt.\n\nDISPATCHED 2026-08-11 -> Kolu terminal bb7211d5 (Grok 4.5 high, always-approve; worktree .worktrees/workflowy-styling, branch workflowy-styling). #stage review — PR #102 (https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/102) open: impl commit + hickey/lowy refactor pushed, evidence screenshots verified by orchestrator against the Workflowy references (bullets/halo/hover ••• +triangle/checkboxes/tag pills/margins/sidebar match; Open Sans via nixpkgs→woff2 at build, no CDN). Review DONE (Claude Opus, split 3c57bd38, 2026-08-11): all builds/tests/fonts reproduced green, visual metrics measured against Workflowy (row pitch 22.6px vs ~23, indent 33px vs ~32); 5 must-fixes filed in PR-REVIEW.md — menu Zoom reloads the document instead of router.go, touch.ts gutter table drifted from the DOM (day-view note alignment regression), hover e2e passes with the feature deleted, stale docs, +1 more — 9 nice-to-haves. #stage addressing-review: Grok fixing, then master merge + odu CI (Linux+macOS) to green. Brief + human's reference screenshots staged at .worktrees/wf-refs/ (BRIEF.md, olai-current-dark.png, workflowy-full.png, workflowy-hover-closeup.png); worker's evidence screenshots land in .worktrees/wf-refs/out/.\n\nFiled 2026-08-11 from the human's side-by-side screenshots (olai dark vs Workflowy): make olai's visual language match Workflowy EXACTLY — bullets, checkboxes, spacing, line-height, fonts, tag styling, the outline's left margin/content gutter, and the sidebar tree; hover affordances included. Decisions (human, 2026-08-11, all resolved): (1) COLORS ride olai's existing theme system — the WorkFlowy-derived palettes in theme/palettes.ts already mirror Workflowy's themes, so this item changes shapes/metrics/typography, not the token system. (2) HOVER: collapse triangle becomes hover-only like Workflowy, AND a new ••• hover menu appears left of the triangle. Orchestrator scope limit: the menu carries only actions the client can already perform (zoom, expand/collapse, copy link) — no write/edit path in this PR, the web client has no write ops yet; structured so future actions slot in. (3) BULLETS: exact match — filled round dot, gray circular halo when the node is collapsed (has hidden children), bullet click zooms into the node. (4) FONT: Workflowy's ACTUAL font (identify from their shipped CSS), fetched and served through Nix itself — no CDN, no vendored font binary in the repo. (5) Tags render as Workflowy does (highlighted pill on the tag text). (6) Sidebar tree matches Workflowy's sidebar styling too. Key code: web/src/client/Tree.tsx, Bullet.tsx, Checkbox.tsx, NodeLine.tsx, touch.ts, styles.css @theme, theme/palettes.ts, Sidebar.tsx."}
{"id":"dates-all-views","parent":"viewing","ord":"aH","title":"Calendar & today derive from ALL dates — dated marks included","done":"2026-08-11T17:47:08-04:00","desc":"SHIPPED as PR #103 (squash cbc636e; done carries the merge instant). Final head 58e5e17 after two review rounds and two human rulings folded in: only date + a dated done place a node on a day (the any-dated-mark rule retracted as orchestrator extrapolation, pinned negatively); only set_done stamps (local-offset instant, never bare true/day-only; doing/todo store true); archives keep their days. TZ=UTC test gap closed against the reviewer's impostor. 20 required odu checks green both platforms.\n\nDISPATCHED 2026-08-11 -> Kolu terminal 90cd9d21 (Claude Opus, YOLO; worktree .worktrees/dates-from-marks, branch dates-from-marks; brief at .worktrees/dates-from-marks-BRIEF.md). #stage addressing-review — PR #103 (https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/103) open: impl + hickey/lowy + /simplify pushed; 525 unit tests, 159 e2e scenarios green locally; evidence in .worktrees/dates-refs-out/. Reviewer CLAUDE (human's pick 2026-08-11, overriding the cross-model default) reviewing in a split. Author flags for orchestrator: /simplify added './testlib' to @olai/ops exports (mirrors @olai/format). No CI yet, per pipeline.\n\nFiled 2026-08-11 by the human, on the heels of the done-datetime migration (49d11d9): format.md says the journal/calendar/today views derive from `date` at view time — but a mark (done/doing/todo) may itself carry an ISO date/datetime, and the roadmap now stores completion instants in `done`, so shipped work vanished from the calendar. Requirement, verbatim intent: the views must take ALL dates into consideration, including the done date. A node done at an instant appears on that day page and lights that calendar day exactly as a date-scheduled node does; a node carrying both appears on both days. Day pages let the reader tell scheduled-on from completed-on, quietly. format.md updated to the new truth. SECOND HALF (human, 2026-08-11, same agent): the MCP done op auto-stamps the completion datetime — set_done stores the current instant with local UTC offset, never bare true, never day-only; existing true/day-only values in the wild keep loading and rendering (validated as text; the write path must not round-trip other nodes' values). Whether doing/todo stamp too is the agent's call to justify — the human mandated done.\n\nREVIEW DONE (Claude, split 3e65a774, 2026-08-11): brief met; reproduced 527 unit / 169 e2e green, zone-portable across 4 TZs, byte-for-byte round-trip via a real olai mcp call. Two must-fixes: the branch diff reverts docs/roadmap.jsonl (orchestrator's rebase re-authored master under it — resolve wholesale in master's favor), and the never-a-Z stamp promise is unpinned under TZ=UTC. Seven nice-to-haves. Human decisions 2026-08-11 on the two escalated: ONLY done stamps — set_doing/set_todo revert to bare true; ARCHIVED nodes keep their days on calendar/day pages (dim ok), as a decided tested behavior. Opus folding in, then master merge + odu CI to green.\n\nRULING (human, 2026-08-11, on sight of /d/2026-08-11 in the running branch): the any-dated-mark placement rule was the ORCHESTRATOR'S extrapolation in the brief, never ratified — retracted. ONLY the date field and a dated done mark place a node on a day page / light the calendar; dates on doing/todo marks are ignored by the views entirely (no row, no badge) though they remain valid format. Occasion reduces to date|done, pinned negatively in tests. Correspondingly the stored day-only dates on doing/todo marks in this ledger are scrubbed to bare true (this commit). Opus reworking, then master merge + odu CI."}
{"id":"wf-gutter-debt","parent":"viewing","ord":"aI","title":"Workflowy gutter follow-ups #techdebt","done":"2026-08-14T23:44:54-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11 at PR #102's merge; the human ratified the reviewer nice-to-haves the author skipped (rationale on the PR conversation). DECOMPOSED 2026-08-13 (the human approved, for the night slate) into the three child items below — Kobalte menu primitive + shared dismiss helper first, then (after edges) the touch affordance and the polish batch, since all three touch NodeMenu. This parent is done when the children are.","see":["wf-styling"]}
{"id":"sidebar-collapsed","parent":"viewing","ord":"aJ","title":"Sidebar tree collapsed by default","done":"2026-08-11T18:01:18-04:00","desc":"SHIPPED as PR #105 (squash 163c9b3; done carries the merge instant). The set tracks EXPANDED paths (absent = shut, inverted from the outline tree); reader toggles client-local and never wiped; the open file's chain force-opened without writing into the preference set (pure ancestorDirs helper, unit-tested); e2e pins fresh-collapsed, nested-chain-expanded, and re-collapse when selection leaves. 20 odu checks green both platforms; merged under the human's pre-approval, review waived.\n\nFiled & DISPATCHED 2026-08-11 on the human's ask -> Kolu terminal 5c5481e8 (Grok, always-approve; worktree .worktrees/sidebar-collapsed). Directory nodes in the sidebar file tree start COLLAPSED; a reader's stored toggle wins over the default; the active file's directory chain auto-expands so the selection is never hidden; e2e pins fresh-page-collapsed and nested-selection-expanded. Process, set by the human: NO review round; squash merge PRE-APPROVED once CI is green (tests + CI are the whole gate).","see":["sidebar-tree"]}
{"id":"git-invisible","parent":"bugs","ord":"a6","title":"Git state is invisible in the UI #human","done":"2026-08-11T19:31:05-04:00","desc":"SHIPPED as PR #108 (squash 70f21dd; done carries the merge instant). Final head f3d0a53: review approve zero must-fixes, three nits taken; Applied.why + the Ops.git cell + the header readout (quiet/no-repo/error-with-reason); reconciled past #106; 20 odu nodes green both platforms.\n\nFiled 2026-08-11 by the human, hit live on his vault instance: writes through the agent reported committed: false with NOTHING in the UI saying so, on a directory he knows is a git repo. By design (ops/src/git.ts) the auto-commit cannot fail the write and collapses every cause — no work tree, git missing from the service PATH, staging/commit refusal, identity unset — into the same quiet flag, with the reason only in the server log. The human's ruling, verbatim intent: even if git detection fails I'd expect it to surface in UI — even just 'Not a Git repo' or 'Git error'. Errors may still be IGNORED (never block or fail the write); they must be VISIBLE. Shape: the server knows workTree and each write's committed + git's said — surface a git status affordance in the app chrome (header, near the live dot) showing repo/no-repo/git-error, and let a write's committed: false say why where the reader can see it. DISPATCHED 2026-08-11 -> Kolu terminal b0ff13b8 (Claude Opus, YOLO; worktree .worktrees/git-invisible; brief at .worktrees/git-invisible-BRIEF.md). #stage review — PR #108 (https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/108) open: the boolean becomes values — Applied.why (one sentence on any uncommitted write's reply, additive) and an Ops.git cell (off/repo/none/error + git's words, probed once per serve, republished on refusal and recovery), drawn as a header readout beside the connection pill (quiet healthy, calm no-repo, error with tip). Classification honesty: nothing-to-commit is never a fault; broken-git is error unless git itself said not-a-repository (LC_ALL=C). Non-blocking guarantee kept. 572 unit + 185 e2e green locally; three-state evidence in .worktrees/git-invisible-out/. REVIEW DONE (Grok, split 0c913c2d, 2026-08-11): APPROVE, zero must-fixes — non-blocking guarantee holds, three UI states reproduced independently, classification honest, purity clean; three nits (dead code field, isolate onGit from write failure, e2e for Applied.why). #stage finishing: nits + master merge past #106 + odu CI; then FIELDED to the human — he decides the merge. Reviewer: Grok (cross-model). The ruling is also now a HACKING.md rule (f2bbd28, the human's own edit): errors are never silently ignored — they surface in the UX."}
{"id":"colourful-palette","parent":"viewing","ord":"aK","title":"A really colourful palette, nichely named","desc":"REJECTED by the human 2026-08-11 — work abandoned unmerged. Left unmarked on purpose: not done, and no longer anyone's task.\n\n- **The ask:** ONE new palette in theme/palettes.ts — saturated and vivid against the fifteen muted ones, 8+ visible hue families, niche one-word name, ink-on-paper AA per #69's precedent, picker-automatic.\n- **What happened:** two rounds never reached the ask — coruscant (near-monochrome purple), then prismatic (purple + orange two-tone). PR #107 closed, branch/worktree deleted, terminal killed. (Process had been: no review, orchestrator fields with screenshots at green CI.)\n- **What survives for a future attempt:** the 8-token system caps a pure palette at 8 hue families — each token must take its OWN family (the failed attempts tinted two); a docs page may need a palette-scoped mechanism beyond the tokens (syntax-highlight theme, heading ramp) to read as a spectrum.","see":["theming"]}
{"id":"form-elicitation","parent":"agents","ord":"aC","title":"Form elicitation: the agent's questions render as forms","done":"2026-08-11T20:26:45-04:00","desc":"SHIPPED as PR #110 (squash 4a7ad66; merged 2026-08-12T00:26:28Z after the human lifted his hold; this done stamp is the ops instant minutes after). The agent's questions render as answerable forms in the chat (AskUserQuestion works over ACP), a refused answer keeps the typed draft, plan mode reaches the human instead of silent auto-approval. Review: approve-with-fixes, all findings addressed; 18/18 required checks green both platforms at 9054de8.\n\nAll five scope items delivered, verified against the actually-pinned adapter 0.66.0. Scope-5 tightened: auto-approval is POSITIVE RECOGNITION of session-handed MCP servers; everything else fails toward asking. Carried forward: the silent-errors cancel-path collision, on that branch's side.\n\nFiled & DISPATCHED 2026-08-11 on the human's decision (\"We want that\") -> Kolu terminal 5931d974 (Claude Opus, YOLO; reviewer Grok, split 47b4e8c9). Origin: the adapter presents AskUserQuestion as an ACP form elicitation when the client advertises elicitation.form and disables the tool otherwise — olai advertised nothing; and agent.ts auto-answered every request_permission with the first allow option, which would have silently approved ExitPlanMode into \"auto\" mode. Out of scope: elicitation.url, permission settings UI. Pairs with orchestrator-in-chat.","see":["chat","orchestrator-in-chat"]}
{"id":"silent-errors","parent":"bugs","ord":"a7","title":"Silently ignored errors, inventoried and surfaced #human","done":"2026-08-11T22:34:22-04:00","desc":"SHIPPED as PR #112 (squash c5774a7), AUTO-MERGED by the orchestrator 2026-08-12T02:34:04Z under the human's pre-approval (evidence waived by his word; gate: review findings addressed + 18/18 checks at the true head 77c73b6 + roadmap zero-diff). The third autonomous merge of the night — and the agent held the gate honest itself: it caught that #117's landing staled its earlier green verdict, re-merged, and told the orchestrator NOT to act on the stale green.\n\nThe whole swallow inventory surfaced: cancels refuse honestly (5-second grace tightened per review); stream health renders beside the connection pill; store platform failures are DATA with the Banner reading the REASON; kolu failure reasons carried for mcp-fail-visible; unresolvable images name themselves; clipboard denial visible; refused session lists say so; non-text agent chunks render markers; publish?. is a throw; preferences warn once. Review: all 12 dimensions passed; findings were hygiene + the cancel-grace question, all addressed. /simplify caught two real leaks en route (absolute-path exposure; 60s identical-frame rebroadcast — both regression-tested).\n\nFiled & DISPATCHED 2026-08-11 on the human's ask (a paste that did nothing; the audit that followed found the three reverted PRs — see docs/RCA/) -> Kolu terminal c65479b2 (Claude Opus, YOLO; reviewer Grok, split b9daf69c). Part 1 (paste restore) split out and shipped as #111.","see":["git-invisible","mcp-fail-visible","chat"]}
{"id":"paste-restore","parent":"bugs","ord":"a8","title":"Restore paste-image: revert the mis-titled dd34849","done":"2026-08-11","desc":"SHIPPED as PR #111 (squash 2de0ef5), merged BY THE HUMAN directly at 2026-08-11T19:42:50-04:00 (this done stamp is the ops-layer instant, ~minutes after; review and refactor waived by the human). Grok cherry-picked dc4c078, verified the restored e2e coverage, odu green both platforms.\n\nFiled & DISPATCHED 2026-08-11 (split from silent-errors at the human's direction: separate quick PR) -> Kolu terminal b3e45fe0 (Grok, always-approve; worktree .worktrees/paste-restore). Cherry-picks dc4c078 (the completed revert-of-dd34849, authored by the silent-errors Opus), verifies the RESTORED e2e coverage passes (the tests dd34849 deleted are the regression fence that was missing), odu CI both platforms, PR against master. Process, set by the human: NO review, NO refactor pass — quick; orchestrator fields the PR at green.","see":["paste-image","silent-errors"]}
{"id":"mcp-commit-manual","parent":"agents","ord":"aF","title":"The MCP face never got manual commits","done":"2026-08-12T09:34:34-04:00","desc":"Shipped in PR #116 (squash 324f8b5, merged 2026-08-12T13:33:55Z, human-approved). Discovered mid-work that #83 had been reverted entirely (see the RCA); after #114 restored it, this PR delivered the original scope: olai mcp takes the same --commit=off|manual|auto tri-state as olai web, manual default, --no-commit still meaning off; the commit verb joins the MCP tool table (last in TOOLS, after #117's mirror ops) calling the one Ops.commit — message required, reply reports the swept pending summary, refuses with reason on a busy repository; pending/last/writer-intent readable identically over MCP per the HACKING consistency rule; Applied.why under manual mode reads as expected-pending, never as a git error. Byte-identical-faces test: both faces produce identical commits for the same pending set. CI caught a real startup race (first pending read accepted the unpublished seed and asserted Off against Ready — darwin-only timing) — fixed by waiting for a published value, README teaches the pattern. Final leg: merged past #109/#112/#115/#117/#118/#119; roadmap verified byte-identical to master by blob hash; noted that origin/master advancing by push mid-merge (shared refs across worktrees) can shortchange a merge — the byte-hash check catches it. NEXT: reconnect the olai MCP server to activate manual mode — one commit per orchestration beat via the commit verb — and #117's mirror ops."}
{"id":"restore-84","parent":"bugs","ord":"a9","title":"Restore title-markdown: revert the mis-titled a56cb65","done":"2026-08-11T20:28:17-04:00","desc":"SHIPPED as PR #113 (squash ae59f9e), merged BY THE HUMAN directly at 2026-08-12T00:27:53Z (this done stamp is the ops instant just after; review and refactor waived per the quick-restore process). Inline markdown in titles is back on master, reconciled to coexist with #102's tag pills in one title; #84's restored tests pass beside #102's.\n\nFiled & DISPATCHED 2026-08-11 -> Kolu terminal c7118f67 (Grok, always-approve). Origin: the orchestrator's stamp-commit audit found a56cb65 ('roadmap: title-markdown done (#84)', seconds after the #84 merge) to be the exact content-inverse of squash e1ab412 outside docs/roadmap.jsonl — the third mass-revert stamp from the same morning session (with dd34849/#87 and 12ea294/#83). Inline title markdown had been absent from master ever since, while the ledger read done."}
{"id":"sticky-header","parent":"bugs","ord":"aA","title":"Header scrolls away — must be sticky","done":"2026-08-11T21:35:40-04:00","desc":"SHIPPED as PR #115 (squash df67730), AUTO-MERGED by the orchestrator 2026-08-12T01:35:17Z under the human's conditional pre-approval — the visual gate passed on inspection (desktop scrolled deep with the header pinned; phone drawer from a scrolled page still meeting the header seam). Review: approve, zero must-fixes (Grok drove both viewports, spot-checked the diagnostic e2e by reverting the fix). 18/18 required checks green at f83a527; one darwin FSEvents flake in an untouched package flagged honestly, rerun, passed. 7 diagnostic e2e each proven failing on the pre-change client. Deliberately untouched: the desktop sidebar still scrolls with the page — filed as sidebar-sticky for the human's ratification.\n\nFiled & DISPATCHED 2026-08-11 by the human (screen recording; it showed the page at rest, so the author reproduced the scroll-away itself — header at y=-4963 — before fixing) -> Kolu terminal b2b467d6 (Claude Opus, YOLO). Interacting chrome kept true: #104's drawer/scrim/sheet anchor at top-[var(--height-header)], contents jumps land clear of the bar (scroll-padding)."}
{"id":"commit-whole-repo","parent":"bugs","ord":"aB","title":"Commit UI is blind to edits outside the ops path — .md edits don't show as pending","done":"2026-08-12T13:26:42-04:00","desc":"Shipped in PR #122 (squash 518f4af, merged 2026-08-12T17:25:56Z, human-approved and human-merged). Everything the human ratified at dispatch landed: PHASE ONE — git.ts extracted to leaf package @olai/git (one dep: effect; audit convention as an argument, trailer returned verbatim for ops to classify; RepoState/Reason owned by the git package, format re-exporting on a ./state subpath so the browser never reaches node:child_process; every plumbing subtlety preserved). THE FEATURE — Pending grew outlines/others/served/unpushed; the survey lifted to the repository root (a dirty root README.md shows while serving docs/); dirty non-outline files are path-level rows with porcelain-derived kind chips, untracked included, .gitignore respected; PIECEMEAL commits are a selection of named paths, never the index — hand-staged work untouched, refused-by-name for paths with nothing waiting; PUSH arrived on both faces (Ops.push, MCP push tool, panel button, header pill count via one survey), refusals verbatim, Blocked-naming-the-rebase like commit; --commit=auto unchanged. Review (Grok, two passes) caught two real MUST-FIXes: a REFUSED commit leaked the git add into the real index (fixed — index bit-identical after failure, test proven against the reviewer's own gpgsign repro) and the stale git README; --no-gpg-sign deliberately refused (a signature is the owner's statement; gpg's refusal carries verbatim). Also fixed from review: pill/panel fence unified (a reformatted outline with zero node changes counts as waiting), git-status failure surfaces as the Unusable/git-error face, docs folded into #123's docs/git.md. Side find filed separately: mcp-stdout-pollution. 914 unit + 261 e2e at review; CI green both platforms. FOLLOW-UP for the orchestrator: reconnect the olai MCP server to gain the push tool and the paths-arg commit — then the orchestrator's last raw-git verb (git push) retires."}
{"id":"one-git-indicator","parent":"bugs","ord":"aC","title":"Two git indicators in the header — there should be one","done":"2026-08-11T22:54:54-04:00","desc":"SHIPPED as PR #119 (squash b1ca909), AUTO-MERGED by the orchestrator 2026-08-12T02:54:36Z under the overnight pre-approval — fourth autonomous merge. Gate record: the review's DO-NOT-MERGE round did its job (the 390pt shrink order had traded one wrap bug for another — the CONNECTION pill went illegible); the fix round resolved it and the orchestrator's visual gate confirmed on the refreshed 40-image evidence: \"● live\" fully legible at 390pt both themes, the commit pill the graceful truncator (glyph intact), exactly one git indicator in every shot, verified in the pinned bar. 22 odu checks green at e045c73 both platforms; roadmap zero-diff; conflicts with #112 resolved keeping both sides. The one-indicator claim is now a control-shaped e2e assertion.\n\nFiled 2026-08-11 by the human (screenshot: \"● git\" beside \"✓ committed · 3m ago\"). DISPATCHED (overnight slate) -> Kolu terminal 7184295a (Claude Opus, YOLO; reviewer Grok, split a18ec355). Bonus: fixed a pre-existing 390pt wordmark-crush bug found while shooting evidence."}
{"id":"load-perf-p1","parent":"load-perf","ord":"a0","title":"P1: markdown/highlight off the critical path","done":"2026-08-12T08:24:20-04:00","desc":"SHIPPED as PR #118 (squash 59e4192), AUTO-MERGED by the orchestrator 2026-08-12T12:23:45Z under the overnight pre-approval — fifth and final autonomous merge of the slate. Gate record: review zero must-fixes with the reviewer running its OWN adversarial network/remount script (outline pages fetch no markdown chunk; markdown-bearing pages fetch once; no remount on a held document); numbers verified honest and reproducible — document words-on-screen −30.8% (203.7→140.9ms), fully-rendered +0–35ms disclosed as run-variance, main chunk byte-for-byte reproducible at the head; 18/18 checks both platforms at f44297e; roadmap zero-diff. The upstream ask lives as splitting-upstream (filed by the orchestrator).\n\nDISPATCHED 2026-08-11 (overnight slate) -> Kolu terminal 4a78220a (Claude Opus, YOLO; reviewer Grok, split 43698764)."}
{"id":"sidebar-sticky","parent":"bugs","ord":"aD","title":"Desktop sidebar scrolls away with the page — should it pin like the header?","done":"2026-08-12T11:21:36-04:00","desc":"Shipped in PR #121 (squash 37648ee, merged 2026-08-12T15:20:50Z) — the human's first same-day pre-approved auto-merge: full pipeline (Grok adversarial review: NO must-fix; odu CI 20/20 both platforms at 219d4ae) then the orchestrator's visual gate on the evidence (before: empty column at deep scroll; after: calendar+tree pinned under the header; collapsed rail pinned; column's own scroll independent of the page; phone shots sha256-identical before/after). Desktop sidebar (calendar + file tree + icon rail) pins below the #115 header with its own scroll region; #105 collapsed-by-default and ⌘\\ survive; mobile drawer untouched; #102 styling not regressed. One non-CSS change: scroll.ts gained a bounded frame-by-frame clamp-restore retry (1s / takeover / position-holds) because the page-tall column had been quietly propping up document height — regression fence proven failing without it (zoom_and_navigate 1px short). Review corrected a false PR-body claim (3 'pre-existing' bun failures — actually 878/0 green both sides). Known residuals recorded as decisions in the PR: SETTLE_MS=1000 doesn't close the slow-/media/* restore case (predates the pin); the collapse chevron overlays a scrolled tree's foot (#105's control, newly visible)."}
{"id":"splitting-upstream","parent":"upstream-dist-socket","ord":"a1","title":"Upstream: buildSurfaceClient takes splitting?: boolean #upstream","done":"2026-08-12T19:49:22-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11 by the orchestrator on the load-perf-p1 author's flag (an #upstream ask with no tracked home is debt-in-prose). One half of the parent's ONE dist socket.\n\n- **The ask:** buildSurfaceClient accepts `splitting?: boolean` (today hardcoded false in its Bun.build call), so the code-split of markdown/highlight off the critical path (PR #118) stops needing a second hand-rolled Bun.build + shell rewrite (packages/web/src/markdown.ts).\n- **When it lands:** olai deletes most of markdown.ts. Details in PR #118's body."}
{"id":"daily-notes","parent":"viewing","ord":"aL","title":"Daily notes: a date-named document IS the day's note — detected, composed into the day view","done":"2026-08-12T17:48:09-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #127, squash f3fb7dd (2026-08-12). View-only v1, all four ratified decisions intact: (1) detection by convention — a document whose basename is exactly an ISO date is THE daily note for that date wherever it lives (the vault's Daily/YYYY/MM/ layout matches automatically; 2026-08-10-recap.md, 2026-8-12.md, 20260812.md do not); two claimants list in path order, no conflict rule invented; ONE derivation beside datedDays in format/src/dates.ts (a grep found no second basename date-parse, and loosening the regex turns two tests red). (2) Composition: /d/<date> and /today render the note FIRST through the existing sanitising pipeline (XSS probe verified), dated DayGroups below unchanged; empty days stay inert. (3) Calendar: four cell states (nothing/nodes/note/both), the note fold shape-distinct from the node dot in both themes, note-days clickable, aria-labels carry the two facts (reviewer NICE-TO-HAVE taken). (4) Relative links: .md→.md routes to /doc in-app resolved against the note's own directory even on the day page; ../ climbs verified; a missing target gets the honest nothing; fragments degrade to plain navigation (the non-taking's stated reason verified true). The journal-is-a-query doctrine amended in architecture.md: the note JOINS the query's answer, never replaces it. Reviewer: no MUST-FIX; all four NICE-TO-HAVEs taken (a11y labels, stale Calendar.tsx header line, two-claimants e2e, the two brief-named non-ISO refusals). CI saga: the Linux e2e failures were CI POOL HEALTH — kolu-ci-3/4 run ~30% slower and flake timing-sensitive scenarios on master too (author proved it with branch-vs-master runs on the boxes themselves); green on a healthy runner, 20/20 both platforms, plus one final run after a post-report master merge reset the checks (that waste is what the CLAUDE.md master-merge-only-on-conflicts rule now retires). Human approved 2026-08-12. FLAGGED, awaiting the human: the ci-pool health itself (slow boxes fail unrelated PRs)."}
{"id":"md-editing","parent":"editor","ord":"a6","title":"Documents become writable: create and edit .md from olai","done":"2026-08-13T19:26:25-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-12 on the human's word. **Shipped as PR #148 (squash 10fd1fbf), 2026-08-13.** Documents are writable end to end through the one ops path on both faces: `write_document`/`create_document` on MCP and the web editor drive the same planner → store.commit → audit trail (X-Olai-Writer); the was-guard refuses an external edit (vim mid-session) in words with an explicit \"Overwrite what is there\" (which clobbers the CURRENT disk, verified); a bare calendar day mints that day's note per the vault's own convention (path inferred from the newest daily note); dirty .md files ride the existing commit flow. The review's MUST-FIX — document→document navigation reused the Solid page instance (mint hand-off broken, cross-file draft possible) — was fixed by construction (keyed identity) with three scenarios, measured against pre-fix code (the cross-file draft was the deterministic red; the creation doors had been green by frame-ordering luck). Seven+ sabotage-verified pins; CI green at merge head e14e0800 on kolu-ci-1; 1249 unit, 396 e2e. Recorded, not fixed (pre-existing): fs.watch only sees a brand-new subdirectory on the next probe — the store's 60s backstop covers it. Follow-up filed: md-rich-editing.","see":["daily-notes","commit-whole-repo"]}
{"id":"precompress-dev-tax","parent":"bugs","ord":"aE","title":"Precompression taxes every dev-server start ~20s — recompresses a never-pruned dist","done":"2026-08-12T21:28:58-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-12 from the human hitting it live (\"this compress stuff is slowing down the dev server like 20 seconds\"). CLOSED 2026-08-13 by PR #129's pin bump — the taxed file no longer exists, and the filed symptom is unreachable. Measured on the merged path (buildSurfaceClient → pruneAssets → precompressAssets): cold 3.87s · no-op rebuild 0.96s · changed-entry rebuild 2.18s; dist/assets stable at 19 files over four consecutive builds.\n\n- **Leg (a) prune — paid upstream** (kolu#2159 names this downstream case in its own body): the hashed dir is reduced to what this build produced before anything is compressed.\n- **Leg (b) skip — paid upstream**: an identically-hashed sibling is left alone; the skip keys on emitted bytes, not source mtime (a minified-away comment does not re-pay).\n- **Leg (c) dev-skip flag — overtaken by ruling**: kolu#2159 refused the precompress knob BY NAME (\"the switch would only ever select between 'the layer works' and 'the layer has nothing to serve'\"). Not implementable in olai; re-opening it is a NEW upstream ask against that ruling, not this node.\n- **Residual, intended**: a build whose emitted bytes changed pays ~1.2s compressing the changed files (slightly up per-file — .zst is a third encoding now — while the total collapsed). Bounded, not growing."}
{"id":"editor-op-parity","parent":"bugs","ord":"aF","title":"Web editor expresses less than MCP — a standing HACKING consistency violation","done":"2026-08-14T12:33:42-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-12 from the human's Editor-subtree review. HACKING.md says \"MCP and Web ops must be consistent; never deviate\" — they deviated, so this was a BUG.\n\nPIPELINE COMPLETE 2026-08-14, PR #166 AWAITS THE HUMAN'S APPROVAL (lane `b9cc35e3` alive; reviewer splits idle): see-edges and after-edges writable from the web (widget-family creation via the (( machinery, × removal, acyclicity refusals verbatim and sabotage-pinned), the sidebar mints outlines (op's validation verbatim) — all through the intent seam, three arms on the union, three in the resolver, no side channels. Panel review (grok 0+2, opencode 0+2, independently CONVERGENT on the same two nits; both confirm the deviation closed): union addressed at 9cbb779 — the one-verb prose is three verbs in four places, feature pins added for both new exclusions, SeeRefs.tsx reference corrected, edge-undo e2e'd for after and × removal (the append-order residual asserted as documented behavior), the edge-search step keys its wait on the query. Five sabotages on the evidence page, each red on its claimed pin. CI 9/9 required at 9cbb779, MERGEABLE/CLEAN; 539 e2e scenarios, 1519 unit tests. Evidence: https://claude.ai/code/artifact/2581c0b9-099a-4b44-bb6a-060669ec9d70\n\nChildren closed by this PR at merge: parity-see, parity-after, parity-create-outline (stamps land with the merge). Deferred, named on the PR: the append-order residual itself (a set_see change, not an undo change), per-row createEdgeEditing signals, edgesOf's algorithmic coupling to the planner.","see":["undo","input-widgets","silent-errors"]}
{"id":"parity-archive","parent":"editor-op-parity","ord":"a0","title":"archive: MCP archive_node vs no web affordance at all","done":"2026-08-18T02:55:00-04:00","desc":"SHIPPED for the mouse in PR #124 (`menu-verbs`, squash 60631fe, 2026-08-12); this stamp is the bookkeeping that never landed. The human's ruling that day was SUBTREE WITH A CONFIRM, and that is what the `•••` menu's `Move to Trash` sends — one `archive` edit through the same gate `archive_node` goes through, behind a question naming how many rows go, counted over the SET rather than over the rows on screen (done-hiding drops finished branches from a reading, and a count that read the page would understate what the write moves). NO KEY, deliberately: there is still no delete key in this app, and the roadmap's own \"key only if the human wants one\" was never answered yes. Three doors reach the op now, and all three ask the same catalog (`web/src/client/menu/verbs.ts`): the row's `•••`, the multi-selection bar, and the ⌘K palette on a zoomed node — the one node no pointer could put away, since a heading has no `•••`. A MIRROR ROW IS NOT OFFERED IT (see `parity-remove-mirror`), and what the write did is on the Trash: `parity-unarchive` gave the archive its way out, and #226 took what is put away off every other page. Pinned by `menu_verbs.feature` (offers, asks, the count, cancel, confirm) and `trash.feature`; the verbs and the confirm are unit-tested beside them. Stamped 2026-08-18 by the `web-archive-unmirror` lane, which was dispatched on this node's own stale desc — it still read \"the web has NO archive verb, key, or menu entry\", false since 2026-08-12 — and which committed the evidence sections `move-to-trash-from-the-menu` and `retire-a-placement` (`packages/tests/evidence.ts`) so the pair is photographed rather than only asserted."}
{"id":"parity-date","parent":"editor-op-parity","ord":"a1","title":"date: MCP set_date (set AND clear) vs no web date editing","done":"2026-08-13T00:38:39-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #133 (95c3a8b), squash-merged 2026-08-13. The web sets and changes a date on the row — the picker, reachable from the date badge and the ••• menu (Set date… / Change date…), sending the same `date` edit #124's Clear already put on the wire.\n\n- **Dates stay text**: `input type=\"date\"` chosen because it cannot mint an instant — the ten picked characters are the ten written; the only reading is @olai/format's dayOf. Sabotage via toISOString went red.\n- **One spelling of clear**: the picker absorbs #124's verb — emptying the box flips the button to Clear date and both doors emit ONE constructor's edit, with a two-door divergence test (review MUST-FIX; sabotage-verified so the split cannot silently return).\n- **The agenda is the consumer, proven**: a past date on a todo moves it into Overdue live, no reload — pinned in e2e (isOverdue sabotage turned 9 cases red).\n- **Evidence caught two bugs pre-review** (empty said-box under the menu; Clear date offered undated) — both fixed and pinned; the author also caught its OWN first e2e pin passing under sabotage (absence-wait always succeeds) and rewrote it read-once.\n- **Recorded, not lost**: touch door for undated rows (the ••• menu is pointer-only below md — the checkbox's same gap) and three /simplify skips, in the PR body.\n- **CI**: 10/10 Linux at c186fca; the sick-pool file is now three-for-three (mid-suite kill ~4½min, truncated log, committing.feature untouched by the PR — kolu-ci-2 and ci-3 implicated, ci-1 green throughout; table in the PR comment).\n- **Evidence** (verified by the orchestrator): picker from badge, picker from menu, the before/after Overdue move, the absorbed clear — archived to the session scratchpad."}
{"id":"parity-marks","parent":"editor-op-parity","ord":"a2","title":"todo/doing marks: MCP set_todo + set_doing vs web toggles done only","done":"2026-08-12T22:52:22-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #132 (f5d130d), squash-merged 2026-08-13. The web writes all three marks without inventing a second writer: ⌘⇧Enter is an INTENT (`walk`) — the server reads the stored mark and emits exactly the ops request an agent would send.\n\n- **The ring**: unmarked → todo → doing → unmarked; unmarked is a stop (the field leaves the record), `done` is deliberately OFF the ring (walking through it would stamp an instant, fire the nudge, and hide the row en route to somewhere else — Ctrl+Enter stays done's own verb).\n- **The refusal is met, not absorbed**: on a finished row the walk asks todo outright and ops' verbatim sentence renders under the row, naming the way through (undo first — two ops, the second the person's).\n- **Bug found and fixed on the way**: `inverseOf` applied two-edit undo whenever a mark displaced another; the rule is only for writes that leave a node done — undo of the menu's Clear-mark was already broken by this. Regression pinned.\n- **Review**: NO MUST-FIX, six hunts held, three sabotages red on their claimed pins. NTHs: mirror-disk tautology assertion dropped + ring-inverse coverage widened; the data-tone markup-for-a-test declined (the sentence is the contract via its testid). Menu verbs and Archive pre-exist from #124 — this PR is keyboard + refusal + inverse only.\n- **CI**: 10/10 Linux at the resolved head; the sick-host file grew hard data (base commit fails identically on kolu-ci-3/4 with none of this PR's changes; green on a free pool).\n- **Evidence** (verified by the orchestrator): all four faces in one glance, the walk stepwise, the refusal verbatim, the two-step through it, menu verbs, keys written down in docs/editing.md — archived to the session scratchpad."}
{"id":"parity-see","parent":"editor-op-parity","ord":"a3","title":"see edges: MCP set_see add/remove vs web renders but cannot write","done":"2026-08-14T12:33:37-04:00","desc":"MCP adds and removes see references; the web RENDERS them (edges-ui, done) but has no affordance to create or remove one. Creation wants a node search (the same widget family as input-widgets' `((` mirror search); removal wants an x on the rendered reference or a ••• verb."}
{"id":"parity-after","parent":"editor-op-parity","ord":"a4","title":"after edges: MCP set_after vs web shows blocked state but cannot change it","done":"2026-08-14T12:33:38-04:00","desc":"MCP adds and removes after dependencies (#117 completed the set); the web draws blockedness (edges-ui) but a person cannot declare or lift a dependency. Same widget family as parity-see; acyclicity refusals must surface verbatim per HACKING."}
{"id":"parity-remove-mirror","parent":"editor-op-parity","ord":"a5","title":"mirror removal: MCP remove_mirror vs no web way to retire a placement","done":"2026-08-18T02:55:00-04:00","desc":"SHIPPED for the mouse in PR #124 (`menu-verbs`, squash 60631fe, 2026-08-12); this stamp is the bookkeeping that never landed. The `•••` menu on a MIRROR row offers `Remove this placement` and does not offer `Move to Trash` — the split this verb exists for, since what the reader is looking at is a line standing for a node that lives somewhere else, and archiving from there would file a subtree out of sight on a click that reads as being about this line. The question is asked of the row's OWN RECORD rather than of what it managed to draw (`web/src/client/menu/verbs.ts`), so the two degenerate placements — a chain that died, one that closed a loop — are covered by construction rather than by a fence. THE OP'S FENCE REACHES THE CLICK VERBATIM: a placement something else still names is refused naming what to re-point first, exactly as `remove_mirror` refuses it. Pinned by `menu_verbs.feature` (\"Retiring a placement takes the line and leaves the node\", \"A placement something else still names is refused, naming what\"). The creation half is `input-widgets`' `((`, whose ⌘Z is an `unmirror`; the retire itself is the one write on this surface with no inverse, said by recording nothing rather than by a ⌘Z that would mint a different id in a slot it cannot name (`server/src/edit.ts`). Stamped 2026-08-18 by the `web-archive-unmirror` lane, dispatched on this node's own stale desc — \"the web has no affordance on a mirror row\", false since 2026-08-12 — which also documented the verb in docs/editing.md (it was the one menu WRITE that doc never named) and committed the `retire-a-placement` evidence section."}
{"id":"parity-create-outline","parent":"editor-op-parity","ord":"a6","title":"create outline: MCP create_outline vs no web new-file affordance","done":"2026-08-14T12:33:38-04:00","desc":"MCP mints a new .jsonl outline (optionally seeded with a tree); the web sidebar lists files but cannot create one. A \"new outline\" affordance in the sidebar; naming/path validation is the op's, surfaced verbatim."}
{"id":"parity-unarchive","parent":"editor-op-parity","ord":"a7","title":"unarchive: missing on BOTH faces — the trash cannot be taken out of","done":"2026-08-13T19:05:02-04:00","desc":"The equal absence the 2026-08-12 review exposed: UNARCHIVE existed on NO face. **Shipped as PR #147 (squash 7457d9ab), 2026-08-13**, under the human's shape ruling: the archive got a real web view named **Trash** (file and MCP vocabulary unchanged) — sidebar entry, read-only rows, \"Put back\" per row; the ops verb born once, exposed on both faces; ⌘⇧Z genuinely brings a row back out (pinned, including a retitled-parent scenario added when the review's own suggestion stayed green under sabotage). The review's MUST-FIX (Put back on a minted ancestor husk minted live duplicates — proven by planner runs) fixed red-first at the ops layer: the notASignpost fence (bare + a live twin at the landing) refuses on both faces identically; Put back deliberately NOT hidden client-side, to avoid recreating the faces split. Archive/remove-mirror menu verbs were #124's (verified, not rebuilt; archive relabeled \"Move to Trash\"). Escalated to the human, still open: does the set freeze archived work, or stay editable on both faces (today: editable, symmetric — recorded in editing-web.md's Open)."}
{"id":"menu-verbs","parent":"editor","ord":"a7","title":"The ••• menu learns to write: marks, clear-date, remove-placement, archive, copy-as-text","done":"2026-08-12T14:24:01-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #124, squash 60631fe (2026-08-12). The mouse's first write path: the ••• menu gained five verbs — the contextual mark section (todo/doing/complete/clear, current mark hidden, ops refusals verbatim in the said line), clear-date (dated rows only), remove-placement (mirror rows only, op's own fence), archive (subtree, inline confirm naming DERIVED descendant count — the author's own /simplify pass caught the count reading rendered rows under done-hidden — honest restore sentence since unarchive does not exist), and copy-as-text (tab-indented plain text, target's subtree on a mirror). Closed four editor-op-parity children for the mouse: parity-archive, parity-date (removal half), parity-marks (menu half), parity-remove-mirror. The menu moved to web/src/client/menu/ (NodeMenu + verbs/actions/writes/subtree, unit-tested). Reconciled with #120 at merge: mark collapsed to ONE arm and one resolver spoken by both callers; archive vs remove stay two verbs with different fences; inverseOf extended so a cleared date is restored as the date it cleared; menu writes record onto the same ⌘Z stack. Survived a 10-point adversarial review and two master syncs (#120's 40-file, #122's 49-file). CI saga: two red x86_64-linux e2e legs turned out to be the author's own harness error (odu client wrapped in timeout 600, killing the lane mid-suite) — proven by a controlled experiment on the CI host itself: master control 7/7 clean, branch equally clean once the timeout wrapper was removed; final gate 21/21 contexts green both platforms. Human approved 2026-08-12."}
{"id":"website","ord":"zzV","title":"Website: GitHub Pages at olai.kolu.dev","done":"2026-08-12T12:16:16-04:00","desc":"Shipped in PR #123 (squash a8a0105, merged 2026-08-12T16:14:32Z) — authored and merged by the human directly, recorded here by the orchestrator after the fact. A static landing page in the app's chalk palette (website/index.html, no build step), CNAME olai.kolu.dev, Pages workflow deploying website/ on master pushes touching it. THE DOCS CONVENTION IT ESTABLISHES (binding on all future PRs; relayed to the three in-flight agents at merge): README.md is a POINTER (site + docs/index.md + HACKING.md) — future user-facing copy changes go to website/index.html; docs/ split into index.md, running.md, git.md, chat.md beside architecture.md and format.md; docs/git.md owns the git-integration story. One-time settings the human owns: repo Pages source = GitHub Actions with the custom domain; Cloudflare CNAME olai → juspay.github.io."}
{"id":"chat-drop-files","parent":"agents","ord":"aG","title":"Drag and drop files into the chat","done":"2026-08-12T17:42:12-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #126, squash 4851ad5 (2026-08-12). Drop lands on the existing attachment machinery — nothing new below the gesture: the panel body (header excluded) is the drop target with a visible affordance, enter/leave COUNTED (not flagged — crossing transcript rows is one drag; sabotaging the counter to a boolean fails the e2e), dragend clears a cancelled drag's overlay (both endings asserted), text drags left alone, multi-file drops attach in order all the way to the order the agent reads them. Refusals survive each other: the batch sorts through the ONE shared gate, Chat.attach answers (stored/refused/gone) instead of drawing, and holding.take says every refusal once on the panel's one line — closing a real silent-loss path (file 3 used to rub out file 2's reason; the reviewer proved nothing went red without its test, so the fix is pinned by a take unit test with type+size reasons). MID-FLIGHT HUMAN RULING (the human hit it driving the build): the gate widened beyond pictures — attachmentRejection now takes DOCUMENT_EXTENSIONS (.pdf .txt .md .csv .json) with honest name+size chips, the picker's accept= matches the gate, .svg keeps its scriptable-document refusal, the 50 MB cap stays, and loosening the gate to accept .svg turns four tests red. Two reviewer rounds (initial: one MUST-FIX + the dragend bug upgraded by the orchestrator; re-verify 1 and 2 both \"the PR stands clean\", every fix sabotage-verified). CI green both platforms at each head. Human approved 2026-08-12."}
{"id":"adapter-interpret","parent":"agents","ord":"aH","title":"Adapter interpretation out of agent.ts: one testable module #techdebt","done":"2026-08-12T15:25:48-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #125, squash e7fc004 (2026-08-12) — same day the kitchen-sinks debate ratified it (debates/kitchen-sinks/conclusion.md item 4; filed and dispatched on the human's word). packages/chat/src/interpret.ts now owns the adapter's interpretation as pure functions: allowedWithoutAsking (the debate's shouldBypass, renamed because it answers with WHICH option, never a boolean — positive recognition of mcp__<server>__ against the given names, never the first allow-flavoured option, so plan mode's \"Ready to code?\" cannot silently flip the session to auto), toolNameIn (_meta.claudeCode.toolName), liveModelIn/modelPickerIn/labelsOf (the forwarded CLI init on _claude/sdkMessage + the free-form \"model\" config select), plus NEW_SESSION_META/SDK_MESSAGE/BYPASS_MODE — two strays (id===\"model\", bypassPermissions) the author proved are adapter vocabulary, not protocol (SessionConfigId and SessionModeId are free-form strings; bypassPermissions appears nowhere in the SDK). 22 unit tests, no subprocess — the safety rule is unit-testable for the first time; onPermission is four lines; agent.ts 1119→1033 with all closure state (session lifecycle, toolNames map, model debounce) unmoved. interpret.ts deliberately NOT on the package's export socket. The reviewer passed it with zero MUST-FIX after two sabotage probes (dropping the ours gate and weakening the prefix match both went red) and arm-by-arm equivalence against the old inline logic. Zero behavior change; e2e green throughout. Human pre-approved on the green gate."}
{"id":"upstream-dist-socket","parent":"infra","ord":"aA","title":"Upstream: buildSurfaceClient completed as ONE dist socket #upstream","done":"2026-08-12T19:49:24-04:00","desc":"Ratified from the lowy-electricity debate (docs/lowy-electricity/debate-2026-08-12.md entry 1; all three stances signed). Shipped as kolu PR #2159, squash-merged 2026-08-12, with drishti pair PR #138 green.\n\n- **The receptacle:** \"what a surface-app dist is.\" Both children landed TOGETHER as one socket — precompressed siblings (.br/.gz/.zst) and `splitting` opened, shell never compressed, sibling-skip/prune in.\n- **The proof on the far side:** drishti's repin alone turned its identity-only dist into one carrying every sibling (main-*.js 640,266 B → 173,165 B brotli) — no drishti source change, exactly the socket thesis.\n- **Review:** independent reviewer; one MUST-FIX (the named .zst test was vacuous — deleting the zstd table row left the suite green), fixed and sabotage-verified; review also flushed a real gap (the extra-asset fixture sat under the 1024 B threshold, so sibling growth was never exercised). ts-pattern dep refusal accepted (Record keyed by the union; keeps the pair PR a pure repin). A late ci::e2e-governance red was an allowlist row for httpDrive.testlib.ts (Effect run-edge scan), delta-verified governance-only.\n- **Follow-up flagged, not taken:** kolu's own vite.config.ts emits .br/.gz but no .zst while its server prefers zstd — tracked in the PR body.\n- **Olai's side is NOT this item:** on the kolu pin bump, olai deletes precompress.ts and markdown.ts — the deletion is the installation. Stays in olai forever: Solid transform, Tailwind, fonts, icons."}
{"id":"upstream-readout","parent":"infra","ord":"aB","title":"Upstream: the connectSurface readout that cannot lie #upstream","done":"2026-08-12T19:50:37-04:00","desc":"Ratified from the lowy-electricity debate (docs/lowy-electricity/debate-2026-08-12.md entry 2). Shipped as kolu PR #2160, squash-merged 2026-08-12, with drishti pair PR #139 green (Linux-only per the macOS rule; drishti has no branch protection at all).\n\n- **The defect paid for:** `connectSurface(...).status` could say `live` while a subscription over the socket had errored — olai shipped the bug this permits (a dead documents.keys rendering as an empty directory under a green light).\n- **The payment:** the conjunction folded into the readout (degraded state naming the stopped subscriptions); `pending does not degrade` as the universal default; `needsReload` travels. Event-folded, not walk-per-read.\n- **The cut held:** kolu owns the five states' TRUTH; the app keeps its LOOK. Notable deviation, reviewer-adjudicated sound: kolu's own client deliberately ignores conn.health (per-member onError policy, documented in wire.ts), so the in-repo proof is the surface-app example rail + seam tests, not a HostStatusPip migration.\n- **Review:** no code MUST-FIX; the vacuous dispose test was required fixed anyway (one standard across the batch) and sabotage-verified; two lying doc lines corrected; sibling-prefix readout assertion added. The rail-needsReload suggestion was declined as unreachable dead-path UI (its wire passes retired: reloadForUpdate).\n- **Olai's migration rides the later pin bump, not this item:** connection/status.ts shrinks to chrome (\"the files on disk reach this page\" stays this house's sentence)."}
{"id":"upstream-chunking","parent":"infra","ord":"aC","title":"Upstream: frame-limit chunking beside RPC_MAX_FRAME_BYTES #upstream","done":"2026-08-12T19:25:21-04:00","desc":"Ratified from the lowy-electricity debate (docs/lowy-electricity/debate-2026-08-12.md entry 3). Shipped as kolu PR #2158, squash-merged 2026-08-12.\n\n- **Why:** an oversized frame is not a failed call — the ndjson decoder closes the socket (1009) and takes every subscription on that tab with it. Every surface-app that uploads must chunk, and the arithmetic has been derived twice in kolu's orbit: padi's upload.ts, then olai's surface/src/attach.ts, which copied it \"because re-deriving it is how the same incident happens twice.\"\n- **The payment:** `chunkBase64` + the envelope/budget helpers landed beside RPC_MAX_FRAME_BYTES (the cap's own home). The arithmetic: base64 4/3, JSON envelope, no third expansion, chunk size a multiple of 3 so every 4-character group decodes alone. padi's upload.ts migrated in the same PR — the first copy is gone.\n- **The seam held (grok's cut, all three signed):** content policy stayed in olai — attachmentRejection, the allowlists, MAX_ATTACHMENT_BYTES, the tmp-directory rule.\n- **Review:** independent reviewer, one MUST-FIX — `frameBytesFor` was never exercised, so sabotage there left all 56 properties green; fixed and sabotage-verified both ways. The review also caught the old 26 MB fixture's expected value was not legal base64 (misplaced ceil). Drishti pair adjudicated unnecessary: purely additive export. CI: 48/48 required contexts green, both platforms.\n- **Still owed here:** olai's attach.ts shrinks onto the shared helpers on the next kolu pin bump — a separate beat."}
{"id":"chat-edit-diffs","parent":"agents","ord":"aI","title":"Chat shows the agent's file-edit diffs, trimmed, expand on click","done":"2026-08-12T22:05:05-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #130 (c7f8fdb), squash-merged 2026-08-13. Two previews, two vocabularies, one fold idiom — as ratified.\n\n- **Direct file edits**: ACP's diff block travels STRUCTURED (FileDiff: path + oldText/newText); the panel renders Claude-Code-style — trimmed to 6 rows, unchanged runs collapsed into gaps first, expanded in place on click. The line diff is a hand-rolled prefix/suffix-strip + suffix-table LCS (~100-line walk; the diff micro-deps bring character algorithms and patch application for one function — same trade as @olai/git over simple-git). Bounded: over budget says \"rewritten whole\" rather than a wrong diff.\n- **Olai MCP writes**: never a text diff — the node-level story (marked done / note rewritten / moved) via Applied.sort on the ops reply, derived by the same changesOf that classifies commit-panel rows; SAID/GLYPH moved to changes.ts, ONE table for both panels. The never-rule is true of the FILE, not just the ops path: a direct Edit on a .jsonl routes to the story row (fileKind → OutlineDiff).\n- **Review**: 1 MUST-FIX (no reconstruction property — a diagonal-index sabotage left all ten tests green; the reviewer property-checked the implementation itself over 22 cases first) + 6 NICE-TO-HAVEs, ALL taken including two upgraded (the .jsonl file-rule; docs/chat.md's now-false \"no filesystem\" paragraph). DELTA-OK. /simplify caught a literal NUL byte that made folds.ts git-binary.\n- **Conflict round**: #129 moved master; resolved keeping both docs truths (+2/−2), full suite on the merged head, CI 10/10 at 6d8fafd.\n- **Evidence** (verified by the orchestrator): trimmed card, in-place expansion, set_done story row with the outline agreeing beside it, and the .jsonl fallback — archived to the session scratchpad."}
{"id":"preferences-panel","parent":"viewing","ord":"aM","title":"Preferences panel: themes, done-visible, and friends in one place","done":"2026-08-12T22:49:23-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #131 (999dcec), squash-merged 2026-08-13. One place for the client's preferences, kolu's SettingsPopover shape over olai's deliberately client-local preference.ts — localStorage, storage-event carry across tabs, never sent to the server (the doctrine is in the panel's own footer copy).\n\n- **Contents v1**: Theme (the fifteen palettes as self-colored chips — the old standalone picker deleted, Picker.tsx gone) and Done-visible (the DEFAULT; any page's own toggle still wins — precedence pinned in e2e). Home: the app header, fourth control.\n- **Review**: 1 MUST-FIX — a real keyboard regression: the portal left focus on the trigger, Tab never entered the panel; fixed with focus-move + e2e, sabotage-verified (deleting the queueMicrotask focus leaves the caret on the trigger and the assertion fails). 3 NICE-TO-HAVEs taken (Done second-tab scenario — sabotage-checked as the real hole; chip focus ring on the accent; two stale comments). Two skipped on doctrine, reviewer-accepted: muted-hint AA is the app's existing muted contract, and contrast.test.ts widening to fifteen palettes would be a NEW promise (architecture.md pins AA on one) — both palette-wide calls, not panel fixes. DELTA-OK.\n- **Conflict round**: #130 moved master; one import-block conflict resolved keeping both truths; 345/345 e2e, CI 10/10.\n- **Follow-up filed**: preference-receptacle (six spellings of read→signal→write→watch want one createPreference).\n- **Evidence** (verified by the orchestrator): panel in header, theme flip chalk→pitch, Done row both hints, phone at 390pt, keyboard Tab-into-first-chip with accent ring — archived to the session scratchpad."}
{"id":"agenda","parent":"viewing","ord":"aN","title":"Agenda: due dates as a reading — one overdue predicate, one /agenda page","done":"2026-08-12T20:11:56-04:00","desc":"Due dates got their UX: a derived `overdue` predicate and an `/agenda` page — the forward half of the journal the day pages serve backward. Shipped as PR #128 (ffc1cdf), squash-merged 2026-08-13.\n\n- **The fix as ratified**: no new field — `date` + mark is the reading (`date` alone = occurrence, never late; `date` + `todo`/`doing` = due work). `overdue(n) ⇔ (todo ∨ doing) ∧ day(date) < today`, spelled once in packages/format/src/agenda.ts, string-compared on the first ten characters. `/agenda`: Overdue (oldest first) / Today (minus done) / Upcoming (only days that have anything, headings link to day pages), grouped by outline. DateBadge takes the attention tone wherever the predicate holds.\n- **Rulings honoured** (human, 2026-08-12): `doing` IS overdue-capable; occurrences never in Overdue and never amber; `done` nowhere on the page; undated tasks absent; empty state writes nothing; no snooze/recurrence. One reviewer-noted extension consistent with \"one predicate\": an archived `todo` with a past date is overdue — the archive exemption belongs to blockedness, not to lateness.\n- **Review**: no MUST-FIX across five hunts; both predicate sabotages red; all six NICE-TO-HAVEs taken (isOverdue now slices `today` — the datetime-today hazard — plus five doc/prose truths), DELTA-OK. CI: Linux green; macOS skipped under the standing rule (platform-neutral PR) — which surfaced that olai's ruleset hard-required nine darwin contexts; they are no longer required (ruleset 20608873 edited 2026-08-13).\n- **Design**: docs/brainstorming/agenda.md."}
{"id":"kolu-pin-sockets","parent":"infra","ord":"aD","title":"Install the three sockets: kolu pin bump — the deletion is the installation #upstream","done":"2026-08-12T21:28:58-04:00","desc":"The olai-side halves of the three lowy-electricity payments, all on ONE pin bump. Shipped as PR #129 (d5b302c), squash-merged 2026-08-13. 30 files, net −563.\n\n- **Dist socket in:** precompress.ts and markdown.ts DELETED; build.ts composes buildSurfaceClient's own precompress + splitting; .zst finally emitted (br 189kB / zstd 199kB / gzip 225kB for the 700kB entry); the markdown chunk is one literal import() and first paint still never fetches it.\n- **Readout in:** status.ts shrank to the LOOK; the client.health() walk, unhealthy, readoutOf, needsReload all left; olai reads conn.readout itself (kolu's client deliberately doesn't auto-adopt).\n- **Chunking in:** attach.ts's derived-constants header replaced by kolu's frame-chunking helpers, imported at call sites; the content gate stayed whole.\n- **Review:** one MUST-FIX (the orchestrator's — a roadmap node naming the deleted file; resolved in this beat) + three NICE-TO-HAVEs taken, DELTA-OK; both sabotage hunts red. Beyond-brief: encoding.test.ts now drives every row of kolu's PRECOMPRESSED_ENCODINGS, .zst-arm sabotage-checked.\n- **Flags filed as nodes:** modulepreload-upstream (the 1.5kB unpreloaded shared chunk). The pipeline-<hash>.js naming right (was markdown-[hash].js) is pinned loudly by e2e.\n- **CI:** nine Linux contexts green at final head, GitHub CLEAN; macOS skipped per standing rule."}
{"id":"depth-pass","parent":"viewing","ord":"aO","title":"Depth pass: three altitudes — elevation replaces borders across the app","done":"2026-08-13T11:22:17-04:00","desc":"Ratified 2026-08-12 from the design artifact (https://claude.ai/code/artifact/c22b5168-ecf5-43f3-876e-d32e54c4f344). The flatness diagnosis: everything at altitude zero. The five moves: three altitudes; elevation replaces borders; accent spent not sprinkled; interactive lifts; the paper is the hero.\n\n**Shipped 2026-08-13 by the human's own hand: PR #136 (branch surfaces, squash befa8dd) — \"The chrome sits on a desk: three surfaces, and a type of our own\"** — authored and merged by the human directly, superseding both orchestrated attempts. Attempt 1: PR #134 (branch depth), closed — right tokens, failed the human's visual review on real data (nine findings). Attempt 2: PR #135 (branch depth2, Grok), closed — addressed all nine, orchestrator-verified, rejected in favor of the human's own take. Both branches remain on origin as prior art. Follow-up still filed: quiet-pill-unify. The website depth touch-up idea was DISCARDED by the human 2026-08-13 — no website restyle follows #136."}
{"id":"modulepreload-upstream","parent":"infra","ord":"aE","title":"Upstream: buildSurfaceClient emits modulepreload for the entry's static chunks #upstream","done":"2026-08-13T11:25:26-04:00","desc":"Shipped as kolu PR #2163 (squash e7f0391, admin-merged past the darwin gate on the human's approval 2026-08-13). buildSurfaceClient walks the Bun metafile's import-statement edges transitively and splices modulepreload links into the shell; dynamic edges never followed; no-split entry byte-identical. Measured on Slow 3G: JS critical path 6110 ms → 4095 ms. Grok review APPROVE; all three NICE-TO-HAVEs taken.\n\n- **olai collected it: PR #139 (squash d7cf177)** — the built shell now carries the modulepreload for the entry's shared chunk, verified in dist before merge. The loop #129 opened is closed.\n- **drishti pair: PR #140 (srid/drishti, squash dfcb26e)** — same repin, 12/12 CI green on the re-run (first run's typecheck miss was a workspace hydration hiccup: @kolu/solid-pwa-install invisible on the box, clean locally and on re-run).\n\nAll three repos now on kolu e7f0391."}
{"id":"preference-receptacle","parent":"viewing","ord":"aP","title":"createPreference: one receptacle for the read→signal→write→watch preference spelling","done":"2026-08-13T14:29:06-04:00","desc":"Flagged by PR #131 (preferences panel); ratified as the headline of the 2026-08-13 lowy-electricity debate (docs/lowy-electricity/debate-2026-08-13.md item 1). **Shipped as PR #142 (squash a66154a), 2026-08-13.** One factory `createPreference` in web/src/client/preference.ts: codec per key, one option (`persist: false` — the drag handles keep the live/commit split), `stored()` as the escape hatch fold memory's union-merge reads through. Eight circuits migrated (sidebar open/width, chat open/width/snap, folds, sidebar folders, done default); theme stays OFF per the debate — the boot script owns the first paint read, theme/state.ts keeps the primitives. Claim test grips the call: /\\b(read|watch)Preference\\s*\\(/ in no client file outside preference.ts, its test, theme/state.ts. #138's fold semantics survive verbatim — and are now LOCKED: the review's one MUST-FIX was that deleting the write-side union-merge left all 24 tests green; the fix round added the setFolded-against-real-storage test that reds under exactly that sabotage.\n\n- Pipeline: Grok review (verified the inert resize-nudge claim against master's own Solid, hunted flattening, found only the missing lock), fix round red-first, NTHs: per-setter opts-hop tests + claim regex + architecture.md taken, resize re-fit declined as a behavior change under binding parity. DELTA-OK. Then #141 landed: master merged (one docs hunk, both sides preserved), CI 10/10 at dc5128d on kolu-ci-1."}
{"id":"quiet-pill-unify","parent":"viewing","ord":"aQ","title":"One quiet-pill: the button class string spelled five times across five modules","done":"2026-08-13T17:09:18-04:00","desc":"Flagged by PR #134 (the depth pass); debate 2026-08-13 reclassified it plain cleanup (a class string is not an axis). **Shipped as PR #146 (squash 5c591e6), 2026-08-13**, under the human's pixel-parity-binding ruling: one definition (`web/src/client/pill.ts` QUIET_PILL) worn by the three sites that render identically (chat Header, git Unpushed's dismiss sibling, menu confirm's Cancel — whose bg-transparent dropped only because Tailwind preflight already paints it, verified in built CSS); the two sites with a geometry of their own stay divergent with comments saying why (AskForm's h-8 dismiss paired to the accent answer; Unpushed's py-0.5 Push inside a text-xs line). Claim test: only pill.ts spells the string (sabotage-verified naming both files). Evidence: all 12 before/after shots byte-identical (cmp clean), chalk+pitch, desktop+phone. CI 10/10 on kolu-ci-1. Review skipped on the human's word; merged on their approval."}
{"id":"fold-memory","parent":"viewing","ord":"aR","title":"Fold memory: the outline remembers how you left it #ui","done":"2026-08-13T11:23:14-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #138 (squash 8098561, merged 2026-08-13). Folds are a preference of this browser: olai.folds via preference.ts, collapsed node ids grouped by defining file, keyed by NODE ID so folds survive reload, zoom round-trips, and moves; mirrors fold together per the ratified ruling (one fold vocabulary, fold/rows.ts). Sidebar folders get the same memory (olai.sidebar.folders, inverted — folders start shut). Grok review found one real MUST-FIX — pruning treated a MOVE (archive) as deletion — fixed red-first at both levels plus a write-side twin; cross-tab writes upgraded from LWW to union-merge so a sibling tab's unrelated fold survives. Delta-verified; CI 10/10 Linux green at head. Docs corrected where they encoded the bug's premise. Note for preference-receptacle: the createPreference factory was deliberately declined here as out of scope — it remains that node's job."}
{"id":"website-refresh","parent":"website","ord":"a0","title":"Website refresh: six shipped PRs the site never heard about","done":"2026-08-13T11:13:42-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #137 (squash df23fe3, merged 2026-08-13). The site caught up with #128–#133: a new Agenda section (predicate byte-for-byte with agenda.ts, the row picker, occurrences), Preferences with all fifteen palettes hex-for-hex as chips wearing their own colors, the mark walk as shipped (the ring corrected against server/src/edit.ts — done is not a stop; the author caught the brief's error), chat file diffs, and a swept #format/hero/daily. Grok review: no MUST-FIX, accuracy verified against packages, both judgment calls accepted; two NICE-TO-HAVEs taken in a final round (the daily-notes mock now shows scheduled and finished side by side — a day-page fact the site never had). CI 10/10 Linux green at ddf0dea. Ruling honored: the site describes MASTER; a depth touch-up follows only if/when depth merges. HACKING.md's website/ checklist line keeps it current from here."}
{"id":"owed-moves","parent":"infra","ord":"aF","title":"Owed moves: GitState home, scale.ts home, wire.ts plugs into surfaceWsUrl #techdebt","done":"2026-08-13T14:33:36-04:00","desc":"Three small debts ratified by the 2026-08-13 lowy-electricity debate (items 3, 5, 6). **Shipped as PR #143 (squash 9bd7048), 2026-08-13.** (1) GitState declared once in format/src/committing.ts beside RepoState — the reviewer diffed the deleted mirrors field-by-field against the new declaration; surface re-exports, zero consumer churn; GIT_OFF traveled, and ops' zero-importer public export (verified zero ON MASTER) dropped. (2) scale.ts → theme/ with every path reference followed (build, CSS banner, tests, fixtures, README). (3) wire.ts:36-39 deleted — the dial calls the pin's surfaceWsUrl(location.origin) as a thunk, laziness verified (import reads no location; URL parity checked across real origins). Claim tests: /connectSurface\\s*\\(/ equality with [\"wire.ts\"] (vacuity-probed both directions), and no client file outside connection/status.ts spells the readout's raw states.\n\n- Pipeline: Grok review — zero MUST-FIX, the zero-behavior-change claim held; NTHs taken (three READMEs follow the restory; regex widened). DELTA-OK twice. Then #141 and #142 landed: master merged (one real conflict in chat/Entry.tsx, both sides preserved; claims sweep extended over #141's new files), CI 10/10 at dba732a on kolu-ci-1. The author also caught and discarded a phantom-green first CI attempt (stream events with no durable artifacts) — re-ran and verified against per-node logs."}
{"id":"fault-root-upstream","parent":"infra","ord":"aG","title":"Upstream: a throw must not be a white tab — kolu takes a required `fault` #upstream","done":"2026-08-13T16:36:24-04:00","desc":"Debate-ratified 2026-08-13 (item 4; human approved every PR). **Fully shipped, both sides.** kolu #2164: composition root takes required `fault` like `retired`; `thrownText` printer upstreamed with the review-hardened lost-message rule (`firstLine === named` — the Grok review caught `includes` dropping short messages behind Safari frames, fixed red-first); the boundary records to console before the LOOK. kolu #2165: `connectSurface.url` optional, defaulting via `surfaceWsUrl`, named error before allocation without a `location` (a fix, per Position C). drishti paired at the master repin. **olai #145 (squash 120904b)**: kolu pin → dad81604; main.tsx's hand ErrorBoundary + record + thrown.ts all deleted — the shell rides the standalone `SurfaceFaultBoundary` with `fault={(text) => <Fault text={text}/>}`; Fault.tsx keeps only the LOOK (pixel-identical, verified chalk+pitch with a real injected fault in the packaged binary); wire.ts drops its `url` line for #2165's default. Riders: undoing.ts takes Apply as an argument; bun-test gets a stub location. Missing `fault` is a compile error (sabotage: TS2322). The white-tab e2e now pins the heading olai owns and names its failure instead of a bare timeout.\n\n- Pipeline: Grok review (one MUST-FIX: README described the deleted wiring; three NTHs all taken), delta-verify caught one more stale comment, final sweep verified zero present-tense mentions of the old seam. CI green at 030b85b on kolu-ci-1."}
{"id":"ask-vocabulary","parent":"agents","ord":"aJ","title":"@olai/acp: the ask vocabulary moves to the wall that speaks ACP","done":"2026-08-13T15:25:45-04:00","desc":"Debate-ratified 2026-08-13 (item 2); Position A resolved by the human to fable's wall. **Shipped as PR #144 (squash fbbc15a), 2026-08-13.** New workspace package `@olai/acp` owns the protocol's words: AskField/AskChoice/AskAnswer/AskOutcome/YES_NO on `./wire` (surface re-exports — no consumer moved an import), FileDiff judged same-tenant with the argument written at the declaration, asks/diffs projections + Form/Refused on the main entry. UsageFailure translated at the seam, not moved: `Refused` (a plain value, never on a wire or Effect channel) is minted into UsageFailure in exactly one place (questions.ts) and read in one (agent.ts). The boundary is machine-checked: a six-claim manifest test over one tree scan (position-based specifiers + Bun.Transpiler.scanImports) — no @olai/* import may appear inside acp; SDK runtime imports only in the subprocess files; matcher covers SDK subpaths.\n\n- Pipeline: Grok review — zero MUST-FIX; the boundary judgment verified file-by-file against Position A (and the losing floor position used to sharpen the cut); all four NTHs taken red-first (subpath matcher via the reviewer's own sabotage). DELTA-OK twice. Parity: 1224 unit, 384 e2e twice (local + CI), packaged-binary smoke; CI green at 8afbc08 on kolu-ci-1. One test-lane flake (pre-existing kolu fixture's SDK parse noise) judged unrelated and green on rerun."}
{"id":"store-graduation","parent":"infra","ord":"aH","title":"Leave @olai/store alone until it has proven stable — then decide whether it moves out","desc":"A watch item, not work. The 2026-08-13 design debate noticed @olai/store's source did not change at all during the busiest thirteen-PR week, which suggests it is finished enough to one day live outside this repo. No move now. What is still open is the trigger for revisiting: two of the debaters said \"untouched across two consecutive releases, or 180 days without edits from 2026-08-12\" is enough; the third wanted 90 quiet days AND a second consumer of Store.make. The human has not picked a wording. When the trigger fires, decide then whether the package graduates.","changed":"2026-08-16T13:15:02-04:00"}
{"id":"chat-subjects","parent":"agents","ord":"aK","title":"Associations: things that belong to things — chats, nodes, notes #needs-brainstorm","desc":"The human's want, 2026-08-13, reframed meta-level at their direction: one association mechanism for \"this is about that\" across olai's three kinds of things — chat ↔ node (the original chat-subjects idea: session named `olai/<node-id>` via claude's named conversations, ••• resumes-or-starts, icon on the node), node ↔ note (wanted regardless; likely an outline edge like see/after), and chat ↔ note (collapses to the other two: a chat about a note is the chat of its home node). Brainstorm: docs/brainstorming/associations.md. Awaits ratification."}
{"id":"hindsight-comparison","parent":"agents","ord":"aL","title":"Olai vs Hindsight: address every con in olai's own grammar #needs-brainstorm","desc":"The human's request, 2026-08-13: thorough comparison against hindsight.vectorize.io (agent-memory: LLM retain/recall/reflect over Postgres) with recommendations to address every olai con. Brainstorm: docs/brainstorming/hindsight.md. Six recommendations, all keeping files-as-the-single-truth (smarter things are derived readings): (1) semantic search as a gitignored derived index off the live store's change stream; (2) temporal+graph recall as Query clauses (the graph and dates already exist); (3) capture-as-proposal at conversation end via the chat-diffs surface; (4) consolidation as an agent-assisted stale/duplicates reading, evidence = git blame; (5) multi-agent scoping via per-connection outline/tag subsets + lifting the singleton ACP session; (6) cross-machine sync stays git, auto pull/rebase with conflicts surfaced per silent-errors doctrine. Deliberately not copied: LLM normalization of the user's words, memory-bank identity config, hosted memory. Awaits the human's picks."}
{"id":"agent-model-pin","parent":"infra","ord":"aI","title":"Each repo should pin which model its agents run — written by apm, not by hand #needs-brainstorm","todo":true,"desc":"Root cause 2026-08-13: every dispatched lane ran bare `claude`, whose machine default was Fable — burning the human's Fable limit all day; nothing pinned the model and Claude Code's footer never shows it.\n\n- **What was proven** (scratch-repo tests, valid): a repo `.claude/settings.json` with `\"model\"` + `\"permissions\": {\"defaultMode\": \"bypassPermissions\"}` makes a bare `claude` boot the pinned model with bypass on, and the repo file beats the machine default.\n- **What was wrongly shipped and REVERTED**: the orchestrator hand-committed that file to olai and drishti WITHOUT authorization — and `.claude/settings.json` is apm-generated output; hand-writing a generated surface was wrong twice over. olai: file removed (this commit); drishti: reverted (e987edc).\n- **The real fix must be apm-based** (the human's direction): these repos will adopt apm, so the model/permissions pin gets AUTHORED in apm's source of truth and COMPILED into .claude/settings.json. Solution being designed — see apm-adoption.\n- Interim, in force now: every spawn passes `--model opus --dangerously-skip-permissions` explicitly, and the orchestrator verifies via /status before chartering. The machine default is also saved as Opus.","changed":"2026-08-16T13:15:07-04:00"}
{"id":"apm-adoption","parent":"infra","ord":"aJ","title":"Adopt apm so agent briefs, skills and settings become versioned packages instead of per-lane rewrites #needs-brainstorm","desc":"Raised by the human 2026-08-13 while fixing the model-pin failure. apm (Agent Package Manager) is a dependency manager for agent CONFIGURATION — skills, .agent.md definitions, prompts, MCP wiring — declared in apm.yml, compiled per-runtime; odu already ships its CI skill via .apm/skills/, and drishti already carries .claude/apm-hooks.json. The prospect: the orchestrator's hand-written per-lane BRIEF.md files, the Grok reviewer checklist, and the sabotage-verify discipline become versioned .apm packages, portable across the claude/grok/opencode cast and installable into kolu/drishti instead of re-spelled per lane. Caveats found in its tracker: Claude Code is a first-class deploy TARGET (settings.json, hooks, skills, marketplace) but not a runtime apm can launch; Grok target support merged experimental (#2420). Watch item: if apm gains Claude Code as a launchable runtime with model config, it could absorb the launch layer that .claude/settings.json (see agent-model-pin) covers today. Awaits the human's word on a design pass.","changed":"2026-08-16T13:15:08-04:00"}
{"id":"done-pref","parent":"viewing","ord":"aS","title":"The Done pill belongs to Prefs","done":"2026-08-13T20:53:53-04:00","desc":"The human's call, 2026-08-13, from a screenshot of the floating \"Done: Hidden\" pill. **Shipped as PR #151 (squash 8ae7ed87), merged 2026-08-14.** #131 had already put the Visible/Hidden control in Prefs; this PR makes Prefs the ONE home: the floating pill retires entirely, and the per-page override dies with it (\"a default for a switch nobody can press is a lie\"); the empty-state copy on an all-done zoomed node stays, now the honest explanation rather than duplicate chrome. Rides olai.done.hidden on the createPreference factory — persisted like theme (the human's requirement), cross-tab follow and reload both pinned (reload pin sabotaged red-first: drop the write, reload forgets). Authored by GROK, reviewed by Claude Opus (the alternation's other direction) — the review verified the #131 claim, re-ran the author's sabotages, and demanded the master merge (five files from #147/#148/#152, resolved with the font PR's prefs work preserved). All 15 checks green at c77cd395 including e2e 6m27s; an earlier local readout claiming five undo reds was a load-era artifact contradicted by the posted statuses. Merged on the human's approval."}
{"id":"orchestrator-home","parent":"infra","ord":"aK","title":"The orchestrator gets a real home inside olai — formalize what the prototype proved #needs-brainstorm","desc":"Opened 2026-08-13 after the Fable-limit burn and the compaction-memory problem; brainstorm at docs/brainstorming/orchestrator.md.\n\nUPDATE 2026-08-16 — much of this is no longer hypothetical. Since 08-15 the orchestration runs as a live prototype in docs/orchestrator/: agents.olai (who can be hired and how), dag.olai (the pipeline template a PR walks), lanes.olai (the live board, one lane per PR, every step a node with after-edges), all written only through olai's own ops, with a terminal orchestrator driving kolu terminals and the human ruling at #human gates. Ten-plus PRs have shipped through it. What this item still owes: turning that proven shape into olai proper — agents as nodes, lanes as nodes, approvals as verbs, launch via kolu — instead of a charter file plus one long-running chat. Open questions from the brainstorm: where the launch layer lives (command-in-node vs kolu structured spec vs apm as launcher), and what of the prototype's conventions become product. Awaits the human's picks.","changed":"2026-08-16T13:15:18-04:00"}
{"id":"font-pick","parent":"viewing","ord":"aT","title":"The typeface is a pick: Atkinson by default, a catalog in prefs #human","done":"2026-08-13T19:25:10-04:00","desc":"Authored and merged personally by the human (PR #152, squash a112993f, 2026-08-13, via their own Grok terminal): Atkinson Hyperlegible becomes the default typeface, with a font catalog in the Prefs panel (theme/fonts.ts + FontSelect, fontCss with its own tests, nix/fonts.nix packaging, e2e fonts.feature). Rides the createPreference factory. Known ripple at merge time: #151 (done-pref) went DIRTY on the shared Prefs panel and preferences.feature — resolved in that lane's round."}
{"id":"e2e-parallel","parent":"infra","ord":"aL","title":"The e2e suite runs parallel by default, sized to the machine #techdebt","done":"2026-08-13T20:56:00-04:00","desc":"The human's dispatch, 2026-08-13, from the observation that the ~400-scenario suite ran serial everywhere. **Shipped as PR #153 (squash 823a679f), merged 2026-08-14 on the human's direct order** (\"just squash merge\" — review and remote CI skipped; the kolu-ci network was down and the change is test-infra). Authored by GROK: parallel by default sized to CPU cores, CUCUMBER_PARALLEL stays the override (=1 serial). All active lanes were told to merge master so their local CI e2e legs speed up immediately. Residual to watch: the write-path 15s-wait scenario family that flaked under box load today — if parallelism surfaces it in normal runs, deflaking at the root becomes its own item."}
{"id":"agenda-fire","parent":"viewing","ord":"aU","title":"The Agenda entry burns when work is overdue","done":"2026-08-13T21:19:50-04:00","desc":"The human's dispatch, 2026-08-13, from a sidebar screenshot. **Shipped as PR #154 (squash 78052f04), merged 2026-08-14.** The Agenda entry now burns for overdue work (alarm ink + a count badge) and nudges for today (quiet count), nothing otherwise — the louder face wins when both hold (face order pinned: 3 unit cases). Derived from the SAME reading the Agenda page computes (one situater — the sabotage that froze the reading froze the page too, the claim proving itself), live to marks/dates across 4 liveness pins, on the phone sheet (burger-tap scenario). /simplify caught a real paint bug pre-merge: text-alarm losing to text-ink on Tailwind emission order. 18 evidence PNGs (3 states × 2 themes × desktop/phone/close-up), archived. Local CI 10/10 at 7e72a68 under the flock (e2e 1m50s on the new parallel suite); no background processes left behind. Authored by Claude Opus (model verified at spawn), merged on the human's approval."}
{"id":"chat-restore-wrong","parent":"bugs","ord":"aH","title":"Restart restores the wrong chat: newest-in-directory is an identity guess","done":"2026-08-13T23:12:05-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-14 on the human's report: after olai restarts, the panel restored some OTHER session — boot adopted the newest-by-updatedAt session in the served directory, an identity guess anything fresher could steal.\n\nMERGED 2026-08-13 (night slate, SIMPLE — autonomous merge per the human's overnight authorization): PR #156, squashed at master. The panel's session id is now olai's own note (chat memory.ts, under $XDG_STATE_HOME): written when the panel enters a conversation, read back at boot; newest-in-directory demoted to first-boot/gone fallback. Picker rows carry honest local timestamps. CodeQL's polynomial-redos finding on the trailing-slash regex fixed by counting slashes. Evidence: e2e scenario fails on the old stored[0] behavior, 50/50 scenarios pass with the fix. Grok review: 1 blocking + 2 nits (all doc staleness), addressed at 935d5705; 9/9 required Linux checks green. The superseded-by-hint dead end moved to its own node under Bugs (#upstream #human)."}
{"id":"fonts-in-store","parent":"infra","ord":"aM","title":"Fonts convert once, in the Nix store — not on every build #techdebt","done":"2026-08-13T23:03:47-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-14 on the human's report. PR #152 had put the seam in the wrong place: nix/fonts.nix copied raw TTFs and build.ts converted all faces to woff2 on every client build.\n\nMERGED 2026-08-13 (night slate; the human pre-approved the merge): PR #155, squashed at master 9f1e1761. woff2_compress now runs in @olai/fonts' package-local default.nix (store outputs .woff2 directly, 70 faces); the client build only copies by name; @olai/fonts owns the catalog (typefaces.ts), the sheet (css.ts), hosted.json and the derivation; nix/fonts.nix deleted. Grok review: 0 blocking, 1 nit (~40→70 docs figure, fixed in 5f113a3). Evidence verified independently by the reviewer: store-vs-dist hashes identical, an independent reconversion byte-identical, a clean `just build-client` runs 0 conversions. CI: 9/9 required Linux checks green at 5f113a3."}
{"id":"gutter-menu-primitive","parent":"wf-gutter-debt","ord":"a0","title":"Menu primitive: Kobalte adoption + shared dismiss helper","done":"2026-08-14T00:51:21-04:00","desc":"Split out 2026-08-13 from the gutter bundle. MERGED 2026-08-14 (night slate, SIMPLE — autonomous merge per the human's overnight authorization): PR #158, squashed. The ••• menu now rides Kobalte's DropdownMenu (lazy Dots mount); dismiss.ts is the shared outside-click/Escape helper for popover.ts and note/expand.ts. Grok review: 1 blocking (Escape did not restore focus to the ••• — Kobalte's onCloseAutoFocus effect never re-runs under the <Show> mount shape; fixed with an explicit restore plus a keyboard-reopen scenario, the previously unwalked path) + nits (focus rationale rewritten after browser-verified cause), addressed at ffc7d70. Evidence re-shot: byte-identical or 0-px shots except the intentional caret band (1.765%) and the new 0.075% Escape focus ring. CI 9/9 required green. Unblocked the two sibling items (touch affordance, menu polish)."}
{"id":"gutter-touch-menu","parent":"wf-gutter-debt","ord":"a1","title":"Phones reach the ••• menu: long-press (or another touch affordance)","done":"2026-08-14T03:02:35-04:00","desc":"Split out 2026-08-13 from the gutter bundle. MERGED 2026-08-14 (night slate, SIMPLE — autonomous merge per the human's overnight authorization): PR #161, squashed at 2a4a1665. Long-press on a row opens the ••• menu on phones (previously hidden entirely); driven through Chromium's own gesture recogniser in five @phone CDP scenarios; door.ts's one signal hands out both doors. /simplify caught {...menu.hold} putting the hottest element on Solid's runtime spread path (classList diffed per row per frame). Grok review: 0 blocking, 3 nits — all addressed: the page-still-scrolls claim became a mutation-proven scenario (touch-none on the row fails it, on scrollY 0, and nothing else; the reviewer's preventDefault-on-pointerdown example corrected on the record — touch-action/captured-pointer/prevented-touchmove are what claim a gesture), the title-collision half of swallowGhost() asserted, three doc leftovers fixed. Deferrals accepted by the reviewer: no on-screen discoverability (Workflowy's price), iOS unverified (-webkit-touch-callout in the sheet, no real Safari in CI). Evidence: byte-identical laptop shots, only the held-phone shot differs. CI 9/9 required green at 2a4a1665.","after":["gutter-menu-primitive"]}
{"id":"gutter-menu-polish","parent":"wf-gutter-debt","ord":"a2","title":"Menu polish: copy-link toast, z-index plan, NodeMenu file split","done":"2026-08-14T04:11:18-04:00","desc":"Split out 2026-08-13 from the gutter bundle. MERGED 2026-08-14 (night slate, SIMPLE — autonomous merge per the human's overnight authorization; the last of the nine): PR #163, squashed at b317637. Delivered: the copy-link/copy-text toast rides src/client/saying.ts, one SAID_MS countdown shared with the Trash remark; a real two-band z-index scale (page stack kept, sealed numbers remapped) with the no-other-file-spells-a-z-* sweep in claims.test.ts; NodeMenu split along the seams the Kobalte adoption exposed (MenuSaid.tsx named like UndoSaid.tsx). Grok review: 0 blocking, 2 nits (stale doc pointers after the sweeps moved) — author fixed three spots, verifying pointers against reality. Evidence: 5 byte-identical layered-chrome scenes vs master; 2 sub-pixel diffs proven run-to-run flake by identical signatures on the same build. CI 9/9 required green at b317637. The sibling debts menu-bundle-cost and dismiss-layer-stack remain recorded, untouched.","after":["gutter-menu-primitive"]}
{"id":"superseded-hint-upstream","parent":"bugs","ord":"aI","title":"Superseded-by hint has no protocol source #upstream #human","todo":true,"desc":"Measured during PR #156 (2026-08-13): the pinned Claude Code adapter's listSessions answers {sessionId, cwd, title, updatedAt} and sets no _meta at all, and ACP's SessionInfo carries no message count — so the roadmap's \"adapter-supplied superseded hint, only where an adapter offers it\" has nothing to read today, and per-row counts have no protocol source. Any fix is upstream (adapter _meta, or ACP SessionInfo). AWAITS THE HUMAN'S RATIFICATION before anything is filed on another repo."}
{"id":"menu-bundle-cost","parent":"wf-gutter-debt","ord":"a3","title":"Kobalte DropdownMenu rides the first-paint chunk (~24 kB brotli) #techdebt","done":"2026-08-14T21:31:30-04:00","desc":"Surfaced by PR #158 (2026-08-13): adopting Kobalte's DropdownMenu added ~80 kB raw / ~23 kB brotli to the first-paint chunk (the node's ~85/24 title figures were the pre-remeasure numbers).\n\nMERGED 2026-08-14 (night slate, autonomous merge per the human's overnight authorization): PR #171, squashed at head 3ae28cd. menu/chunk.ts is the entry-import() split (markdown/chunk.ts's pattern); Panel.tsx and Confirm.tsx follow the primitive into the chunk; src/client/arriving.ts (createArrival) is the shared arrival receptacle for both chunk files; features/menu_arrives.feature pins WHEN the bytes are fetched over the network (tests/support/chunks.ts shared with the markdown steps). Review: Grok 4 nits + opencode 3 nits, zero blocking, all addressed as docstring/test-only commits — emitted client byte-identical, so the bundle table, six byte-identical screenshots and network trace held without re-shooting. CI 9/9 required green at 3ae28cd.\n\nResidue recorded, deliberately not taken: dismiss.ts:53 keeps ~10 kB raw / ~3 kB brotli of Kobalte on first paint (20+ first-paint panels import it — reviewer-verified not removable); no hover prefetch (a second ask on a different trigger; menu/Dots.tsx is where it would go); chunk-failure sentence has no countdown (matches Markdown.tsx's decision). The stray e2e flake grok attributed to input_widgets.feature:209 got its own bug node."}
{"id":"dismiss-layer-stack","parent":"wf-gutter-debt","ord":"a4","title":"Dismiss is not one layer stack: Escape with popover + menu up shuts both #techdebt","done":"2026-08-14T23:44:50-04:00","desc":"Surfaced by PR #158 (2026-08-13): dismiss.ts's two callers were not on Kobalte's DismissableLayer stack, so Escape with both a popover and the menu open closed both instead of the topmost; chat/Sessions.tsx had no dismissal at all.\n\nMERGED 2026-08-14 (night slate, autonomous merge per the human's overnight authorization): PR #172, squashed at efe934b5. topmost.ts is the one stack — popover, menu, Sessions AND the ⌘K palette (found off-discipline by the author's own altitude pass: whileEditing:true opened it over popovers and one Escape took both), with the un-enforceable rule written where the stack lives: a layer answers a dismissal from the document or later, never from its own box. Review: Grok + opencode, zero blocking; opencode walked pairs the e2e doesn't (triples, phone viewport); all five nits fixed as isolated commits including the reverse-order dismiss_stack scenario. CI 9/9 required green at efe934b5. Two menu scenarios that fail on master pass here.\n\nResiduals on the record: palette/Shortcuts.tsx modal still ignores Escape (missing affordance, Sessions' old class — both reviewers concurred it is its own item); SlashMenu's capture handler taking Enter aimed elsewhere (pre-existing, judged against the new rule during review); mobile scrims' one-tap-two-things (both reviewers walked it: the picker rides inside the sheet's dismissal because it is IN the sheet — lawful); no unit test for topmost.ts (Solid's server build does not propagate signal→memo — held by e2e and two claims.test.ts sweeps, arriving.ts's precedent, Grok-endorsed)."}
{"id":"undo-feature-flaky","parent":"bugs","ord":"aJ","title":"undo.feature races a write against the assertion after it #techdebt","done":"2026-08-14T09:00:48-04:00","desc":"The e2e flake was one mistake in six places: a write goes out through a DRAFT, the draft is the only receipt this tab gives, and no key step waited for it.\n\nMERGED 2026-08-14 (PR #164, the human approved, squashed at 96b1dfb): I press / I type / I select all and type / I click away now wait for the caret to be in the line before aiming and to leave the PLACE (Row.key: chain + seat) after — or for the page to say why not; 'I press … without waiting' remains for the two scenarios that mean the race. Two disk assertions fixed: waiting readers go through world.servedNodesSoFar (ENOENT and nothing else — a non-JSON line still faults), and the holding-negative 'holds no node titled' split from its waiting twin (five holding uses remain, listed on the PR). Proof: 5/5 undo runs and 3/3 five-feature runs failed under load before; 12/12 green after, 12/12 again post-/simplify, 3/3 after the review round; no timeout raised, no retry added, no claim loosened — red runs now name what they waited for (mutant-proven wording). CI 9/9 required at 96b1dfb.\n\nTwo product findings reported, not fixed, each its own node under Bugs: new-line-blur-caret and client-quiescence (#human).\n\nMERGE-ORDER NOTE for the open PRs: #159 and #162 each carry their own copies of the two disk fixes; whichever lands after this conflicts textually in editing_steps.ts — keep THIS reader (servedNodesSoFar, ENOENT only) and one copy of the step; their local recordsIn blanket-catch swallows malformed records and must not survive."}
{"id":"client-quiescence","parent":"bugs","ord":"aK","title":"The client publishes no quiescence: a refused key is indistinguishable from the last #human","todo":true,"desc":"FINDING from PR #164 (2026-08-14): structural re-focuses the row and collapses its selection after a write answers (RowEditor.tsx's takeCaret), and a refused key redraws nothing — so a second refusal is indistinguishable from the first, and no harness can know a refused key has been answered. Under load this landed inside the next gesture (keyboard_editing.feature:264: ⌘A collapsed mid-retype, title prepended instead of replaced — twice in ~30 loaded runs before #164's move-key receipts, not since). #164's receipts cover every key that MOVES the caret; keys that redraw a row without moving it (Control+Enter and the walk beside it) still have no receipt, and the module says so. The honest fix is the product publishing its own quiescence — a counter bumped when a key is enqueued, cleared when the queue drains — which covers all keys at once. A product change made for tests: THE HUMAN'S CALL."}
{"id":"new-line-blur-caret","parent":"bugs","ord":"aL","title":"Clicking away from a brand-new line leaves the caret in the row it just made","done":"2026-08-15T16:47:25-04:00","desc":"FINDING from PR #164's era (2026-08-14): clicking away from a brand-new line left the caret in the row it just made. MERGED 2026-08-15 (pre-approved on the orchestrator's visual verification): PR #187, squashed at 4d031cb. Root cause exactly the node's hypothesis: every caret keeps its address for life EXCEPT a brand-new line's — the write that lands it mints the row's id, so the blur guard read \"the reader moved\" about a reader who never did. Fix: the landed row carries the slot it was typed at (Editing.was — one writer, one reader, always-a-row forwards nothing, sabotage-proven), blur asks stillAt. Three red-first scenarios across all three anchors; the disclosed timing window (click inside IDLE_COMMIT or the idle timer masks the bug) proven asymmetric by review — a slow runner gets a loud timeout, never a silent pass. Reviews: Grok + opencode, zero blocking, zero nits. The orchestrator verified the before/after frames by eye: an open editor where the click should have landed, then nothing being typed."}
{"id":"multiselect-completion","parent":"editor","ord":"a9","title":"Picking completed: drag-across marquee, edge auto-scroll, touch drag","done":"2026-08-14T16:33:15-04:00","desc":"The deferrals PR #159 named, taken up 2026-08-14 at the human's word. MERGED 2026-08-14 (PR #169, the human approved, squashed at e9adceb): drag-across marquee (allowlist not preventDefault; band not box; the flat page gains its rail at the ul so depth 0 sweeps like everywhere else), edge auto-scroll (window decides whether, page decides where; still pointer keeps scrolling), touch drag (long-press on the bullet lifts the row; scroll claimed at the deadline with non-passive touchmove, not touch-action:none; the mutation-proven fence green with a sister scenario). Design ruling shipped panel-converged: the bullet is the one drag handle on all devices (both reviewers independently recommended it). Panel grok 0+2 / opencode 0+3, union addressed; the userSelect single-writer premise is a claims.test.ts sweep. CI 9/9 at e9adceb; 551 scenarios / 4110 steps. Still deferred: the delete key (the human's ruling), tone/role unification, sweep-union modifier, finger-sweep (permanent no)."}
{"id":"reads-on-the-floor","parent":"surface-mcp","ord":"a1","title":"Query-shaped reads move to the format floor","done":"2026-08-14T22:41:42-04:00","desc":"Deferral from PR #167 (2026-08-14), recorded with THREE traps: (1) the shared read-request vocabulary must go to @olai/format, NOT @olai/ops; (2) Applied cannot be included — the two sides are deliberately different types; (3) an Outline schema in format/searching.ts would collide with the Outline the package already exports from set.ts:62 via index.ts.\n\nMERGED 2026-08-14 (night slate, autonomous merge per the human's overnight authorization): PR #170, squashed at head 95d69fc — a pure refactor by the end. All three traps respected. The drift property proven by experiment and extended twice by the review round: the `as SearchHit` cast became an annotation (a SearchHit-only field now fails ops+web+server where it compiled clean before), and NodeContext borrows Found's field schemas (dropping Found.path now fails @olai/chat too). Review: Grok + opencode, zero blocking, every nit fixed or answered on the record; all commits verified standalone. CI 9/9 required green at 95d69fc.\n\nThe one behavior change the branch had carried — trimming list_outlines' torn-file answer — was REVERTED before merge (nothing unratified lands overnight) and became its own #human node. The three concurred declines became their own #techdebt nodes: the four-way {file,line} spelling, read()'s request-half inference, outlines()'s O(files × nodes) walk."}
{"id":"per-face-expose","parent":"agents","ord":"aB","title":"Upstream: serveSurfaceApp cannot scope a verb per face #upstream #human","done":"2026-08-15T09:03:32-04:00","desc":"Deferral from PR #167 (2026-08-14): the 18 write tools have no surface procedure, and giving them one puts the ops request vocabulary on the surface — where serveSurfaceApp took handlers whole with no expose list, so every open tab could call every verb.\n\nDONE 2026-08-15: the human approved and kolu PR https://github.com/juspay/kolu/pull/2170 is squash-merged at bfb4eb56 — @kolu/surface/expose gives every serving face the same ExposeMap grammar (classifyExpose), a checked FaceExposure at the handler seam, SurfaceMemberNotExposed as the per-request refusal. The night's review round contributed one real fix (Object.prototype keys no longer listable as members). Drishti pair srid/drishti#142 re-pinned to bfb4eb56 (tarball byte-identical to the reviewed tree) and MERGED on the human's word; lane torn down clean.\n\nIssue https://github.com/juspay/kolu/issues/2169 STAYS OPEN for the writable-primitive-on-gated-face spelling — the PR's `Closes #2169` auto-closed it at merge and the author reopened it with the landed-vs-open split written out. LESSON, worth a HACKING line someday: a multi-slice issue wants \"Part of #n\" in PR bodies, never \"Closes #n\". Two framework-shape questions stay recorded on #2170 for srid (one expose currency across faces; expose-required + exposeEverything), cheapest while a drishti gate is open. WHAT THIS UNBLOCKED: mcp-bridge, dispatched the same day."}
{"id":"mcp-bridge","parent":"agents","ord":"aBV","title":"The bridge shape: an external agent attaches to the RUNNING olai","done":"2026-08-15T11:16:32-04:00","desc":"Deferral from PR #167 (2026-08-14). UNBLOCKED 2026-08-15 by kolu #2170 (bfb4eb56); ratified as ONE PR, three stages.\n\n**Now** (2026-08-15):\n- Kolu terminal: `cd13fd33` (Claude Opus, YOLO), olai repo, branch `mcp-bridge`\n- PR: #175 — pin bump; ALL write verbs (19, not the node's 18 — md-editing postdates it, noted in code) as surface procedures, MCP face exposed, browser face denied; the bridge: `olai mcp` attaches to a running server over the unix socket, boots its own store only when none runs. Design doc: docs/brainstorming/mcp-bridge.md\n- AUDIT CORRECTION carried honestly: the 418 MB two-store price was misread — an olai mcp over an EMPTY directory is already 213.8 MiB (Bun runtime, not corpus); memory does not halve; the fd story stays with bun-watcher-bump (the bridge's remaining 1048 descriptors are the watcher's)\n- Decisions for srid at fielding: writer became a per-FACE binding (ops.commit twin deleted — it existed only to carry the field and had drifted on its error channel); the three READS became procedures too (stage 3 could not leave them behind)\n- Deferred on the record: socket.ts:86 realpath vs directory.ts:16 string-trim — two canonicalisations of \"which directory\" (symlink → same socket, different chat memory), pre-existing, not widened; chat-agent face's writer has no e2e trailer test (fenced only, same as before); socket latency unmeasured, said so in the doc\n- Stage: #review — Grok (`dc5279c5`) and opencode (`0be27fed`), briefed to run the DRY drift experiment themselves, re-verify the 213.8 MiB baseline, and attack the canonicalisation seam\n- CI: 8/9 at 47c5d52c with e2e finishing under the author's monitor (10/10 at both preceding heads)\n- Then: FIELDED to the human — the orchestrator merges only on his word","after":["per-face-expose"]}
{"id":"watcher-fd-cost","parent":"surface-mcp","ord":"a4","title":"The recursive watcher holds one fd per served file, per store, for life #techdebt","done":"2026-08-14T23:32:02-04:00","desc":"FINDING from PR #167's two-store measurement (2026-08-14): the store's recursive watcher holds one open fd per served file, per store, for the process's lifetime — 14 fds with watch:false vs 1050 with watch:true on a 1020-file vault.\n\nCLOSED 2026-08-14 (night slate) as an investigation, per the human's small-or-stop ruling — NO CODE, one doc: PR #173, squashed at df2a646, docs/brainstorming/watcher-fd-cost.md. The cost is BUN'S: 1.3.13's fs.watch routes through Bun's internal bundler watcher, one open(2) per path under the ROOT (.git and node_modules included — the real multiplier); bun 1.3.14's rewrite onto inotify/FSEvents/kqueue drops it to ~15 descriptors and closes the post-boot-subdir blind spot. Per-directory watching, the brief's own proposal, measured at identical cost on 1.3.13 — no small fix existed. Both reviewers reproduced every conclusion independently (fact-check round: nits only, all re-measured and folded, one honest 13-fd run-divergence explained in the doc; grok's intelligence about nixpkgs#519796 being held on a bun-compile SIGSEGV olai wouldn't hit is left in the review record). Successor nodes: bun-watcher-bump (#human, the ruling) and watcher-postboot-blind (the bug the bump also fixes)."}
