{"id":"semantic-recall-parked","parent":"someday","ord":"a0","title":"Semantic recall — PR #165 drafted and parked","desc":"Parked 2026-08-14 at the human's word (\"I don't want to actively do it now\") with the pipeline COMPLETE: panel-reviewed (grok 0+5, opencode 0+4, union addressed at b3721c2), CI 9/9 there, evidence and design doc done (docs/brainstorming/semantic-recall.md — llama-cpp + bge-small nix-native, +196 MB, exact-first, ≈ marker, byte-for-byte off-pin; measured 0% CPU idle, ~3 ms/warm query, 71–84 MB RSS).\n\nState: PR #165 converted to DRAFT, open. Lane `cfad244a` (worktree `.worktrees/semantic-recall`, branch `semantic-recall`) left running at the human's word; reviewer splits killed.\n\nTO RESUME: (1) merge master and re-fit — the search seam moved twice since (#167's format/searching.ts declaration, #168's filter grammar); (2) the 2026-08-14 debate ratified two RE-FIT OBLIGATIONS that bind at merge: `matched: \"meaning\"` declared on the format floor as optionalKey([...SEARCH_FIELDS, \"meaning\"]) with the PR's local surface schema deleted, and `searchWith` consuming the parsed filter (refused query keeps `refusals` and does not fill; no operator tokens as prose to the embedder; paraphrase hits honor is:/file/under — the recall leg gets the residual text); (3) mark ready, re-run CI, field for approval. Related but independent: the ratified @olai/child receptacle (debate ledger, local debates/lowy-electricity-2026-08-14/conclusion.md) would absorb embedder.ts:509's swallowed exec error."}
{"id":"child-receptacle","parent":"infra","ord":"aN","title":"One shared owner for starting and stopping subprocesses (@olai/child) — awaiting your go-ahead #human","todo":true,"desc":"The 2026-08-14 debate's ratified yield (three debaters, all-three signatures; the precise ledger is local at debates/lowy-electricity-2026-08-14/conclusion.md — the docs/ write-up was deleted at the human's word). AWAITS THE HUMAN'S RATIFICATION before dispatch. The bundle:\n\n1. **A workspace leaf `@olai/child`** owning the subprocess plumbing spelled three times (chat/pipes.ts, @kolu/detect's private copy, recall/embedder.ts): spawn, the exec-error race as typed `unstartable`, drained stderr with the caller naming the sink, kill-with-scope (SIGTERM → grace → SIGKILL). Explicitly OUT (unanimous): readiness, transport, restart policy, death-LOOK. Three deletions ride it: embedder.ts:509's swallowed exec error (a bad OLAI_EMBED_SERVER should name the file, not time out on /health); agent.ts's graceless bare kill() on clean stop; pipes.ts's stale two-callers claim → claim test.\n2. **Sidecar build unification**: scripts/embedder.sh is scripts/acp-agent.sh re-typed (its own comment confesses); one nix helper + one scripts/sidecar.sh replace both. The human's stronger shape from review: nix/sidecars/<name>/default.nix per sidecar (fonts spirit), the script shrinking to one generic lazy-loader for the dev loop only — the packaged binary is already fully baked.\n3. **Two fences**: only recall/ reads the embedder env vars (gripping the read, scoped to packages/); only recall/ spells the cache path/tmp-rename.\n4. **The kill-9 experiment** (decides whether the orphan sweep joins the leaf): nix-built serve, chat panel open, kill -9 the olai pid, check ps for the adapter. Dies → sweep stays llama-own as ratified; survives → sweep is shared and a 589 MB orphan is the strongest argument.\n5. **detect's private unstartable**: ruled leftover-of-a-paid-socket; disposition open (export from @kolu/detect / wait for the leaf / leave 15 lines). Nothing filed upstream without the human.","changed":"2026-08-16T13:15:23-04:00"}
{"id":"sidebar-pierre-tree","parent":"viewing","ord":"aV","title":"The directory tree gets file icons: adopt @pierre/trees, Workflowy-exact or fall back to icons-only","done":"2026-08-15T00:22:04-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-14 (the human, from a sidebar screenshot: markdown files, olai outlines and folders indistinguishable). RULED the same night: adopt `@pierre/trees` with a pre-ratified icons-only escape hatch.\n\nMERGED 2026-08-15 (night slate, autonomous merge per the human's overnight authorization): PR #174, squashed at 8ea77c16 — THE HATCH, taken on evidence, not on the expected wall. Styling pierced the shadow DOM fine; pierre was mounted inside the real sidebar and declined on four measured walls, each reproduced by both reviewers (grok's 62 kB figure a byte-identical rebuild): rows are never anchors (this column's every entry is a real <a> Link); virtualized fixed heights truncate where fileTree.ts wraps; an internal scroller against the one-scroll-region rule; 252,776 raw / 62,250 brotli of Preact on a first-paint surface. Fifth, for whoever tries again: expansion has no event and no getExpandedPaths — fold persistence would poll.\n\nDelivered: client/file/icons.tsx — folder + document vendored VERBATIM from pierrecomputer/icons (Apache-2.0, attribution + the when-a-real-NOTICE-is-owed rule in the header), the olai outline drawn to the set's own metrics; currentColor so accent/muted rides the row's ink; the rail's divergent icons unified (the app had two answers to \"what does an outline look like\" — a sabotage-proven scenario now pins both faces). +2,009 raw / +776 brotli at the merged head, same-base rule now stated beside the table in packages/web/README.md. Review: zero blocking, five nits all fixed or answered on the record. CI 9/9 required green at 8ea77c16; e2e 590 scenarios at the merged head."}
{"id":"input-widgets-escape-flake","parent":"bugs","ord":"aM","title":"input_widgets.feature:209 flakes: Escape sometimes fails to close the editor draft #e2e","done":"2026-08-15T17:39:32-04:00","desc":"Caught loose during PR #171's night run (2026-08-14), grok's attribution; bit at least five separate runs across the week. HUNTED 2026-08-15 (Grok author, terminal `06697db6`): THE FLAKE WAS REAL, AND DOUBLE — (1) Escape could resurrect the draft it had just dismissed (isolated repro red on run 3/200, state captured); (2) with that fixed alone, a too-early ⌘Z still tripped it at run 150. Both fixed in PR #190; proof discipline: both-fixes 300/300 on :209, neighbors 100/100, full suite clean, unit kept(null, held) red-then-green; CI 9/9 at d3e6a75. Stage: #review — opencode SOLE reviewer per the human (`91e57e62`), briefed to re-derive both mechanisms from the captured states and loop the proof at its own timing. FIELDED to srid; merge is his word."}
{"id":"torn-file-answer-trim","parent":"agents","ord":"aM","title":"list_outlines answers a torn file with dead zeros — trim to {file, unreadable}? #human","todo":true,"desc":"Raised inside PR #170 (2026-08-14 night slate): an unreadable outline currently answers {file, nodes: 0, roots: [], unreadable} — the zero fields are noise beside the flag. The PR briefly changed it to {file, unreadable}; both reviewers verified both in-tree consumers read .file only; the orchestrator had it reverted before the overnight merge because no unratified behavior change lands while the human sleeps. The case is preserved verbatim in reading.ts's OutlineSummary header, packages/format/README.md:270, the reverted commit 525f737a, and the PR comment — nothing needs re-deriving. The human says keep the old shape or re-apply the revert's revert; either is one small commit."}
{"id":"four-way-site-spelling","parent":"infra","ord":"aO","title":"\"A place in the loaded set\" is spelled four ways in @olai/format #techdebt","done":"2026-08-15T17:30:07-04:00","desc":"Found during PR #170's refactor round (2026-08-14), both reviewers concurring: {file, line} spelled four ways in @olai/format. FIELDED FOR THE HUMAN 2026-08-15: PR #188 at 5f92af7e, CI 9/9 green — and the four turned out to be SIX (the PR found validate.ts's inline return type and a web component literally named Site hand-rolling the pair). One Site in node.ts now; the drift property proven both halves (a field added reddens all seven packages and lands on every carrier at once; before, it named only one of four); public surface 150→151 with Site deliberately reachable; behavior byte-identical, verified by printing export keys. Reviews: Grok + opencode, zero blocking, no seventh spelling found by either; two doc nits taken (the index header now carries no number beside an appendable list — it was wrong once before, #170's \"Eight\" — and confesses why). DECLINED ON THE RECORD, reviewers endorsing: no Order.Order&lt;Site&gt; for one-and-a-half consumers of different shapes. Merge is the human's word."}
{"id":"read-request-inference","parent":"infra","ord":"aP","title":"Read tools: TypeScript cannot infer their argument types, so every one spells them by hand #techdebt","done":"2026-08-17T22:27:25-04:00","desc":"Pre-existing, surfaced during PR #170 (2026-08-14): at ops/tools.ts:188 the request half's union Schema.Codec&lt;A,never,never,never&gt; | Schema.Top defeats inference of A, so every read tool hand-annotates its args. The answer half infers since #170. Either a tighter union or a second signature; small type-level work, no runtime change.","changed":"2026-08-17T22:27:25-04:00","custom":{"pr":"230"}}
{"id":"outlines-quadratic","parent":"infra","ord":"aQ","title":"outlines() walks O(files × nodes), three passes per file #techdebt","done":"2026-08-15T21:57:00-04:00","desc":"Pre-existing, surfaced during PR #170 (2026-08-14): ops/query.ts:333's outlines() is O(files × nodes) with three passes per file. Untouched by #170 (out of its scope, both reviewers concurred). Matters as vaults grow; one pass with per-file accumulation is the obvious shape, measured before/after when taken.","changed":"2026-08-15T21:57:01-04:00","custom":{"pr":"198"}}
{"id":"watcher-postboot-blind","parent":"bugs","ord":"aN","title":"A directory created after boot is invisible to the watcher until the 60s backstop","done":"2026-08-15T21:36:05-04:00","desc":"Found 2026-08-14 by the watcher-fd-cost investigation, in what ships today (bun 1.3.13's watcher, Linux): create a file in a post-boot subdirectory → NO EVENT; edit it → NO EVENT; only store.ts:157's 60s poll backstop keeps this from being a correctness bug. Measured shape-by-shape; the method and transcript are in docs/brainstorming/watcher-fd-cost.md. Fixed upstream by bun 1.3.14's watcher rewrite (the same bump that drops 1050 fds to 15) — so this node closes with whichever bump path the human rules on bun-watcher-bump, and exists so the blind spot is not forgotten if that ruling waits.","see":["bun-watcher-bump"],"changed":"2026-08-15T21:36:05-04:00","custom":{"pr":"196"}}
{"id":"bun-watcher-bump","parent":"infra","ord":"aR","title":"File-watching still costs one file descriptor per file — bun 1.3.14 fixed it; pick how we take the fix #human","todo":true,"desc":"The watcher-fd-cost investigation's ask (2026-08-14 night slate; full pricing in docs/brainstorming/watcher-fd-cost.md): the one-fd-per-file cost is bun 1.3.13's watcher, O(every file under the root) — .git and node_modules included (2055 fds on a git-shaped corpus, 1004 of them on files olai never reads). bun 1.3.14's rewrite (Linux/macOS/FreeBSD together) is per-directory: 1050 → 15 fds, and it also closes the post-boot-subdir blind spot (see watcher-postboot-blind). Nine mutation shapes verified across the bump: 1.3.14 delivers all nine, 1.3.13 seven; the one disagreement (create-by-rename source-vs-destination naming) costs olai nothing since disk.ts:80-85 drops the payload at the edge. bun test 1597/1597 on both binaries. OPTIONS, priced: (1) wait for nixpkgs — zero risk, unknown date, keeps cost + blind spot ← investigator's recommendation; (2) bun override now — buys the fix, forks the runtime, darwin hashes on faith; (3) hand-rolled per-directory watcher — halves the git case, still O(corpus), obsolete on the next bump, not recommended (and kqueue on Darwin makes it lossy); (4) polling — forbidden by the ruling (changes latency/granularity). The surface-mcp bridge is orthogonal: collapsing two stores halves the count but halving O(corpus) is still O(corpus).","changed":"2026-08-16T13:15:28-04:00"}
{"id":"shortcuts-modal-escape","parent":"viewing","ord":"aW","title":"The shortcuts modal has a scrim but ignores Escape","done":"2026-08-15T17:58:54-04:00","desc":"Found by PR #172's altitude pass and both its reviewers (2026-08-14): palette/Shortcuts.tsx:22 is a modal with a scrim and no Escape dismissal at all — the same missing-affordance class chat/Sessions.tsx was before #172 fixed it. Deliberately not folded into #172 (new affordance, not stack discipline; author + both reviewers concurred it is its own item). The fix joins topmost.ts's stack like every other dismissable.\n\nClosed 2026-08-15: #172 delivered the fix after all (commit 138c66af touches Shortcuts.tsx — it joins topmostWhileOpen and answers Escape on the window; the file's own header documents it). Human confirmed Escape dismisses the dialog. The \"kept as its own item\" plan was overtaken before this node was ever picked up.","changed":"2026-08-15T17:58:54-04:00"}
{"id":"tests-stray-debris","parent":"bugs","ord":"aO","title":"Stray probe files committed to packages/tests, one hardcoding a dead worktree path","done":"2026-08-15T17:47:07-04:00","desc":"Found by the sidebar-pierre-tree lane (2026-08-15): packages/tests/_h.ts and _probe.ts, agents' probe scripts accidentally committed, one hardcoding a dead worktree path. RESOLVED the same day by the human's ruling — no CI check, just hygiene: both files git-rm'd directly on master (718e312e) and the human's own \"no junk files\" line committed to HACKING.md (aa23635e). The check-that-would-catch-them idea lives on at stray-file-green-ci, unratified."}
{"id":"chat-model-stale","parent":"bugs","ord":"aP","title":"The chat header's model goes stale after /model","done":"2026-08-15T11:16:32-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-15 by the human with a screenshot. DIAGNOSED systemic: the picker's values are adapter ALIASES while the live system/init id is CANONICAL — labels.get() missed all five rows, so the stale-model warning fired on every change ever and the header answered raw or stale.\n\n**Now** (2026-08-15):\n- Kolu terminal: `485cd9bb`, branch `chat-model-stale`, PR #176 at head 46db630\n- Review round done: Grok 1 BLOCKING (constructed the lie — \"Opus (1M context)\" on a 200k session) + nits; opencode 2 nits. ALL ADDRESSED: the lane-borrowing inference is gone — an undated live id resolves, a dated pin shows raw, the opus lie is REFUSED (one scenario drives all three arms; the cost, paid deliberately: a common alias now shows as a raw id); the if(running) guard deleted on its own demerits (a refusal fallback re-picks via config_option_update — back to whichever-moved-last-wins, pinned by a reconfig scenario); video caption corrected; docs/chat.md documents the refusal. One deferral argued on the record: no test for first-live-id-disagrees-at-boot (needs a per-scenario spawn knob for one assertion; no baseline branch left to regress into)\n- Stage: CI at 46db630 running under the author's monitor → then FIELDED to the human; PR 2 (chat-token-usage) cuts only after his word on PR 1"}
{"id":"chat-token-usage","parent":"agents","ord":"aN","title":"Chat shows token usage, so /compact stops being a guess","done":"2026-08-15T12:31:51-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-15 by the human: the chat gave no view of context consumption, so /compact was a guess.\n\nMERGED 2026-08-15 (pre-approved by the human): PR #178, squashed at fcbb155f. The header draws the newest usage_update frame as a FRACTION (22k/1M) beside the model — a percentage was rejected on captured evidence: the adapter corrects the window mid-conversation (1000000 → 200000 on the wire), and a percentage read 2% on both sides of a fivefold drop in real headroom. Read in @olai/acp (any agent may send usage), newest frame wins — pinned MID-TURN with a hold verb after Grok caught the fake agent reproducing only half the correction, sabotage-verified both directions. cost arrives on the wire and is deliberately undrawn, with a unit test asserting the drop. Reviews: Grok (2 findings + the deeper recursion of its own fix) and opencode (1 blocking), all addressed with per-finding replies; the round also produced the pending-review-draft lesson (a review created without a submit event sits invisible — the orchestrator submitted it by API) and the detached-HEAD reviewer hazard. Deferred in the PR body: no compaction-threshold cue yet (wants the human's eye on a real session); a loaded conversation shows nothing until its first turn (session/load replays without a usage frame — documented where a person meets it)."}
{"id":"olai-extension","parent":"infra","ord":"aS","title":"Outlines are .olai files: the .jsonl extension retires","done":"2026-08-15T11:35:30-04:00","desc":"Ratified 2026-08-15 (docs/brainstorming/orchestrator.md Question 3): PURE RENAME, hard cutover, manual mv; this repo migrates in the same PR.\n\n**Now** (2026-08-15):\n- Kolu terminal: `939eb88c` (Claude Opus, YOLO), branch `olai-extension`, PR #177\n- Delivered: docs/roadmap.olai + docs/Archive.olai via git mv (byte-identical); OUTLINE_EXT carries the rule (code that decides reads it, prose that describes spells it out — a two-reviewer argument settled on the record); THE FENCE: tests/extension.test.ts sweeps every git ls-files path for .jsonl, string and filename, six named exemptions, all sabotage-proven — its own review round caught the sweep reading packages/ only and choking on gitignored screenshots (git ls-files fixed both)\n- Stage: #review — Grok (`5f4fbcb7`) and opencode (`990a07e9`), briefed to hunt spellings outside the fence's reach and to verify the e2e suite actually exercises .olai fixtures (the silent-no-op failure mode)\n- Parallel-lane fact: if #175 lands first, this branch re-runs post-rebase and the fence catches #175's .jsonl spellings — the fence working as designed\n- Merge: AUTONOMOUS once review clean + CI green (the human's ruling). OPERATIONAL NOTE at merge: every running olai server — the orchestrator's own ledger MCP included — reads .jsonl until restarted; the ledger goes quiet between merge and restart, and the human's other vaults migrate by hand (README carries the mv)\n- Deferrals filed: stem-strippers-twins, tree-sweep-fixture (which also carries the RCA-path human call)"}
{"id":"agents-outline","parent":"orchestrator-in-chat","ord":"a4","title":"The agents outline: who, as nodes — the launch command lives in a note","done":"2026-08-15T11:20:54-04:00","desc":"Step 1 toward orchestration-in-olai (docs/brainstorming/orchestrator.md): one node per agent, the exact launch command in its note; every launcher reads the node before spawning and a missing node refuses loudly — no machine default can decide the model again (the Fable burn's fix). Zero new olai code. Kolu's structured launch spec remains the later upgrade the notes would migrate into.\n\nDONE 2026-08-15: docs/agents.olai (born agents.jsonl — the create_outline call was handed a repo-shaped path and minted docs/docs, fixed by git mv; then PR #177's fence renamed the extension in flight). Three agents recorded: claude-opus (author), grok (reviewer, --always-approve), opencode (reviewer). Grok earned an authoring entry the same day (PR #180, solo, review waived)."}
{"id":"workflow-template","parent":"orchestrator-in-chat","ord":"a5","title":"Author the shared workflow outline: the step DAG every lane clones","todo":true,"desc":"The human's idea, 2026-08-15: the pipeline every lane walks (implement → refactor → review ×2 → address → CI → evidence → merge) stops being gist prose and becomes a TEMPLATE subtree — each step a todo node, dependencies as after edges, fan-out (both reviews before address) expressed natively. The app draws blocked-vs-ready for free. Existing grammar only: marks, after, add_node, descs — no new olai features.\n\nHOME RULED 2026-08-15: ONE SHARED PLACE — a single orchestration outline every repo's lanes clone from, so the process is defined once. Cross-repo progress ruled the same day: the lane's step-marks live in the orchestration outline; repo roadmaps keep their own items. A worked example is in docs/brainstorming/orchestrator.md.","after":["node-properties"]}
{"id":"lane-cloning","parent":"orchestrator-in-chat","ord":"a6","title":"Dispatch clones the DAG: a lane is the template copied, marks carry progress","todo":true,"desc":"The lanes half of the doc's organizing idea: dispatching a task clones the workflow template under the lane's node — real copies via add_node (not mirrors: each lane's steps carry their own marks) — and the PR's progress is marks flipping todo → doing → done, with terminal id, PR and evidence links in the step descs (props, once node-properties lands). The orchestrator's memory becomes the outline and survives anything.\n\nTEMPLATE RULE, learned from a live incident (2026-08-15: the orchestrator instructed a rebase while two reviewers were still reading the same worktree): a step that MUTATES the worktree sits `after` every step that READS it — readers fan out, writers wait. And the enforcement half: dispatch IS set_doing on the step, so set_doing REFUSING on a blocked node (today it only nudges) turns that discipline into a mechanism — the machine cannot instruct what the DAG forbids. That refusal is this node's one candidate code change; everything else is authoring and practice.","after":["agents-outline","workflow-template"]}
{"id":"olai-subscribes","parent":"orchestrator-in-chat","ord":"a7","title":"PR: olai subscribes to kolu's urgency/terminals and flips lane marks itself","todo":true,"desc":"The answer to oic-wake, as architecture rather than workaround: a server never sleeps between turns, so olai itself holds a real kolu-MCP subscription/stream and updates lane step-nodes live — agent-agnostic by construction, because the listener is not an LLM harness. This is what retires the terminal orchestrator's detached debrief watchers and the hand-flipped marks of lane-cloning's transition. Depends on the bridge-era plumbing (mcp-bridge) and on kolu's stream verbs behaving (the kolu-watch question noted in git history of the brainstorm).","after":["lane-cloning","mcp-bridge"]}
{"id":"node-properties","parent":"orchestrator-in-chat","ord":"a5V","title":"Org-style properties: facts stop living in desc strings #needs-brainstorm","done":"2026-08-15T17:46:15-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-15, the human's observation on the ledger's own habit: every \"**Now** (terminal, stage, PR)\" block is structured data smuggled into desc prose — unqueryable, eyeball-parsed — and lane-cloning is about to make it systemic.\n\nRULED 2026-08-15, in four rounds:\n- Freeform key→value; brainstorm before code; full web parity in the same PR (quiet drawer under the note, ••• menu edits).\n- Marks are not special — todo/done/date/see/after are LEGACY PROPERTIES; the record unifies into one props map, olai AUTO-MIGRATING each file's shape at startup. BUILT-IN KEYS ARE POLICED: set_prop refuses every system key toward its own verb (status→set_done and kin, date→set_date, after→set_after); set_prop owns only the keys olai does not read or write. A user key is promoted by gaining a READING, never by changing shape.\n- The stage is NEVER a property: a lane's stage is its cloned DAG's `doing` step.\n- Workflowy's pair joins as stamps: `created` (at capture) and `changed` (re-stamped by every write op) — no verb at all, nothing else may write them; absent on migrated nodes (the ledger does not invent a past) and appearing as nodes are touched.\n\nBRAINSTORM: [docs/brainstorming/properties.md](/doc/docs/brainstorming/properties.md) (example-first; UNCOMMITTED, awaiting the human's review), with the interactive drawer prototype linked inside: https://claude.ai/code/artifact/48a09a59-5079-44fe-b99a-7f3a5fe49c90 (insertion order, not alphabetical — a position on open question 1). STILL OPEN: whether search HITS carry props (one query = the lane board); whether the roadmap's existing Now blocks migrate in a sweep or rot away as lanes close."}
{"id":"stem-strippers-twins","parent":"infra","ord":"aT","title":"Two functions strip an outline filename's extension with opposite rules — unify them #techdebt #deferral","done":"2026-08-17T16:00:22-04:00","desc":"Found by PR #177 (2026-08-15), pre-existing: message.ts:148 and dates.ts:387 both strip an outline's suffix, with opposite stated arguments (spell the suffix vs find it). Unifying them needs its own argument. Related micro-fact from the same sweep: node.ts:253's isArchived allocates a string per call on a per-file-per-probe path.","changed":"2026-08-17T16:00:23-04:00","custom":{"from":"177","pr":"227"}}
{"id":"tree-sweep-fixture","parent":"infra","ord":"aU","title":"Three tests each walk the whole repo tree their own way — share one walker when a fourth appears #techdebt #deferral","done":"2026-08-17T16:00:24-04:00","desc":"Counted by PR #177 (2026-08-15): acp/manifest.test.ts:76, the claims sweeps, and now tests/extension.test.ts each walk the whole tracked tree their own way. The author's own rule: a shared fixture is the right move at four, not three — this node exists so the fourth sweep triggers it instead of becoming the fourth spelling. Also owed a human call, recorded here rather than lost: docs/RCA/2026-08-11-roadmap-stamp-reverts.md:64 still names docs/roadmap.jsonl inside a dated, explicitly-unbuilt proposal — correct the path or let the dated document stand as written.","changed":"2026-08-17T16:00:24-04:00","custom":{"from":"177","pr":"227"}}
{"id":"props-floor","parent":"node-properties","ord":"a0","title":"PR: properties, whole — the props map + migration, set_prop + search + drawer, created/changed stamps","done":"2026-08-15T17:46:07-04:00","desc":"RULED 2026-08-15 through three shapes, final: record unchanged + ONE open top-level key `custom` (string → text-or-list), no migration, top level closed and typo-refusing; set_prop writes only custom; prop:key / prop:key=value search; drawer with read-only system rows (id visible; zoomed always, tree rows once props exist — the human kept that at merge); created/changed stamped flat, excluded from changesOf.\n\nMERGED 2026-08-15 (the human's manual approval, the day's one reserved read): PR #179, admin-squashed at head 8d0f512e after the conflict merge with #188/#189/#190 — nix build was the sole gate at that head, a ONE-TIME exception on the human's explicit word (full odu CI stands for everything else). The journey's receipts: grok's same()-ignores-custom blocking fixed as a no-op guard (a same-value set_prop is a no-op everywhere); the ⌘Z double-removal on list values caught and fixed; the fresh double round found nothing further. Rulings at the gate: drawer stays only-when-props on tree rows; hits-carry-custom filed as its own PR node. 1711 unit / 612 e2e / CI green at the reviewed head 5b1933e."}
{"id":"doing-refuses-blocked","parent":"lane-cloning","ord":"a0","title":"PR: set_doing refuses on a blocked node — the DAG's law becomes a mechanism","done":"2026-08-15T14:22:10-04:00","desc":"Born from the 2026-08-15 incident (a rebase instructed while two reviewers still read the worktree): set_doing on a node whose after-targets are unfinished refuses in words instead of allow-with-nudge. The asymmetry is deliberate and argued in code: only the STARTING verb refuses; set_done keeps its nudge; set_todo untouched. Deliberate non-gates argued at plan.ts:876: set_after onto a started node records a true discovery; capture cannot arrive blocked (verified on the schema by review).\n\n**Now** (2026-08-15): Kolu terminal `bf2dbd48` (Claude Opus), branch `doing-refuses-blocked`, PR #181. Built and verified (1655 unit, 601 e2e); stage #review — Grok posted NITS ONLY (pin the two non-gates with planner cases; the click-away note; capture-can't-arrive-blocked verified on the schema); the round's sharpest question was the relocated undo scenario — what undo does restoring doing onto a now-blocked node wants its own assertion; VIDEO EVIDENCE ordered by the human. MERGE: PRE-APPROVED — autonomous once findings addressed + CI green at final head."}
{"id":"commit-op-staged-rename","parent":"bugs","ord":"aQ","title":"PR: pending and commit learn the index — a staged rename stops reading as a deletion","done":"2026-08-15T14:30:39-04:00","desc":"One hole, two faces, two live reproductions (2026-08-15): the pending/commit machinery reads the working tree against HEAD and never reads the INDEX, so hand-staged work — renames above all — is invisible or misclassified.\n\n- MCP face (orchestrator, fixing the docs/docs mint): with `git mv old new` staged, the commit op failed \"pathspec old did not match any files\" instead of committing the rename or refusing in its own words.\n- WEB face (the human's vault, post-.olai migration): `git mv`'d Archive.jsonl→Archive.olai and Tasks.jsonl→Tasks.olai staged; the commit panel showed \"Archive.jsonl deleted\" / \"Tasks.jsonl deleted\" — renames unread, the new .olai files invisible — and committing died with `fatal: pathspec '/home/srid/Vault/Archive.jsonl' did not match any files`, git's words surfaced raw because the sweep offered a path that exists only in HEAD.\n\nTHE HUMAN'S CONSTRAINT, binding on whoever takes this: REPRODUCE IN A TEST FIRST — a failing test holding a hand-staged rename against both the pending view and the commit path, before any fix lands. The fix reads the index (staged state) as part of pending, draws a rename as a rename, and never hands git a HEAD-only path. Related but separate: create_outline minted docs/docs silently when handed the served root's own name — a refusal wanting a sentence (kept here so the two path-shaped stumbles travel together)."}
{"id":"external-links-newtab","parent":"bugs","ord":"aR","title":"An external link opens in a new tab","done":"2026-08-15T12:31:56-04:00","desc":"Filed and fixed 2026-08-15 the same afternoon (the ledger was frozen at filing time — this node is the after-the-fact record, the freeze's one debt). The bug: an external link rendered in olai navigated the app tab away. MERGED as PR #180 (squash, review WAIVED by the human, Grok 4.6 solo author — its first authoring lane here): cross-origin http/https anchors gain target=_blank rel=noopener noreferrer at render time; /doc/ links, node navigation and anchors untouched, pinned in render.test.ts's own style. CI 9/9 required at fbf49eee. A grok-TUI quirk recorded for future dispatches: a paste during boot needs a second Enter once the footer says send-now."}
{"id":"regressions","ord":"zzVVV","title":"Regressions","desc":"Behavior that shipped worse than what it replaced, each node naming the offending PR. Ruled into existence 2026-08-15 by the human during the first live one."}
{"id":"stdio-retires","parent":"regressions","ord":"a0","title":"PR: the stdio MCP retires — agents dial the running server over HTTP","done":"2026-08-15T14:54:33-04:00","desc":"RATIFIED 2026-08-15 after the bridge regression's root-cause conversation: one brain, and the door to it is HTTP. The `olai mcp` subcommand is DELETED; .mcp.json points at the running server's /mcp route (the user runs `just run`); the ACP chat keeps its self-server MCP unchanged. Rulings sealed at the door: (a) LOOPBACK NEEDS NO TOKEN — requests from 127.0.0.1 skip auth on /mcp, the chat keeps its bearer tokens, same trust stdio had; (b) the #175 attach/unix-socket machinery goes WITH it — serveOverUnixSocket usage, the dial, OwnedSurfaceConnection wiring, dead code deleted not dormant (the kolu per-face-expose upstream work stays: the web face rides it); (c) stable per-repo port so the .mcp.json URL holds — just run binds it. Docs move with it: .mcp.json example, docs/running.md, the charter gains the precondition that unattended agent runs need the server up. This PR obsoleted three nodes filed hours earlier (bridge-no-fallback, socket-litter, mcp-process-leak — all archived with their stories). ASSIGNED: Grok, solo, review WAIVED by the human. Fielded to the human at green — merging changes his own session config (.mcp.json → http) and his run habits, so the coordination note rides the fielding."}
{"id":"brain-lock","parent":"regressions","ord":"a1","title":"PR: the brain locks its directory — a second olai over the same vault is refused","done":"2026-08-15T16:23:56-04:00","desc":"RATIFIED 2026-08-15, the enforcement half of one-brain. MERGED the same day (pre-approved on verified evidence): PR #186, packages/server/src/lock.ts — bun:ffi flock, zero dependencies (the lockfile-family libraries failed the human's own kill-9 requirement: their validity is file-existence plus a staleness window), keyed on realpath, pid in the file as DIAGNOSIS only. The orchestrator verified the evidence transcript by eye: second olai refused naming pid; symlinked spelling refused identically; kill -9 left the lock file on disk and the next boot served anyway — validity is the kernel's. Review: Grok + opencode, nothing blocking; the EINTR disagreement settled IN the docstring with both readings and a bounded retry; kimi's CodeQL HIGH fixed at the line (0o600). The audit's prediction came true: e2e workers were four olai over one shared fixture vault — per-worker copies now, which also closed a stray-write-into-tracked-fixtures hole.\n\nOPEN FOR THE HUMAN, reviewers split: LockUnavailable (cannot determine a holder) ships FAIL-CLOSED with no escape — kimi argued a narrow bypass for only that case (macOS is CI-unproven; a portability miss is a no-dial outage), grok argued a macOS miss should be a bugfix, not a knob. Both paths' tests already exist either way. Cosmetic residue: the VaultInUse refusal prints inside a raw Effect stack trace rather than as one clean sentence."}
{"id":"pr-body-wrap","parent":"infra","ord":"aV","title":"PR: repo markdown unwraps — one line per paragraph, 80 columns stays for source comments","done":"2026-08-15T14:47:49-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-15 from the human's root-cause exchange with a lane: agents hard-wrapped PR bodies at ~80 because the repo's own docs/*.md do — the habit inherits from the corpus. The human's fix targets the SOURCE: unwrap the repo's markdown prose (README, docs/*.md, brainstorming, package READMEs) to one line per paragraph — code blocks, tables, ASCII diagrams untouched, rendered output identical — and write the convention in HACKING.md (markdown prose never hard-wraps; ~80 stays for source comments). PR bodies themselves were explicitly scoped OUT (the human's correction mid-dispatch — the habit dies at the corpus, not by editing history).\n\n**Now** (2026-08-15): Grok solo (terminal `3e7619c0`, branch `pr-bodies-unwrapped`), review WAIVED, fielded at green. A mid-dispatch scope correction was sent and acknowledged-by-conduct; the whitespace-shaped diff rebases trivially under whichever sibling lands second."}
{"id":"quote-safe-selectors","parent":"infra","ord":"aW","title":"PR: e2e steps should build their data- attribute selectors through one quote-safe helper #techdebt #deferral","done":"2026-08-17T16:00:24-04:00","desc":"Filed at PR #182's close (2026-08-15), opencode's nit answered-not-fixed with the receipt on the record: Solid sets dynamic attributes via setAttribute so the DOM is escaped by construction — this is a test-selector concern only, and data-from was the fourth instance of one house idiom (data-path, data-file, data-node-id). Hardening only the newest made the suite inconsistent for zero coverage; the right change is one quote-safe helper across every data- step in packages/tests/step_definitions/.","changed":"2026-08-17T16:00:25-04:00","custom":{"from":"182","pr":"227"}}
{"id":"chat-diff-wrap","parent":"bugs","ord":"aU","title":"PR: the chat's diffs wrap — no horizontal scrollbar in the panel","done":"2026-08-15T15:32:13-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-15 by the human with a screenshot: an agent's file-edit diff in the ACP chat clipped long lines behind a horizontal scrollbar. MERGED the same day (pre-approved on verified evidence): PR #185, squashed at 61f22f0 — content wraps inside the box (grid + the min-w-0 chain on Panel/Entry/ToolFrame/Transcript/DropTarget, overflow-x-hidden on the transcript, both halves credited); gutter and +/- keep their column across wrapping add, remove and same rows, pinned with 700-character UNBROKEN tokens of each kind. Grok authored; opencode sole reviewer per the human (nothing blocking, 3 nits fixed); CI 9/9 at head; the orchestrator verified the before/after evidence by eye on laptop and phone before firing — the before shows the token escaping the box, the after wraps mid-token with the gutter intact."}
{"id":"html-preview","parent":"viewing","ord":"aX","title":"PR: .html joins the previewable types — through a registry future types register in once","done":"2026-08-15T17:46:07-04:00","desc":"Filed and dispatched 2026-08-15 (srid). FIELDED FOR HIS WORD the same day — PR #189, CI green at the post-Escape-fixes master merge. The registry is real (a future type is one entry; suffix-outside sweeps red), the sandbox held every fresh attack from both reviewers, and the nit round produced the PR's best fix: CSP asserted as a parsed DIRECTIVE-SET EQUALITY instead of substrings — the substring version would have waved `img-src data:` straight through; sabotage-proven by that very example. sandbox=\"\" now carries its warning ON the attribute line, where a diff-only reader lives.\n\nAWAITING SRID under his deferral rule, four deferrals posted in full on the PR: (1) no pictures in a .html — img-src 'none'; closing it means parsing foreign markup into /media rewrites, which is where a preview could start serving files nobody meant to serve; (2) every .html body resident for process lifetime, no cap — memory scales with vault content; (3) fixed 70dvh frame (sandboxed frames cannot report height); (4) .htm unclaimed, one line the day somebody has one. Plus two judgment calls flagged for his eye: the kind is `hypertext` in source but \"page\" in the one user-facing sentence; write_document/doc stay .md-only. Terminal `1ae9b6fc` rests until he rules."}
{"id":"html-images","parent":"viewing","ord":"aY","title":"PR: pictures inside a previewed .html — /media rewrites under the sealed frame's CSP","done":"2026-08-16T10:43:54-04:00","desc":"Deferral from PR #189 (2026-08-15): the sealed frame ships img-src 'none', so a saved page's pictures do not draw. Closing it means parsing foreign markup to rewrite src into /media/… — which is exactly where a preview could become a way of serving files nobody meant to serve, so the rewrite must be allowlist-shaped (vault-relative only, no traversal, no remote fetch). Security review non-negotiable when taken. Receipt: docs/format.md:120, sealed.ts:44.","changed":"2026-08-16T10:43:54-04:00","custom":{"pr":"201"}}
{"id":"html-memory","parent":"infra","ord":"aX","title":"PR: .html bodies stop living whole in the set — big pages want lazy bodies or a cap","done":"2026-08-16T12:55:44-04:00","desc":"Deferral from PR #189 (2026-08-15): every .html body is held in the loaded set for the process lifetime (ops/codec.ts:44, format/set.ts:53), and saved pages are megabytes with assets inlined — resident memory scales with vault content, no eviction, no cap. Named in docs/format.md:122. The fix direction: bodies loaded on read (the snapshot-scale precedent — documents already stopped riding the first frame once) or a size-gated claim that refuses in words.","changed":"2026-08-16T12:55:45-04:00","custom":{"pr":"204"}}
{"id":"html-frame-height","parent":"viewing","ord":"aZ","title":"PR: the sealed frame sizes to its page — 70dvh stops being a guess #techdebt","done":"2026-08-16T08:43:21-04:00","desc":"Deferral from PR #189 (2026-08-15): a script-less sandboxed frame cannot report content height, so Hypertext.tsx fixed it at 70dvh. RESOLVED by PR #197, and the old rule here — \"the empty sandbox attribute is not negotiable\" — is AMENDED by the human's ruling (2026-08-16, \"accepted and approved\"): the frame runs sandbox=\"allow-scripts\" with allow-same-origin forbidden by name and by e2e test, execution sealed to one hash-pinned measuring script under default-src 'none', and a load-count gate that re-seals the frame if a previewed page navigates it away (grok's review found that delta; it is closed and probe-tested). The line that is not negotiable now: allow-same-origin never joins the token list.","changed":"2026-08-16T08:43:21-04:00","custom":{"pr":"197"}}
{"id":"hits-carry-custom","parent":"orchestrator-in-chat","ord":"a8","title":"PR: search hits carry custom properties — the lane board becomes one query","done":"2026-08-15T20:19:52-04:00","desc":"Ruled 2026-08-15 at PR #179's merge gate: hits do NOT carry custom yet (read_node does); this node is the future PR. What it buys: \"every lane with pr=X\" / \"every node with source=Y\" as one search_nodes call — the orchestration board's native query. Shape: hits gain a custom field the way they carry see/after; the wire cost of big values wants a look (hits are many, values can be long — truncate or key-only?).","changed":"2026-08-15T20:19:52-04:00","custom":{"agent":"claude-opus","pr":"192","terminal":"48b46d55-9779-4fe7-b206-b89fe61fe87d"}}
{"id":"suite-load-flakes","parent":"bugs","ord":"aV","title":"Under load, the e2e suite drops 1–3 unrelated scenarios — never the same twice #e2e","done":"2026-08-15T23:47:22-04:00","desc":"Named independently by three lanes and two reviewers on 2026-08-15 (props, one-Site, caret, html-preview runs all saw it): a full or tag-filtered suite on a box hosting other worktrees' servers intermittently fails 1–3 UNRELATED scenarios (inbox capture, a date pill, server restart, palette, edge editing, ⌘Z pair) — 15s timeouts, always green in isolation and on re-run, never twice on the same scenario. Not any PR's diff; parallel workers vs machine load. Distinct from the FIXED Escape flake (#190, a real bug). The hunt starts with worker-count vs load instrumentation; the fix may be as dull as backoff on the 15s step timeout under load — but measured, per the house law.","changed":"2026-08-15T23:47:23-04:00","custom":{"pr":"199"}}
{"id":"chat-speaker-discern","parent":"bugs","ord":"aW","title":"PR: the human's chat messages are hard to tell from the agent's","done":"2026-08-15T18:38:28-04:00","desc":"Reported by the human 2026-08-15 with a screenshot (kept at /tmp/olai-dispatch/chat-discern-before.png): in the web chat panel, the human's own messages (\"scrim?\", \"I confirm Esc does dismiss…\") render nearly identically to the agent's — a faint box is the only cue, and it does not read as a speaker change. The fix is visual differentiation of the human's messages in the chat transcript. Dispatched to grok as AUTHOR at the human's explicit instruction, with no reviewer lane — a deliberate exception to agents.olai's reviewer-only contract for grok. The PR must include before/after visual evidence (screenshots), per the human's dispatch order.","created":"2026-08-15T18:00:51-04:00","changed":"2026-08-15T18:38:32-04:00","custom":{"agent":"grok","pr":"191"}}
{"id":"chat-steer-on-send","parent":"orchestrator-in-chat","ord":"a9","title":"PR: chat sends immediately — mid-turn messages steer the agent, the queue dies","done":"2026-08-15T20:30:07-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-15 from a live freeze: the orchestrator spawned a babysitter subagent, the ACP adapter holds a turn's settlement open while a spawned subagent lives (claude-agent-acp `turnAwaitingSubagents`/`Turn.deferredSettle`), and olai's queue-until-idle branch (`packages/chat/src/chat.ts` send(): `if (turn !== null) { queue.push(prompt) }`) sat on 4 user messages for the turn's whole lifetime — then cancel ran `dropQueue(\"cancelled\")` and destroyed them (no copy anywhere: the transcript is not persisted, the agent session is the persistence, and a queued message never reached the session).\n\nTHE FIX, learned from how Zed's composer never locks: send the prompt immediately, always. The adapter already supports a prompt arriving mid-turn — it STEERS: the message is injected into the running turn (`steeredSettle`, the pre-emption lanes) — so a user can talk to the agent while a subagent holds the turn. The queue and its drop are DELETED, not patched.\n\nCANCEL SEMANTICS fall out: with steer-on-send everything typed was already delivered, so cancel means stop-the-agent and nothing else. For a residual undelivered message (steer in flight at cancel), keep the row marked unsent with a resend affordance — the panel never silently destroys user words.","created":"2026-08-15T18:37:10-04:00","changed":"2026-08-15T20:30:07-04:00","custom":{"agent":"claude-opus","pr":"194","terminal":"333bf4f1-ed35-48ed-8d27-de46102f1b88"}}
{"id":"chat-subagent-lanes","parent":"orchestrator-in-chat","ord":"aA","title":"PR: subagent activity renders as its own lane in the chat panel","done":"2026-08-15T20:19:53-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-15, the human's ask from the freeze incident (it was one of the 4 dropped queued messages, resurrected): in-session subagent activity renders flat and unattributed — a tool call made by a spawned subagent looks exactly like the main agent's own, so subagents are invisible-yet-leaking. The ACP adapter already tags subagent traffic with `_meta.claudeCode.parentToolUseId` (claude-agent-acp, `liveBackgroundTasks`); olai drops the meta on the floor between `packages/chat/src/agent.ts` and the panel. Group/attribute subagent tool frames under the Agent call that spawned them.","created":"2026-08-15T18:37:16-04:00","changed":"2026-08-15T20:19:53-04:00","custom":{"agent":"claude-opus","pr":"193","terminal":"a5cb10bc-f92d-4659-a02d-c2c2fbb2f9bf"}}
{"id":"instructions-reconcile","parent":"orchestrator-in-chat","ord":"aB","title":"Update instructions.md to match how we now work #human","todo":true,"desc":"The instructions were written before the orchestrator/ folder existed (2026-08-15). Some sections now say things we no longer do. Each child below is one edit to make.","created":"2026-08-15T18:47:08-04:00","changed":"2026-08-15T19:06:48-04:00"}
{"id":"ir-now-section","parent":"instructions-reconcile","ord":"a0","title":"Replace the \"Now\" section — done, two small fixes left","done":"2026-08-15T19:03:40-04:00","desc":"Done: the section now points at lanes.olai. Two leftovers: (1) it says lane entries are \"mirrors\" — they aren't; each lane is a real copy of the template, and the `item` property links it back to the roadmap. (2) The deleted paragraph \"prose is not a ledger\" (anything worth tracking must be a node, not a sentence) was a good general rule — consider moving it to the Planning section instead of losing it.","created":"2026-08-15T18:47:08-04:00","changed":"2026-08-15T19:06:51-04:00"}
{"id":"ir-pipeline-prose","parent":"instructions-reconcile","ord":"a1","title":"The step-by-step PR process is written twice — keep it only in dag.olai","todo":true,"desc":"The sections \"Implementation guidelines\", \"Reviewing the PR\" and \"When human approves a PR\" describe the same pipeline that dag.olai now defines as nodes. Shorten them to \"the pipeline is the dag-pr template; a dispatched task walks it in lanes.olai\". Keep only what nodes can't hold: how to upload screenshots/videos as evidence, the footnote about checking CI properly, merging approved PRs in an order that avoids conflicts, and killing the terminal as a separate step after merge.","created":"2026-08-15T18:47:08-04:00","changed":"2026-08-15T19:06:57-04:00"}
{"id":"ir-babysit-split","parent":"instructions-reconcile","ord":"a2","title":"The watching instructions are wrong for the chat agent — split them","todo":true,"desc":"The doc says: watch every terminal with a background shell loop. Two problems. (1) An earlier ruling says the waits should be kolu MCP tool calls, not shell commands. (2) The chat agent must never watch at all: on 2026-08-15 it spawned a helper agent to watch grok, and the chat froze for the whole wait — a chat turn cannot end while a helper it spawned is still running. So the doc needs two sets of rules: a terminal orchestrator watches with MCP waits; the chat agent just dispatches and answers when poked. Both become unnecessary once olai itself watches the terminals (the olai-subscribes item).","created":"2026-08-15T18:47:08-04:00","changed":"2026-08-15T19:07:02-04:00"}
{"id":"ir-ops-only-rule","parent":"instructions-reconcile","ord":"a3","title":"Outline files were hand-written once — ruled: always use the MCP tools","done":"2026-08-15T19:06:07-04:00","desc":"The doc says outlines are only ever written through olai's MCP tools. I broke that: lanes.olai and dag.olai started as hand-written files. You ruled: no exceptions. dag.olai was deleted and recreated properly through the tools. lanes.olai stays as-is — it was hand-born but every edit since went through the tools, and recreating it would have disturbed lanes mid-work. Also seen: a brand-new file isn't noticed by the server until touched, and a deleted one lingers briefly — the first is a real (small) bug.","created":"2026-08-15T18:47:08-04:00","changed":"2026-08-15T19:07:06-04:00"}
{"id":"ir-agent-menu","parent":"instructions-reconcile","ord":"a4","title":"When asking which agent to use, list them from agents.olai","todo":true,"desc":"The doc hardcodes example names (\"eg: Grok, Codex\"). Instead, the choices should come from agents.olai: nodes with role=author for who writes, role=reviewer for who reviews. Then adding an agent is one node, not a doc edit.","created":"2026-08-15T18:47:08-04:00","changed":"2026-08-15T19:07:11-04:00"}
{"id":"ir-watcher-idle-safe","parent":"instructions-reconcile","ord":"a5","title":"The watch command returns instantly on an idle terminal — fix the recipe","todo":true,"desc":"Found live 2026-08-15: `kolu debrief` in a loop works while the agent is busy, but on an agent that is already sitting idle it returns the same screen immediately — loop it and it spins forever. Fix for idle terminals: first `kolu wait <id> --until working` (the agent started doing something), then the debrief loop. The doc's watching section should give both shapes.","created":"2026-08-15T18:56:02-04:00","changed":"2026-08-15T19:07:15-04:00"}
{"id":"ir-merge-master-rule","parent":"instructions-reconcile","ord":"a6","title":"When to merge master into a PR: \"always, just in case\" or \"only when needed\"?","todo":true,"desc":"Ruled 2026-08-15: never rebase a PR; merge master into it instead (faster). One thing left to pick: instructions.md says merge master before CI \"just in case\" (always), but CLAUDE.md says only when the PR has conflicts or CI needs newer code. The dag-pr template currently follows CLAUDE.md (only when needed). Make the two docs agree.","created":"2026-08-15T19:00:32-04:00","changed":"2026-08-15T19:07:17-04:00"}
{"id":"prop-value-bound","parent":"orchestrator-in-chat","ord":"aC","title":"PR: property values get a size limit","todo":true,"desc":"Ruled at PR #192's gate (2026-08-15): nothing stops a huge value being stored in a property. The PR argued ops/src/plan.ts is where a write-side bound belongs; nothing has hit this yet. Filed so it isn't folklore.","created":"2026-08-15T20:18:18-04:00"}
{"id":"search-props-ui","parent":"orchestrator-in-chat","ord":"aD","title":"PR: search results show properties, and say when a property is why a hit came back — with an e2e test","done":"2026-08-15T21:35:10-04:00","desc":"Ruled at PR #192's gate (2026-08-15): three of that PR's leftovers fold into ONE follow-up PR, done by the same agent. (1) The web app's search results still draw only title and place — a person can't see the properties the wire now carries. (2) A hit found by prop:agent=X can't say a property is why it matched — growing `matched` an arm changes what that field means, and this PR must rule that design point. (3) With something finally on screen, the e2e scenario that #192 rightly skipped becomes writable.","created":"2026-08-15T20:18:21-04:00","changed":"2026-08-15T21:35:10-04:00","custom":{"agent":"claude-opus","pr":"195","terminal":"48b46d55-9779-4fe7-b206-b89fe61fe87d"}}
{"id":"chat-subagent-asks","parent":"orchestrator-in-chat","ord":"aE","title":"PR: a subagent's permission form names who is asking","done":"2026-08-19T10:57:20-04:00","desc":"Ruled at PR #193's gate (2026-08-15): when a subagent triggers a permission question, the form is drawn in the main column and reads as the main agent's. The adapter already stamps parentToolUseId on session/request_permission and on a subagent's AskUserQuestion elicitation — ChatEntry.parent is tool-only and transcript.ask is never handed it. Grok's sharpening, recorded: the unattributed row also breaks the lane stretch, so the lane re-opens and names itself again (visible, not silent). The plumbing exists; recorded in packages/chat/README.md and docs/architecture.md.","created":"2026-08-15T20:18:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-19T10:57:29-04:00","custom":{"pr":"#255 merged 0fa068bb"}}
{"id":"unsent-discriminate","parent":"orchestrator-in-chat","ord":"aF","title":"PR: an unsent message says timed-out vs refused — AgentGone learns the difference","done":"2026-08-18T10:06:53-04:00","desc":"Ruled at PR #194's gate (2026-08-15): a message marked unsent after a steer timeout cannot be told apart from one refused by a dead agent — the timeout arm is an inference, and today only the honest comment at the site says so. The real fix discriminates answered-from-unanswered inside AgentGone so a timeout routes like a dead turn (notice, no resend button) while a refusal keeps its button. Redesigns ask, a shared primitive with five call sites — a real project, which is why #194 rightly deferred it.","created":"2026-08-15T20:18:27-04:00","changed":"2026-08-18T10:06:53-04:00"}
{"id":"siblings-of-quadratic","parent":"model-indices","ord":"Zz","title":"Slice 1: the index shape — byFile, mirrorsOf, edgesTo in Derived; every scan becomes a lookup #techdebt","done":"2026-08-16T12:30:00-04:00","desc":"Ruled 2026-08-16: docs/brainstorming/model-indices.md commits to direction C — patch the in-memory view when a file changes instead of rebuilding it three times per edit (the emanote shape). C lands in four shippable slices; this node is slice 1: give `Derived` real indexes — `byFile` (file → its nodes, killing every by-file scan: `siblingsOf`, `nodesOf`, the trash page, the per-candidate loops), plus the reverse maps the later patcher needs (`mirrorsOf`: target → mirrors resolving to it; `edgesTo`: target → sources whose after/blocks edges land on it) — built in the same single pass as today's tables, with every scan site converted to index reads. Still full-rebuild-per-edit at this slice. Slices 2–4 (one computation per edit; the patcher with its oracle property test; patches on the wire) become their own roadmap items when the human says dispatch. The library scan concluded: hand-rolled Maps, no dependency — d2ts/TanStack DB are the ideas' productized form but the wrong bite. Grok's #198 warning about a \"blind\" shared grouping helper is answered by putting the grouping inside `Derived`, always paired with its revision.","created":"2026-08-15T21:31:01-04:00","changed":"2026-08-16T12:30:01-04:00","custom":{"pr":"205"}}
{"id":"ir-evidence-recipe-reach","parent":"instructions-reconcile","ord":"a7","title":"Workers can't see the evidence-upload recipe — it lives in the orchestrator's file","todo":true,"desc":"Found live 2026-08-15 (night shift): PR #195's screenshots landed on catbox.moe because the GitHub-CDN upload recipe is written in docs/orchestrator/instructions.md — a file worker agents never read. Either the recipe moves to where workers look (HACKING.md or CLAUDE.md), or every dispatch briefing that asks for screenshots must carry the recipe inline. Until ruled, the orchestrator includes it in briefings.","created":"2026-08-15T21:33:15-04:00"}
{"id":"viewport-helper-promotion","parent":"infra","ord":"aZ","title":"viewport() joins OlaiWorld — nine step files spell it inline #techdebt","done":"2026-08-16T09:17:25-04:00","desc":"Filed from PR #197's deferral (2026-08-15): the e2e world's own convention says a helper belongs on OlaiWorld once three or four features ask; nine step files spell viewport() inline. Converting them was six files outside #197's diff. Mechanical.","created":"2026-08-15T21:43:03-04:00","changed":"2026-08-16T09:17:26-04:00","custom":{"pr":"200"}}
{"id":"drawnorsaid-extra-frame","parent":"infra","ord":"aa","title":"drawnOrSaid waits one extra frame on the happy path — grok's #199 follow-up #techdebt","done":"2026-08-16T09:17:27-04:00","desc":"Filed from grok's #199 review (2026-08-16): the one follow-up it would take — an extra frame on drawnOrSaid's happy path so the pointer and the keys agree about when the tab has the way back. Small, e2e-harness precision.","created":"2026-08-15T23:47:10-04:00","changed":"2026-08-16T09:17:27-04:00","custom":{"pr":"200"}}
{"id":"client-draw-crash","parent":"bugs","ord":"aX","title":"The web page crashes while drawing: \"Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'remove')\"","done":"2026-08-16T11:07:05-04:00","desc":"Seen 2026-08-16, mid-chat, on the production build (myolai.rooster-blues.ts.net). The whole page dies to the \"This page broke\" screen. The human says it started in the last ~24 hours of PRs — the window is #192 through #200 (of those, #192 hits-carry-custom, #195 search-props-ui and #197 html-frame-height touched client drawing; #200 was tests-only).\n\nThe throw is inside the UI framework's own list-reconciliation code, not olai's (frames `qf`/`ft`/`rn` in the minified bundle; `dev.js` in dev). \"reading 'remove'\" during reconciliation usually means the framework tried to take a DOM node out of a list it no longer holds — the classic causes are two rows sharing a key, or a keyed list being mutated while it draws.\n\nStack (prod bundle main-hesa85kj.js):\n\n```\nTypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'remove')\n    at qf (main-hesa85kj.js:1:22637)\n    at ft (main-hesa85kj.js:2:3293)\n    at Object.fn (main-hesa85kj.js:2:3019)\n    at rn (main-hesa85kj.js:1:7545)\n    at y (main-hesa85kj.js:1:7326)\n    at U (main-hesa85kj.js:1:1596)\n    at ft (main-hesa85kj.js:2:3011)\n    at Object.fn (main-hesa85kj.js:1:25143)\n    at rn (main-hesa85kj.js:1:7545)\n    at y (main-hesa85kj.js:1:7326)\n```\n\nNot yet reproduced on demand. A fix PR should first find a reproduction (what was on screen: the chat page, while messages streamed), then bisect the #192–#200 window if the reproduction doesn't point straight at the cause.","created":"2026-08-16T09:33:17-04:00","changed":"2026-08-16T11:07:05-04:00","custom":{"pr":"202"}}
{"id":"html-preview-relative-links","parent":"bugs","ord":"aY","title":"HTML preview: clicking a relative link to another .html doc does not navigate there #deferral","done":"2026-08-16T17:55:15-04:00","desc":"RULED by the human at #206's gate (2026-08-16), with the example that settles it: folder A holds foo.html and bar.html; foo links to bar; clicking that link must open **bar.html in Olai** — the app navigates to bar's own page (URL, sidebar selection, everything), the same place clicking bar.html in the sidebar lands. What #206 shipped is NOT this: the click swaps the bytes inside the preview frame (the sibling renders sealed, in-frame) while Olai's own page still says foo.html. That in-frame behavior was #206's honest scope; this node is the remaining distance.\n\nShape of the fix: the frame's navigation (or a click intercept) tells the host page which vault file it moved to, and the host navigates the app to that document — likely a postMessage from the injected prefix script or intercepting same-vault link clicks before the frame navigates. Must stay inside the seal's rules: only same-vault .html targets get this treatment; everything else keeps #206's behavior. Needs the e2e scenario the original filing asked for: click a relative link, assert Olai's own page is now bar.html's.\n\n(Original filing, for history: follow-up to #201, whose base made relative links 404 under /media/ — #206 then made them render in-frame; ruled insufficient.)","created":"2026-08-16T09:35:51-04:00","changed":"2026-08-16T17:55:20-04:00","custom":{"from":"201","pr":"209"}}
{"id":"noderefs-duplicate-target-crash","parent":"bugs","ord":"aZ","title":"NodeRefs: a repeated see/after target crashes the page the same way #202 fixed","done":"2026-08-16T12:05:05-04:00","desc":"Found by #202's author while hunting client-draw-crash, deliberately left out of that PR. NodeRefs.tsx draws see/after links with `<Key each={props.refs} by=\"id\">` — but an id names the *target*, not the row, so a hand-edited .olai repeating a target (`\"see\":[\"x\",\"x\",\"x\"]`), opened at /n/&lt;node&gt;, dies with the identical reconciler stack on the next store frame. Verified against a real server.\n\n**RULED by the human, 2026-08-16: draw ONE — deduplicate at the read.** Matches the write layer's set semantics (a re-add is a silent no-op in plan.ts): a repeated target means one link. The fix is a dedupe where the refs are read, keeping `by=\"id\"` honest, plus its own scenario (a hand-edited duplicate target draws one pill and the page survives). Reproduction and both options are written on PR #202.","created":"2026-08-16T10:21:13-04:00","changed":"2026-08-16T12:05:10-04:00","custom":{"pr":"203"}}
{"id":"seal-base-uri","parent":"viewing","ord":"aa","title":"Seal hardening: decide whether base-uri joins form-action #techdebt","done":"2026-08-16T14:40:15-04:00","desc":"Answered by #206, found while sweeping deferrals 2026-08-16: the preview rebuild added base-uri 'none' to the network policy — needed once the seal stopped winning by tree order — which is exactly the second bar this node asked to be decided. Originally #201's deferral 1.","created":"2026-08-16T10:35:10-04:00","changed":"2026-08-16T14:40:21-04:00","custom":{"pr":"206"}}
{"id":"preview-pictures-coverage-gaps","parent":"viewing","ord":"ab","title":"Preview pictures: the named test-coverage gaps #techdebt #deferral","done":"2026-08-17T16:00:26-04:00","desc":"#201's own report names these and both reviewers ruled them bounded and correctly deferred; filed so they are tracked rather than prose: (1) no e2e for `srcset`, `<picture><source>`, or CSS `background: url()` — they work by consequence of the shared base, unit-level agreement asserted; (2) a `loading=\"lazy\"` picture arriving after load keeps its pre-load height (clamped by CSS) — named in the seal's comment, untested; (3) no component-level unit test for the two-readings height logic (this client has no harness for Hypertext.tsx) — covered by the held-picture e2e and its negative control. The relative-links cost is NOT here — it is its own bug, `html-preview-relative-links`.","created":"2026-08-16T10:35:15-04:00","changed":"2026-08-17T16:00:26-04:00","custom":{"from":"201","pr":"227"}}
{"id":"ir-closing-keywords","parent":"instructions-reconcile","ord":"a8","title":"Orchestrator commit messages must not spell GitHub's closing keywords","todo":true,"desc":"Learned 2026-08-16 the hard way: the board commit \"grok closes #202's review pair\" landed on master and GitHub read `closes #202` as a closing keyword — the PR was auto-closed mid-lane (not merged; the author caught it, verified nothing was on master, and reopened it). Any lane can lose its PR to a sentence. Rule to add to instructions.md: in commits the orchestrator pushes to the default branch, never put \"close/closes/closed/fix/fixes/fixed/resolve/resolves/resolved\" directly before a PR/issue number — write \"grok finishes #202's review pair\" or put the number first.","created":"2026-08-16T10:48:10-04:00"}
{"id":"model-indices","parent":"infra","ord":"ab","title":"Model indices: patch the view, don't rebuild it (direction C)","desc":"The plan of record, ruled 2026-08-16: docs/brainstorming/model-indices.md. Four shippable slices, in order — each a PR with its own evidence. The end state: one small edit costs what it touched, not three whole-corpus rebuilds; the patcher speaks Surface's own collection-delta frame; from-scratch derive() stays as the oracle a property test holds the patcher to.","created":"2026-08-16T11:11:04-04:00"}
{"id":"model-indices-2","parent":"model-indices","ord":"a0","title":"Slice 2: compute the view once per edit — validate's Derived flows through to publish","done":"2026-08-16T15:51:51-04:00","desc":"Thread the validated view through to the publish step instead of recomputing it over the whole corpus (today's second rebuild). Old direction B, now C's plumbing. Open question 4 of the doc (serving derived facts through Surface's derived.collection reconciler) is a later refinement, not this slice.","after":["siblings-of-quadratic"],"created":"2026-08-16T11:11:04-04:00","changed":"2026-08-16T15:51:56-04:00","custom":{"pr":"208"}}
{"id":"model-indices-3","parent":"model-indices","ord":"a1","title":"Slice 3: the patcher — patch(derived, delta) in format/, held to the derive() oracle","done":"2026-08-17T08:45:18-04:00","desc":"One function beside derive() in format/, consuming Surface's collection-delta frame ({upserts, removes}). Local updates (byFile, children keys owned by the file, byId with duplicate-claimant promotion), then a dirty set from the reverse indexes (mirrorsOf, edgesTo) recomputing only touched status/blocked entries. Cycle checks re-run only when edges/mirrors changed. Copy-on-write: a patch returns a NEW Derived, sharing untouched maps. The safety line is the oracle property test — patch(derive(before), delta) deep-equals derive(after) over generated edit sequences, duplicate ids on purpose — and the patcher may bail to full derive() on hairy cases. Server uses it; the browser still rebuilds in this slice.","after":["model-indices-2"],"created":"2026-08-16T11:11:04-04:00","changed":"2026-08-17T08:45:19-04:00","custom":{"pr":"211"}}
{"id":"model-indices-4","parent":"model-indices","ord":"a2","title":"Slice 4: the browser joins — the derived memo folds delta frames through the same patcher","done":"2026-08-17T12:06:05-04:00","desc":"Swap web/src/client/outlines.ts's derived memo from flatten-and-derive() to the patcher over the delta frames the outlines collection already delivers. No wire change — outlines-as-collection paid that cost. Say the mixed per-entry revs contract out loud in the patcher's contract (same as today, not new unsoundness). If the generic fold-a-collection combinator proves itself olai-free here, offer it upstream to kolu Surface as a small PR (the precompress-upstream route) — consequence of the design, not a prerequisite.","after":["model-indices-3"],"created":"2026-08-16T11:11:04-04:00","changed":"2026-08-17T12:06:05-04:00","custom":{"pr":"223"}}
{"id":"chat-model-reverts-on-restart","parent":"bugs","ord":"aa","title":"Chat: /model choice does not survive an olai restart — the session comes back on sonnet","done":"2026-08-16T22:52:24-04:00","desc":"Reported 2026-08-16: switch the chat to fable with /model, restart olai (new deploy), and the restored chat runs sonnet again.\n\nROOT CAUSE — CONFIRMED 2026-08-16. The deploy container's ~/.claude/settings.json holds \"model\": \"sonnet\" (the human read it out). The pinned adapter (0.66.0, dist/acp-agent.js ~5470-5530) picks the model on session/load by priority: (1) ANTHROPIC_MODEL env, (2) settings.model, (3) the resumed transcript's live model, (4) default — and for a RESUMED session it deliberately RE-ASSERTS an env/settings pin with query.setModel (\"a resumed session lands on the transcript's model regardless of env/settings, so the override must be re-asserted\"). /model is handled inside the wrapped CLI, so the change lives only in the transcript — which the pin then clobbers on every restart.\n\nIMMEDIATE REMEDY (no code): delete the \"model\" line from the container's ~/.claude/settings.json. /model choices then survive restarts via the transcript, and fresh sessions start on the adapter's default.\n\nDURABLE FIX (if a settings pin should coexist with /model): olai remembers the session's chosen model itself and re-applies it after session/load through the model config option — or upstream: argue the transcript should outrank settings on resume. Note the adapter's floor either way: /model's change only becomes visible to olai one turn late (the next turn's init message), because nothing else on the wire carries it.","created":"2026-08-16T11:18:38-04:00","changed":"2026-08-16T22:52:25-04:00","custom":{"pr":"212"}}
{"id":"chat-subagent-live-face","parent":"bugs","ord":"ab","title":"Chat: a running subagent has no live face — lanes only fill as tool frames arrive","done":"2026-08-17T01:11:57-04:00","desc":"Reported 2026-08-16 with a screenshot: during a fan-out, the panel shows the Agent call as a plain pending dot and a lane of already-arrived tool rows — nothing says a subagent is running RIGHT NOW.\n\nIT IS possible in ACP — that part of the question has a clear answer. The pinned adapter streams a subagent's tool calls live, stamped with parentToolUseId in _meta.claudeCode (that stamp is exactly what #193's lanes draw), and pending/completed status rides each frame. The e2e suite proves the lane draws them (the_agent.feature 82-100).\n\nWhat is actually missing, in the client (chat/src/agent.ts ~420-470 and the lane drawing):\n1. The Agent frame itself gets no live treatment — a running Task shows the same \"·\" as anything pending: no spinner, no \"running · N calls so far\", no elapsed time.\n2. A subagent's TEXT and THINKING are never drawn. agent_message_chunk is emitted as \"said\" with the parent meta ignored — so between tool calls a working subagent produces nothing visible (and if the adapter forwards its narration, it would land in the MAIN agent's voice — check whether that interleaving is reachable, which would be its own defect).\n3. A subagent that has been spawned but has not made its first tool call has no lane at all — there is nothing to hang the lane on until a tool frame arrives.\n\nFIX SHAPE: give the Agent frame a running state (derived from its own tool_call status plus the presence of parent-attributed frames since), and decide whether a subagent's narration deserves a collapsed line in its lane (Claude Code's own UI shows \"N tool uses · last: ...\" — a compact live summary, not the full stream).","created":"2026-08-16T11:18:52-04:00","changed":"2026-08-17T01:11:58-04:00","custom":{"pr":"214"}}
{"id":"chat-at-completion","parent":"agents","ord":"aO","title":"Chat: typing @ completes file paths into the message","done":"2026-08-17T00:31:16-04:00","desc":"Requested 2026-08-16. In the chat input, typing `@` should open a completion of the served directory's files (the same set the sidebar knows: outlines and documents), filtered as you type, inserting the path into the message — the way Claude Code's own `@` works in the terminal. The chat client already has a completion precedent to lean on: the editor's tag autocomplete (input-widgets). The file list is already on the wire for free — the `outlines` collection's keys plus the `documents` collection's key set — so no new server surface should be needed. Decide alongside: whether a completed `@path` is plain text the agent reads, or carries the same attach semantics the `+` button has.","created":"2026-08-16T11:19:18-04:00","changed":"2026-08-17T00:31:17-04:00","custom":{"pr":"213"}}
{"id":"html-preview-scripts-dead","parent":"bugs","ord":"ac","title":"HTML preview: the page's own JavaScript does not run — ruled unacceptable; adopt kolu's iframe shape","done":"2026-08-16T13:58:26-04:00","desc":"Ruled 2026-08-16 by the human: a previewed .html whose scripts are dead is unacceptable. Today this is by design — the seal (#189, hardened in #197/#201) inlines the document via srcdoc with a CSP whose script-src allows exactly one hash, the seal's own measure script, so the file's scripts are blocked on purpose. That rule (#189's \"not negotiable\" line) is hereby overruled; both #197 reviewers had already asked for that line to be amended.\n\nHOW KOLU DOES IT (the reference, read 2026-08-16): kolu/packages/solid-fileview/src/renderers/iframe.tsx renders an iframe with src pointing at a file-serving route and sandbox=\"allow-scripts\" (WITHOUT allow-same-origin) — the page's JS runs in an opaque origin: it cannot read the host's cookies/localStorage, and CORS-gated fetch from inside is blocked. The route (kolu/packages/server/src/iframePreviewRoute.ts + packages/serve-dir) serves the raw bytes with content-type, guarded per-segment against traversal on the UN-normalized request target. A postMessage bridge is available via ref for host↔frame channels. The sandbox is the boundary; CSP hash-pinning is not part of it.\n\nTHE OLAI SHAPE OF THAT: serve the .html body by URL (extend the /media route family or a sibling /preview route, traversal-guarded like #201's mediaTarget but not picture-only), switch the frame from srcdoc to src, keep sandbox=\"allow-scripts\" alone. Open design points for the PR to argue:\n1. The privacy promise (\"a saved page cannot tell anybody else's server what you are reading\") — an opaque origin still loads remote no-cors embeds (img/script src). If the promise is to survive, the route can send a Content-Security-Policy HEADER permitting inline/own-origin scripts but no remote hosts — JS works, nothing leaves. If the human would rather drop the promise, that's a deferral to rule.\n2. The measure script (two-readings height, #197/#201): srcdoc injection dies with srcdoc. Options: inject into the served bytes server-side, or a postMessage bridge like kolu's, or read the frame's height another way.\n3. Relative links: with a real URL base, a relative link navigates INSIDE the frame to the sibling file — this may fix html-preview-relative-links for free; say so with a scenario if it does.\n4. Docs: format.md's seal story and #189's \"not negotiable\" sentence must be rewritten to the new rule.\n\nSecurity review non-negotiable on the PR, as with #197/#201.","created":"2026-08-16T11:22:02-04:00","changed":"2026-08-16T13:58:27-04:00","custom":{"pr":"206"}}
{"id":"just-e2e-node-leak","parent":"bugs","ord":"ad","title":"just e2e breaks before running a scenario: a system node leaks into the nix e2e shell","done":"2026-08-17T01:51:47-04:00","desc":"Found by #206's author, 2026-08-16, and reproduced on a clean checkout of master — not that PR's doing. `just e2e` fails before a single scenario runs: a system `node` leaks into `nix develop .#e2e` and cannot type-strip @kolu/surface's TypeScript sources under node_modules. Workaround the author used: run the suite through `bun ./node_modules/@cucumber/cucumber/bin/cucumber.js` directly. CI's e2e leg is unaffected (green). Fix direction: the e2e dev shell should pin/front its own node (or run cucumber under bun) so PATH's node never wins.","created":"2026-08-16T12:15:59-04:00","changed":"2026-08-17T01:51:48-04:00","custom":{"pr":"216"}}
{"id":"check-targets-index","parent":"model-indices","ord":"a3","title":"checkTargets still walks per node — convert to the reverse index without moving error order #techdebt #deferral","done":"2026-08-17T02:04:15-04:00","desc":"Resolved 2026-08-17 overnight: the conversion turned out already done by #208 (slice 2), held behind three pins two of which fail on the walk. PR #218 records it in model-indices.md with the measurement (walk 4.0ms vs index 0.40ms clean; 144ms vs 3.8ms with 200 unknown namings — didYouMean per-unknown-id, 25 calls vs 200, is the larger factor). Honest close: done by #208, measured by #218.","created":"2026-08-16T12:17:29-04:00","changed":"2026-08-17T02:04:19-04:00","custom":{"from":"205","pr":"218"}}
{"id":"html-unreadable-refused-state","parent":"infra","ord":"ac","title":"A page olai cannot read should say so on the page, not only in the log #deferral","todo":true,"desc":"#204's deferral 1. Under load-on-read, an unreadable .html (permissions, vanished mid-read) reports only to the server log — the reader sees the page with no body. Telling them needs a \"refused\" state on DocumentEntry (a wire change with its own argument) and a face for it. Still quieter than what it replaced, which failed the whole probe and bannered the whole directory.","created":"2026-08-16T12:49:32-04:00","changed":"2026-08-16T14:40:36-04:00","custom":{"from":"204"}}
{"id":"watch-set-per-connection","parent":"infra","ord":"ad","title":"Bodies stay live by refcount, not a 16-slot guess — the wire learns when a reader leaves","done":"2026-08-17T20:41:08-04:00","desc":"Reframed 2026-08-17 after reviewing kolu's watchers (was: \"per-connection LRU?\", #204's deferrals 3–5). The 16-path set in packages/server/src/bodies.ts is not file-watching (the store already watches the whole directory) — it decides which OPEN pages get their bodies re-read when a revision touches them. The bound exists only because the wire says when a reader OPENS a key and never says when the last one LEAVES (\"the framework owns subscription lifetime and does not publish it\" — bodies.ts), so eviction is a guess aged out of an LRU, and sixteen newer opens anywhere silence a still-visible page (stale, never wrong; reopen heals; documented in docs/format.md).\n\nKolu shows the honest shape (packages/integrations/io/src/refcounted-dir-watcher.ts, packages/integrations/git/src/working-tree-watcher.ts): refcounted subscription per key — first subscribe installs, every consumer shares, last unsubscribe tears down. No cap, no LRU, no per-connection question, because the leave signal EXISTS.\n\nThe fix, then: expose subscription lifetime on olai's wire (the \"keys-style member for who holds this key\" bodies.ts names as its own replacement), and refcount bodies on it — live for exactly the pages somebody has open, no magic number anywhere. Both documented format.md edges (the silenced page; the birth-announce gap in published.ts) get revisited by the same change. The original per-connection-LRU idea is superseded: it would shrink the blast radius while keeping the guess.","created":"2026-08-16T12:49:38-04:00","changed":"2026-08-17T20:41:08-04:00","custom":{"from":"204","pr":"229"}}
{"id":"done-over-open-work","parent":"bugs","ord":"ae","title":"A done parent must not be able to hide unfinished tasks — gate both doors","done":"2026-08-16T15:26:01-04:00","desc":"Ruled by the human 2026-08-16 after the model-indices slices vanished from the roadmap view.\n\nSYMPTOM: the Infrastructure root stored `done`, and done-hiding — correctly, per its own rule that a parent's mark is a claim about the whole branch — hid everything under it, including the four freshly-filed model-indices slices.\n\nROOT CAUSE (traced through git): nobody marked it over open children. PR #90 (\"Status is stored, never derived\", 2026-08-11 morning) ended derived parent status and, as its data migration, STAMPED the then-derived rollup into storage — that morning every child under Infrastructure happened to be done, so the derived \"done\" froze onto the root as a stored fact (`\"done\":\"2026-08-11\"`; f69d26d0 reformatted it to an instant the same afternoon). Then five days of new open children accumulated under a root that silently carried done. The mark was true when written and went stale by design: a stored fact over a growing branch, with no constraint at either write. Sweep of the other roots: `editor` and `website` also store done but currently hide nothing; `infra`'s mark was cleared in a40583f3.\n\nTHE RULING: this must be a preventable constraint, not a nudge. And the history shows one gate is not enough — the state is reachable through two doors:\n1. **set_done over open work**: marking a node done while its SUBTREE holds unfinished tasks (todo/doing — an unmarked bullet is not a task, per #90's own creed, and must not block) is REFUSED, naming the unfinished tasks — the same refusal shape set_doing already has for `after`.\n2. **open work arriving under a done ancestor** — the door this incident actually walked through: add_node with a todo/doing mark, set_todo/set_doing, or a move that lands an unfinished task under a done ancestor. Choose and argue: refuse it, or auto-undo the ancestor's done (louder and kinder to capture flows), but never let it land silently.\nWeb and MCP share the planner, so each gate is written once. Archived subtrees stay exempt as everywhere else. The nudge that set_done currently returns becomes the refusal's message body.","created":"2026-08-16T13:09:12-04:00","changed":"2026-08-16T15:26:01-04:00","custom":{"pr":"207"}}
{"id":"preview-body-not-shipped","parent":"viewing","ord":"ac","title":"A previewed page's body crosses the wire twice — the page only needs to know the file changed #deferral","done":"2026-08-17T03:57:34-04:00","desc":"#206's deferral 4, sharpened by its #204 merge. To preview a .html, the page's subscription makes the server read and ship the whole body over the websocket — and then the frame fetches the same bytes again from the /media route, which is the copy actually drawn. The websocket copy is only used to notice the file changed on disk. The fix is a head/revision-only member on the documents collection (the page learns \"it changed\" without the bytes) — and #204's own client notes such a change should be measured, not guessed at.","created":"2026-08-16T13:46:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-17T03:57:36-04:00","custom":{"from":"206","pr":"219"}}
{"id":"preview-postload-growth","parent":"viewing","ord":"ad","title":"A previewed page that grows after loading never gets a taller frame #deferral","todo":true,"desc":"#206's deferral 5 — found by three refactor checks and confirmed by a verifier; it predates #206, but running JavaScript is exactly what makes drawing late content common, so #206 makes it matter. A chart drawn from a fetch, a details element the reader opens, a late web font: the frame keeps its pre-growth height. The converged fix: instead of rationing height reports per width, accept reports but REJECT THE ECHO — a report within a pixel or two of the frame's own current height is a vh-page feeding its container back, and everything else is honest growth. A behavior change wanting its own scenario.","created":"2026-08-16T13:46:29-04:00","changed":"2026-08-16T14:40:52-04:00","custom":{"from":"206"}}
{"id":"chat-rewind","parent":"agents","ord":"aP","title":"Chat: no /rewind yet — the adapter has laid the plumbing but not shipped the verb","desc":"Asked by the human 2026-08-16. Investigated: (1) olai's chat has no rewind verb or UI; (2) the pinned adapter (claude-agent-acp 0.66.0) implements none either, but maintains a messageId→SDK-uuid map on every live and replayed message whose own comment says it exists \"so rewind/resume can translate a client-supplied id — Not read yet\" — upstream is preparing rewind but has not shipped it; (3) the CLI's own /rewind is an interactive terminal feature (the Esc-Esc checkpoint picker), not an SDK slash command, so nothing passes through the wire.\n\nTwo paths: WAIT for the adapter to ship its rewind verb, then surface it in the chat (watch item — check on each adapter pin bump); or TODAY, offer \"branch this conversation\" via the adapter's unstable_forkSession (ACP session/fork) — a new chat continuing from the full current state, covering the try-a-different-direction half of rewind with zero upstream work. The human has not asked for the branch button; it becomes a lane on their word.","created":"2026-08-16T14:03:07-04:00"}
{"id":"kolu-create-placement","parent":"infra","ord":"ae","title":"Upstream: kolu's create-terminal endpoint should demand explicit placement — toplevel or child-of #upstream","done":"2026-08-16T21:20:12-04:00","desc":"Proposed by the human 2026-08-16, after the orchestrator drifted into spawning reviewers as top-level terminals when the charter said splits — the default absorbed the mistake silently, and the canvas's hierarchy (who works for whom) quietly flattened.\n\nTHE DESIGN, simplified on the human's challenge (\"what interactive CLI?\"): humans create terminals through the kolu web canvas, not the CLI — the CLI's real audience is scripts and agents, exactly the callers that drift. So no TTY carve-out, no default anywhere: the padi create endpoint takes a REQUIRED placement — toplevel | childOf(terminalId) — and the CLI requires exactly one of --toplevel or --parent &lt;id&gt;, erroring otherwise. SCOPE ADDED by the human same day: the rule also covers kolu's MCP surface — the create-terminal tool kolu mcp offers agents takes the same required placement with no default, refusing a call that omits it; agents over MCP are exactly the script-shaped callers the rule exists for. Migration is one word per script. A kolu PR, dispatched (lane kolu-create-placement).","created":"2026-08-16T14:15:53-04:00","changed":"2026-08-16T21:20:12-04:00","custom":{"pr":"2173"}}
{"id":"quiet-outline","parent":"viewing","ord":"ae","title":"PR: The Quiet Outline — titles first, notes behind a ¶, tags inline but quiet","done":"2026-08-16T17:21:00-04:00","desc":"The decluttering proposal the human commissioned 2026-08-16 (artifact \"The Quiet Outline\", rev 2), dispatched with its two rulings baked in: tags stay INLINE in the title Workflowy-style (quieted by contrast, never boxed, never moved to a column), and nothing table-shaped anywhere. Six moves, one PR: (1) notes fold behind a small ¶ per row, opened by click/space, with a view-wide density switch Compact/Cozy/Open (Open = today's always-shown behavior, kept); (2) the outline column caps near 80 characters, opened notes wrap near 62; (3) tags render dim inline, brightening on hover/open; one hot fact may ride inline after the title (pr number on shipped work, fraction on a branch); (4) top-level sections get a heavier name with the fraction beside it and stick while their branch scrolls; (5) one fixed-width glyph column — bullet, todo, doing, done — with doing the only accented glyph; (6) done rows dim and a collapsed branch says \"+N done\" instead of hiding silently. The OPEN STATE has exactly three inline layers: title line, then a properties run (key value pairs, dot-separated, one dim wrapping line — a byline, not a form), then the note. PIPELINE NOTE: this lane carries an extra #human gate — the human reviews the design (screenshots) after implement, BEFORE any reviewer agent is spawned.","created":"2026-08-16T14:22:55-04:00","changed":"2026-08-16T17:21:05-04:00","custom":{"pr":"210"}}
{"id":"ir-item-shape","parent":"instructions-reconcile","ord":"a9","title":"Write into instructions.md: a roadmap item describes what to do (a PR), never a category","todo":true,"desc":"The human's rule, 2026-08-16, stated over the old \"Search &amp; filter\" node: a bare category name marked todo is meaningless — every roadmap item's title should say what a PR would do. Umbrella/category grouping belongs to parent bullets with no mark; work items are PR-shaped sentences. First application: the search umbrella became \"PR: the search grammar's deferred half — …\".","created":"2026-08-16T14:32:58-04:00"}
{"id":"search-relative-dates","parent":"search","ord":"a0","title":"PR: date: understands relative words — yesterday, last-week, this-month #deferral","done":"2026-08-17T02:24:41-04:00","desc":"Today date: takes only literal dates and ranges (2026-08, 2026-08-01..2026-08-14). Add the relative vocabulary — yesterday, last-week, this-month — resolved against the server's today, refused verbatim like every other bad operator value.","created":"2026-08-16T14:35:18-04:00","changed":"2026-08-17T02:24:42-04:00","custom":{"from":"168","pr":"217"}}
{"id":"search-quoting-or","parent":"search","ord":"a1","title":"PR: quoted phrases and OR join the search grammar #deferral","done":"2026-08-17T03:37:12-04:00","desc":"Today every word is AND-ed and matched alone; \"exact phrase\" quoting and an OR between terms are the two compositions people reach for first. One grammar change, both faces, refused verbatim on bad input like the rest.","created":"2026-08-16T14:35:27-04:00","changed":"2026-08-17T03:37:12-04:00","custom":{"from":"168","pr":"220"}}
{"id":"search-is-blocked","parent":"search","ord":"a2","title":"PR: is:blocked finds what is waiting on something #deferral","done":"2026-08-17T01:31:31-04:00","desc":"The format already derives blockedness (Derived.blocked, drawn on every row) — the operator just reads it. is:blocked = the node is waiting on at least one unfinished task; -is:blocked composes like every operator.","created":"2026-08-16T14:35:29-04:00","changed":"2026-08-17T01:31:32-04:00","custom":{"from":"168","pr":"215"}}
{"id":"search-everywhere","parent":"search","ord":"a3","title":"PR: the day, agenda and trash pages honor the filter box like the outline pages do #deferral","done":"2026-08-17T05:17:24-04:00","desc":"Filter-in-place works on outline pages; the day page, the agenda, and the trash ignore the same box. Same grammar, same ancestors-kept behavior, one filter meaning everywhere.","created":"2026-08-16T14:35:35-04:00","changed":"2026-08-17T05:17:25-04:00","custom":{"from":"168","pr":"221"}}
{"id":"search-saved","parent":"search","ord":"a4","title":"PR: saved searches — name a query, get it back with one click #deferral","todo":true,"desc":"A named query the reader can re-run — where it is stored (client preference vs a node in the vault) is the one design question to argue in the PR; the vault-node answer would make saved searches shareable and agent-visible for free.","created":"2026-08-16T14:35:37-04:00","changed":"2026-08-16T14:40:15-04:00","custom":{"from":"168"}}
{"id":"search-document-bodies","parent":"search","ord":"a5","title":"Document bodies are invisible to search — decide how the text inside .md and .html gets found #needs-brainstorm","todo":true,"desc":"Surfaced by the human's 2026-08-16 question (\"what does search NOT search?\") — the biggest answer: search covers a node's title, id, tags and note, and never the text inside a document file. A note pasted into a .md is unfindable. Directions to weigh (its own brainstorm before a PR): a word index maintained per document with add/remove on change (the MiniSearch shape model-indices.md footnoted); or emanote's answer — invalidate on any change, rebuild lazily on first search; interacts with #204 (bodies are now load-on-read, so indexing must not re-inflate memory) and with the parked semantic recall (which searches meaning, not words — different tool, same hole).","created":"2026-08-16T14:35:47-04:00"}
{"id":"kolu-preview-packages","parent":"viewing","ord":"af","title":"Weigh adopting kolu's solid-browser and solid-fileview where they actually fit #needs-brainstorm","todo":true,"desc":"The human's question at #209 (2026-08-16), answered from the packages' own source (kolu/packages/solid-browser, solid-fileview):\n\nWHAT THEY ARE. solid-browser is a pure history controller — createBrowser over an opaque location type (back/forward stack, forward-truncation, reactive) — plus resolveRelativePath (GitHub-style relative-ref resolution, framework-free) and back/forward mouse-button wiring. solid-fileview is a FileView dispatching to renderers: iframe (plain sandbox), image, markdown, pdf, video.\n\nTHE ASSESSMENT, piece by piece:\n- Do NOT swap the preview onto fileview's IframeRenderer — olai's is deliberately MORE: the injected measure prefix (auto-height), the network-only CSP header, walk-off custody, and #209's link-to-app bridge. Adopting the plain renderer would be a downgrade.\n- createBrowser is UNNECESSARY under the ruled design: #209 routes link clicks into olai's own pages, so the real browser's history gives back/forward for free. It becomes relevant only if an in-pane browse mode (navigating without moving the app) is ever wanted — which the human just ruled against.\n- resolveRelativePath IS worth adopting where olai resolves relative refs in rendered MARKDOWN prose (client/markdown/rewrite.ts) — one GitHub-rules resolver shared with kolu instead of a private spelling. Small, real.\n- fileview's image/pdf/video renderers are the honest opportunity: olai only gives faces to .md and .html today — a vault png/pdf/mp4 clicked in the sidebar has nowhere to go. If those types deserve pages, these renderers are the cheap way in.\n\nSo: no wholesale adoption; two targeted candidates (the relative-ref resolver; the media renderers if media files get faces). Awaits the human's pick.","created":"2026-08-16T14:39:30-04:00"}
{"id":"ir-deferral-tag","parent":"instructions-reconcile","ord":"aA","title":"Write into instructions.md: a deferral node wears #deferral and carries from=&lt;PR&gt; as a property","todo":true,"desc":"The human's rule, 2026-08-16: every roadmap node born as a PR's deferral is tagged #deferral in its title, with the deferring PR as the from property — so prop:from=168 lists one PR's leftovers and #deferral lists them all, and no note begins with \"Deferred on PR #N\" prose. Applied across the open roadmap the same day (twelve nodes); the sweep also found seal-base-uri already answered by #206 and closed it.","created":"2026-08-16T14:41:20-04:00"}
{"id":"preview-links-deferrals","parent":"viewing","ord":"ag","title":"What #209 left open, each its own call","done":"2026-08-16T17:55:40-04:00","created":"2026-08-16T15:54:20-04:00","changed":"2026-08-16T17:55:40-04:00"}
{"id":"preview-olai-links","parent":"preview-links-deferrals","ord":"a0","title":"A preview's link to a sibling .olai outline should open that outline in Olai #deferral","done":"2026-08-16T17:55:20-04:00","desc":"#209 claimed .html and .md siblings; an .olai link keeps #206's in-frame behavior because an outline is not a bodied kind — its page is /o/, its existence check a different list. A second route and membership question, same navigate-the-app shape as #209.","created":"2026-08-16T15:54:20-04:00","changed":"2026-08-16T17:55:24-04:00","custom":{"pr":"209"}}
{"id":"doc-page-anchors","parent":"preview-links-deferrals","ord":"a1","title":"The document page cannot land on an anchor — so fragment links to siblings stay in-frame #deferral","done":"2026-08-16T17:55:24-04:00","desc":"bar.html#section stays the frame's click because /doc/ has no anchor support; #209 backs the kept behavior with a scenario. The honest fix is anchor support on the document page, then fragment links can carry.","created":"2026-08-16T15:54:20-04:00","changed":"2026-08-16T17:55:29-04:00","custom":{"pr":"209"}}
{"id":"preview-open-channel-budget","parent":"preview-links-deferrals","ord":"a2","title":"The preview's open channel needs a budget — a page naming itself can spam history #deferral #human","done":"2026-08-16T17:55:34-04:00","desc":"Found by #209's refactor passes, confirmed by a verifier, deliberately not fixed: router.go pushes a history entry and scrolls to top per message, unbounded; a page naming itself never re-keys DocumentPage, so the frame survives to send again — the WALK_OFFS hazard class on a new channel. Three candidate rulings, each a behavior change: refuse the self-open; a budget that outlives one mount; replace over push. Needs the human's pick, then its own scenario.","created":"2026-08-16T15:54:20-04:00","changed":"2026-08-16T17:55:40-04:00","custom":{"from":"209","pr":"209"}}
{"id":"preview-dropped-click-silent","parent":"preview-links-deferrals","ord":"a3","title":"A dropped preview click tells the reader nothing #deferral","done":"2026-08-16T17:55:29-04:00","desc":"When the host refuses a posted path (not in membership — e.g. a .html under a pruned directory, servable yet unlistable), the click silently does nothing, against HACKING's never-silently-ignore rule. Surfacing it is a behavior change; the odd seam it rides has its own scenario in #209.","created":"2026-08-16T15:54:20-04:00","changed":"2026-08-16T17:55:34-04:00","custom":{"pr":"209"}}
{"id":"kolu-watch-next-cli","parent":"infra","ord":"af","title":"Upstream: the ring — padi nags every settled terminal by default until it is worked, snoozed with a reason, or killed #upstream","done":"2026-08-19T10:57:20-04:00","desc":"Rev 3 of docs/brainstorming/kolu-watch-cli.md — simplified on the human's ruling (rev 2's five-command watchdog was too much; we own both tools, no compat debt). The whole design: (1) the ring, default padi behavior — a terminal settled ≥60s is *ringing*, level-triggered, cleared only by work/death/snooze, so missed wakeups are structurally impossible; (2) one verb, `kolu snooze <id> --for 2h --reason \"…\"` — the only way to legitimize idleness, reason+expiry mandatory, expiry re-rings; (3) one face, `kolu ring --wait` — block until any terminal rings, print, exit; its exit is the orchestrator's wake. Same state via the urgency resource for MCP (lingerIds already half-exists). Deletes rev 2's subscriptions, ack cursors, expect/park, --settled-ms, screenTail-in-events — all of it was delivery machinery for a problem level-triggering doesn't have.","created":"2026-08-16T17:18:14-04:00","changed":"2026-08-19T10:57:31-04:00","custom":{"pr":"kolu #2181 merged by the human 2026-08-18; dogfooded live (snapshot/transition/nag all observed)"}}
{"id":"kolu-darwin-unit-fsevents","parent":"infra","ord":"aeV","title":"Upstream: darwin's unit lane is structurally red (git FSEvents churn tests), which silently skips every darwin daemon suite and blocks merges #upstream","done":"2026-08-17T00:40:26-04:00","desc":"Surfaced by #2173's author and ruled DISPATCH by the human 2026-08-16. Two coupled facts: (1) ci::unit@aarch64-darwin has 20+ consecutive reds on ci@petit from the git FSEvents churn tests (packages/integrations/), reproducing byte-identically on unmodified master; (2) ci/mod.just declares `daemon: install unit`, so while unit is red on darwin the daemon suites there are SKIPPED — darwin daemon coverage is not exercised at all, and the red required context forced #2173 to merge via --admin. Fix the churn tests for real if the failure mode allows (preferred), or skip them properly on darwin with the reasoning pinned — note PR #2172's branch already skips two of them darwin-only, so reconcile rather than collide. Success = darwin unit green on master, daemon suites running on darwin again.","created":"2026-08-16T21:21:04-04:00","changed":"2026-08-17T00:40:27-04:00","custom":{"pr":"2176"}}
{"id":"slice4-path-order-landmine","parent":"model-indices","ord":"a4","title":"Slice 4 must pick one path order — assemble's plain string compare vs the client's segment-wise sortByPath disagree on where an arriving file goes #deferral","todo":true,"desc":"Recorded by the slice-3 author (PR #211): the patcher places an arriving file by assemble's plain string path compare, while the web client's sortByPath sorts segment by segment. The two already disagree today — not new unsoundness — but slice 4 (the browser folding delta frames through the same patcher) has to pick one ordering or the patched browser view will disagree with the assembled one. Filed on the human's overnight draft ruling (\"file roadmap entries for those three\").","created":"2026-08-16T23:15:14-04:00","changed":"2026-08-16T23:15:23-04:00","custom":{"from":"211"}}
{"id":"patch-bench-harness","parent":"model-indices","ord":"a5","title":"Commit the 1,000-file patch-vs-rebuild benchmark as a just leg so the order-of-magnitude figure is reproducible #deferral","done":"2026-08-17T22:38:02-04:00","desc":"Open question 5 of docs/brainstorming/model-indices.md. The slice-3 author measured 2–3ms patched vs 25–40ms rebuilt on a 1,000-file / 21,000-record vault as a one-off; the harness is not in the tree, so README/architecture.md deliberately call it an unreproducible laptop sample. Commit the harness with a `just` leg. From PR #211; filed on the human's overnight draft ruling.","created":"2026-08-16T23:15:17-04:00","changed":"2026-08-17T22:38:02-04:00","custom":{"from":"211"}}
{"id":"patch-byid-overlay","parent":"model-indices","ord":"a6","title":"Replace the patcher's new Map(byId) clone — roughly half of every patch — with an overlay or persistent map #deferral","done":"2026-08-17T22:38:02-04:00","desc":"Open question 1 of docs/brainstorming/model-indices.md, now answered with a measurement by the slice-3 author: the byId clone is the dominant remaining cost (~half a patch). The lever is an overlay map or a persistent structure — take it when a bigger vault asks. From PR #211; filed on the human's overnight draft ruling.","created":"2026-08-16T23:15:20-04:00","changed":"2026-08-17T22:38:02-04:00","custom":{"from":"211"}}
{"id":"agenda-shows-trashed","parent":"bugs","ord":"af","title":"Agenda shows trashed nodes — archived dated work keeps its place on a page about what is owed","done":"2026-08-17T15:18:51-04:00","desc":"Reported by the human 2026-08-17; RULED same day: archived (trashed) nodes appear ONLY on the trash page — stripped from the agenda, day pages, and every other page. This reverses the three-archived-doors doctrine #221 documented (trash, day, agenda): trash becomes the sole door. The fix must sweep: agendaOf's keep and the \"archive keeps its place\" test (inverted, renamed); day-page collection; #221's Scope.archived (collapses to the trash page alone — the filter exception simplifies), docs/search.md and format README prose; is:archived in search remains the explicit way to reach archived nodes from anywhere. Dispatched to Opus.","created":"2026-08-17T08:51:48-04:00","changed":"2026-08-17T15:18:52-04:00","custom":{"pr":"226"}}
{"id":"second-pane","parent":"infra","ord":"ag","title":"The Second Pane — split views: N panes, each a full page, the layout in the URL","done":"2026-08-17T17:56:34-04:00","desc":"Proposed in the artifact \"The Second Pane\" (claude.ai/code/artifact/980a4438-9c7f-4e1b-b5b7-18a647c6b379), researched against Workflowy's native panes and the MultiFlow extension. Principles: a pane is a route (page + zoom + filter) rendering the full page component — no second-class panes; focus is explicit with a visible ring and all shortcuts/palette/filter acting on it; links deterministic (click = this pane, mod-click = open right); the whole layout serializes into the URL (share/restore/back-forward); drag between panes native (one store, no frames); saved layouts are outline nodes. Dispatched to grok 2026-08-17 on the human's word — draft PR, NO agent reviewers, the human reviews by running the branch.","created":"2026-08-17T10:39:40-04:00","changed":"2026-08-17T17:56:34-04:00","custom":{"pr":"225"}}
{"id":"olai-batch-verbs","parent":"infra","ord":"ah","title":"MCP writes stop costing one round-trip per fact — add_node children carry the whole record, a batch apply runs many ops in one atomic write, and update writes several fields of one node","done":"2026-08-17T12:39:00-04:00","desc":"From the 2026-08-17 orchestration retrospective: a lane spawn cost ~13 olai MCP calls (1 add_node + 9 set_after + 3 set_prop) because each fact is its own round-trip — and the agent's turn latency, not the server, is the cost. One PR, three verbs: (1) add_node's child shape gains after, see, and props so a captured subtree arrives with its edges and facts in the same atomic write; (2) apply — a list of ops executed in order, one validation, one write, one rev, all-or-nothing with the failing op named; (3) update {id, mark?, desc?, props?, after?} for the done+sha+note trio. Every refusal the single verbs make (shadowed keys, unknown ids with closest match, cycles named, done-over-unfinished from #207), the batch forms must make identically. Ruled: auto-merge on clean evidence.","created":"2026-08-17T10:44:55-04:00","changed":"2026-08-17T12:39:00-04:00","custom":{"pr":"224"}}
{"id":"kolu-one-shot-submit","parent":"infra","ord":"ai","title":"Upstream: dispatching a message to an agent terminal becomes one call — sendInput grows submit, and create grows message #upstream","todo":true,"desc":"PR #2177 CLOSED UNMERGED by the human's ruling 2026-08-18 after three rounds of live field testing (full reason on the PR): the core premise — output-quiet means prompt-ready — is false in the field. Round 1 lost a brief to a boot race; round 2's recognition fix was circular (recognition is downstream of the first message); round 3 fixed claude via foreground-process identity but grok still starved both one-shot paths — its idle TUI repaints on a ~0.8–1.5s cadence, so the 1500ms default settle never holds (settleMs:800 submits instantly; the manual trio works). POST-MORTEM FOR THE SUCCESSOR: keep the mid-turn refusal semantics, the 30s wire-pinned bound, the readiness-proof structure, and the commandName/adapter recognition work (branch one-shot-submit preserved); replace the quiet-conjunct with a signal that is agent-state-shaped — the adapter already knows idle; padi should ask it, not the byte stream. Related: kolu-watch-next-cli (PR #2181 in flight) encodes the same lesson for supervision.","created":"2026-08-17T10:44:59-04:00","changed":"2026-08-18T10:07:01-04:00"}
{"id":"faithful-addresses","parent":"infra","ord":"aj","title":"The address system is not faithful to the directory — a file's URL should be its path, and special pages should be marked as special #needs-brainstorm #human","todo":true,"desc":"Raised by the human 2026-08-17 while designing saved layouts, and parked for their own brainstorm before any dispatch.\\n\\nThe complaint: `/o/notes.olai` and `/doc/docs/x.md` invent structure the disk does not have — the file lives at `notes.olai`, so the address should be `/notes.olai`. And routes.ts convicts itself: it refuses a `/html/` prefix on the grounds that \\\"the suffix is what fileKind reads, and it is in the address either way\\\" — a prefix would be \\\"the same fact spelled twice, free to disagree with the name it carries\\\" — but `/o/` vs `/doc/` is that same duplication, since `.olai` vs `.md` already says which kind of page it is.\\n\\nThe human's sketch: file paths ARE the addresses (`/notes.olai`), and special/computed pages wear a marker that cannot collide with a filename — something like `/:today`, `/:agenda`, `/:trash`. And they want this done properly, as a SYSTEM, from the hickey/lowy standpoint rather than patched address by address: hickey — is the address complecting \\\"which file\\\" with \\\"which kind of page\\\" and \\\"which view of it\\\"? lowy — what volatility does the address layer encapsulate, and would its interface survive the axis it claims to own changing (new page kinds, new view parameters, nesting)?\\n\\nThe two obstacles a design must answer explicitly:\\n1. COLLISION — once file paths sit at the root, a file named `agenda` collides with the view. Hence the human's `:` marker (or another reserved shape) — decide it, don't leave it implicit.\\n2. THE NODE PERMALINK — `/n/<id>` deliberately spells ONLY an id, because ids survive renames and moves across files; a faithful `/notes.olai?id=hinge-notes` re-introduces a file that can disagree with reality after a move. Either the file is a hint the resolver repairs, or the id stays canonical with the path decorative, or something better. This is the real tension, not a detail.\\n\\nDownstream: saved layouts (and anything else that stores a route) must not be built until this is settled, or they bake in the current spelling. Also: #225 landed the pane-list address (the workspace URL) on top of the current scheme, so a change here touches that bijection and its tests.","created":"2026-08-17T18:02:56-04:00"}
{"id":"chat-at-nodes","parent":"infra","ord":"ak","title":"Chat: typing @ completes NODES too, not just file paths — hand the agent a task by name","done":"2026-08-17T21:09:49-04:00","desc":"Chosen by the human 2026-08-17 as the next feature. PR #213 gave the chat composer @-completion over FILE paths (server's own prune + kind registry, WeakMap fold, eight rows, tagOpensAt reused so @person habit does not collide). This is the other half: the same @ also completes NODES — you type @hinge and pick the node, so you can say \"look at @pick the hinges\" instead of pasting a path and hoping the agent finds the row. Reuses the completion machinery just built rather than inventing a second one.","created":"2026-08-17T18:05:22-04:00","changed":"2026-08-17T21:09:49-04:00","custom":{"pr":"228"}}
{"id":"mentions-index-one-sigil","parent":"infra","ord":"al","title":"The mention index files only @ — one taggedBy would also free the tag completion from walking the corpus #techdebt #deferral","done":"2026-08-18T21:55:00-04:00","desc":"Filed at PR #237 by the altitude pass. mentionedBy filed the @ namespace and dropped the sigil, but the fold it rode (titleParts) already yielded #topic parts and threw them away — so what the index really was is HALF of a reverse index of tags written in prose. SHIPPED in PR #249 as one taggedBy keyed by the WRITTEN form (@herbs and #herbs are two keys), re-keyed in one change across derive.ts, patch.ts's taggings(), the oracle's readable() and backlinksOf; mayHoldSigil went with it. web/src/client/complete/tags.ts now reads the index instead of scanning every node of the corpus per derivation. MEASURED, which is the condition this item was deferred under: two new `just bench` legs print both halves — the completion at 18.5ms walked against 0.47ms read (and 12.2ms for the title-only walk that was literally there, which answered 60 tags against the index's 1,052), and the corpus-wide fold at 6.5ms filing @ alone against 18.6ms filing both sigils. End to end, interleaved across two worktrees: a rebuild goes 32.8ms → 46.7ms and a one-file patch 0.67ms → 0.85ms, against ~18ms saved on every derivation while a tag is being typed. The verdict is a yes with the cost stated: the saving is per keystroke, the cost is per cold rebuild. It also WIDENED the completion — a tag written in a note is now offered, and the count is one vote per record rather than per occurrence.","created":"2026-08-18T06:05:00-04:00","custom":{"from":"237"}}
{"id":"patch-refile-fifth-spelling","parent":"infra","ord":"am","title":"patch.ts spells re-file-a-reverse-index five times — extract it when a sixth index appears #techdebt #deferral","todo":true,"desc":"Counted at PR #237 by the reuse pass. containment, namings, mentions, orderings and blockage each walk the same five steps: copy the before-map, build arriving, gather the keys the edit could have moved from arriving + outgoing, per key take the survivors (filtered by TOUCHED FILE, not by record) plus the arrivals in a promised order, then drop the key if it ends empty rather than storing an empty list. #237 added the third of those and designed the extraction rather than skipping it: a generic refiled<T> needs the entry Located projected back out (the indexes hold different entry shapes) and then has to hand back three things — the rewritten map, the keys that VANISHED, and whether a first-namer MOVED — of which only namings asks the last two, because only namedBy promises key order. That is two axes behind one contract with the caller that needs neither paying for both. The author rule this repo already follows (a shared fixture is the right move at four, not three) says the SIXTH index is what should force it; this node exists so that one does rather than becoming the sixth copy. The invariants are re-argued per index today and patch.test.ts oracle is the only thing holding the copies in step.","created":"2026-08-18T06:05:00-04:00","custom":{"from":"237"}}
{"id":"editor-verbs-2026-08-17","parent":"editor","ord":"aA","title":"Editor: three verbs the keyboard still lacks","done":"2026-08-19T11:02:12-04:00","created":"2026-08-17T21:27:03-04:00","changed":"2026-08-19T11:02:12-04:00"}
{"id":"move-to-picker","parent":"editor-verbs-2026-08-17","ord":"a0","title":"Move-to picker: fuzzy-search a new parent anywhere, from the keyboard","done":"2026-08-19T10:57:19-04:00","desc":"Approved overnight 2026-08-17. A keyboard dialog (Workflowy's ctrl-shift-M): invoke on the current node, fuzzy-search every node across all outlines as a destination, Enter moves the node (with its subtree) under the chosen parent. Reuses the search machinery; the move is the existing move op. Cross-file moves: refuse with a clear message if the op layer cannot express them (outlines are independent trees — archiving is what crosses files), and say so in the dialog rather than hiding the limit.","created":"2026-08-17T21:27:03-04:00","changed":"2026-08-19T10:57:29-04:00","custom":{"pr":"#245 merged 7d6706c1a"}}
{"id":"duplicate-subtree","parent":"editor-verbs-2026-08-17","ord":"a1","title":"Duplicate subtree: copy a node and everything under it in one action","done":"2026-08-18T10:06:54-04:00","desc":"Approved overnight 2026-08-17. One keyboard/menu action clones the current node and its whole subtree as a sibling below (fresh ids throughout, marks preserved or reset? — the author decides and documents; Workflowy preserves). Mirrors under the copied subtree: copy the placement, not the identity. For templates and repeated structures.","created":"2026-08-17T21:27:03-04:00","changed":"2026-08-18T10:06:54-04:00"}
{"id":"recurring-dates","parent":"editor-verbs-2026-08-17","ord":"a2","title":"Recurring dates: a repeat rule on dated nodes — done spawns the next occurrence","done":"2026-08-18T10:06:54-04:00","desc":"Approved overnight 2026-08-17. A dated node gains an optional repeat rule (every day / week on Mon / month / year — keep the grammar small and spelled in the file). Completing it stamps done AND creates the next occurrence at the next date (a fresh node, so the journal keeps the honest history of each completion). Agenda and daily pages show the next occurrence like any dated node. Web editing of the rule beside the date; MCP parity in the same PR.","created":"2026-08-17T21:27:03-04:00","changed":"2026-08-18T10:06:54-04:00"}
{"id":"viewing-2026-08-17","parent":"viewing","ord":"ah","title":"Viewing: the graph's missing reverse face, and panes learn to hand off","created":"2026-08-17T21:27:09-04:00"}
{"id":"backlinks","parent":"viewing-2026-08-17","ord":"a0","title":"Backlinks: a zoomed node shows what references it","done":"2026-08-18T05:20:00-04:00","desc":"Approved overnight 2026-08-17. Zoom into a node and see a \"referenced by\" section: every node whose see-edge points here, and every node whose title/note mentions this node by its @<id> spelling (#228). SHIPPED in PR #237. The reverse index is one new table in the derivation — mentionedBy, keyed by the WORD a title or note writes after an `@` rather than by a node, which is what lets a mint or a delete cost nothing (existence is asked at the READ, backlinks.ts); see-edges needed no new index, namedBy already carried them. Patched per changed file like the rest of the view, so the section follows the disk with nothing reloaded. RULED, and documented in docs/format.md's new References section: a mirror is NOT a reference (a placement is a view, not a claim — and read_node's mirrors already answers where a node is drawn), an after/blocks is not one either (the ordering graph, already drawn both ways on the page), and an archived referrer is left out (#226). Collapsed by default, each entry navigates, and read_node answers the identical reading as referencedBy so a person and an agent are told one thing about one id.","created":"2026-08-17T21:27:09-04:00","changed":"2026-08-18T05:20:00-04:00","custom":{"pr":"237"}}
{"id":"drag-across-panes","parent":"viewing-2026-08-17","ord":"a1","title":"Drag across panes: pick a node up in one pane, drop it in the other","done":"2026-08-18T09:30:00-04:00","desc":"Approved overnight 2026-08-17, SHIPPED in PR #238. With split panes merged (#225) a drag was still a fact about ONE page; it is now a fact about the WORKSPACE. Every editable page on screen joins a register as it mounts and leaves with itself (web/src/client/drag/fields.ts), a lift measures all of them, and which one the pointer is in is decided per move on X alone, because a split is columns (drag/aim.ts, unit-tested). Over a page of the carried file the arithmetic and the affordance are the ones that already shipped — the same planDrop, the same drop line with the same data-parent / data-after / data-depth — drawn over whichever pane the pointer is in. SAME FILE IN BOTH PANES reconciles for nothing: the write goes to the file and both trees redraw off the one store, so the row leaves one pane and arrives in the other on the same frame. DIFFERENT FILES is a REFUSAL raised at the AIM rather than at the write: the pane fills with the reason before the hand lets go, and the same sentence lands on the selection bar after it (drag/Refusal.tsx, one spelling carried on the aim). Sending the drop and quoting the ops layer would have been wrong twice — the line would have promised a landing the release could not keep, and a drop at the other file's TOP LEVEL names `parent: null`, which is not a refusable request at all but a legal move to the top of the row's own file, landing silently in a pane nobody was pointing at. TWO THINGS A SECOND PANE BROKE rather than extended, both fixed here: a Row.key is a chain from the roots of ITS page, so measureLines now takes the page to read (both row gestures scope to their own pane — unscoped, a drag in the left pane was planned against the right pane's boxes); and 'a branch is never offered a place inside itself' is asked of the RECORDS in a row's chain plus the page's zoom ancestry rather than of the key's prefix, because across two pages there is no shared path and the branch a pane is zoomed into is not drawn there at all. No new wire verb, no new op: a drop is still one `place` per row. Pinned by features/drag_across_panes.feature (5 scenarios) plus drag/aim.test.ts; the in-pane suite is unchanged and green. Evidence: two sections in packages/tests/evidence.ts.","created":"2026-08-17T21:27:09-04:00","changed":"2026-08-18T09:30:00-04:00","custom":{"pr":"238"}}
{"id":"menu-under-headers","parent":"bugs","ord":"ag","title":"The node context menu renders under section headers — the ••• menu is cut in two","todo":true,"desc":"Reported by the human with a screenshot, 2026-08-17 night: open a node's ••• context menu (Zoom in / Ask agent / Copy link / marks / Set date / Add property / Link to a node / … / Copy as text) and scroll-adjacent content paints OVER it — in the capture, the \"Viewing 46/48\" section header row cuts the menu in two (menu visible above and below the header; \"Move to Trash\" half-swallowed). Almost certainly a stacking bug: the sticky section header creates a stacking context above the menu popover. The menu must overlay everything while open. Screenshot (if readable): /run/user/1000/kolu-523140/scratch/d4b8e3f6-0562-4129-adcd-7cae70e60f86/image.png","created":"2026-08-17T21:37:35-04:00","changed":"2026-08-17T22:00:10-04:00","custom":{"pr":"233"}}
{"id":"agenda-spine","parent":"viewing","ord":"ai","title":"The agenda is a spine of time — now is a place on it, and the future recedes","done":"2026-08-18T11:52:08-04:00","desc":"Ruled by the human 2026-08-18, from a three-way design exploration: the `/agenda` page redraws as ONE continuous spine of time — no OVERDUE/TODAY/UPCOMING section boxes — because the flat sections gave every item equal claim on attention (an item 73 days out shouted as loudly as one due Monday), ISO dates demanded mental subtraction to feel urgency, and the repeated chrome (file heading + crumb per day) outweighed content ~4:1. The visual spec is the design canvas at https://claude.ai/code/artifact/3d13d467-18ed-49e2-8c95-64d9287683ef — it opens on the chosen **B · Spine** artboard, drawn in the chalk palette with the app's real row anatomy; A \"Fog\" and C \"Horizon\" on the same canvas are the rejected alternates (A's distance-fade ramp is folded INTO B).\n\n- **The spine**: a 2px vertical line in the left gutter running the page's height, its ink a gradient down the flow of time — alarm above the today point, accent at it, then ink → muted → rule fading to transparent in the far future. Each listed day is a 7px dot on the line, the dot's ink fading with distance (ink, muted, rule); overdue days sit ABOVE the today dot with alarm dots. TODAY is an 11px accent dot with a paper ring, drawn even when nothing is due on it (muted italic \"nothing due today\") — now is a place on the line, not a section that vanishes.\n- **Days say felt distance, not just dates**: \"Mon, Aug 24\" + muted \"in 6 days\"; overdue \"Yesterday · Mon, Aug 17\" in alarm ink with the pill reading \"1 day late\"; today \"TODAY · Tue, Aug 18\" in small caps accent. Rounding is fine (19 days → \"in 3 weeks\"); roughly: under two weeks in days, then weeks, past ~2 months in months-and-halves.\n- **Gaps are content**: the stretch between consecutive listed days draws a small italic muted label beside the spine (\"two quiet weeks\", \"seven quiet weeks\") and the vertical whitespace grows with the wait — log-ish, never linear, so a 52-day silence is visibly longer than a 2-day one without costing a screen.\n- **Rows fade with distance**: whole entries ramp ~1 → 0.78 → 0.74 → 0.5 opacity as they get further out.\n- **Chrome cut**: no per-day file heading (the \"Tasks.olai\" over every group goes); the ancestry trail is one muted line UNDER the row; the per-row date pill is dropped in the future half (the day label already says the date) and kept only where it adds a fact — the \"14:00\" time on a datetime. Overdue keeps DateBadge's alarm-tone semantics; occurrences still never amber, `done` still nowhere on the page.\n\nWhere the code goes, for whoever implements:\n\n- **Date arithmetic lives in @olai/format** (packages/format/src/agenda.ts, beside `isOverdue`/`agendaOf` — the one place already allowed to compare dates): weekday + relative-label phrasing, and the gap sizing between days. `DateBadge.tsx` stays verbatim-printing and dumb; its header states the verbatim principle and this is its sanctioned extension — keep the parsing out of components.\n- **AgendaPage.tsx** loses its three `Section`s for the spine layout. `DayGroups.tsx`/`DayNode.tsx` are SHARED with the day pages, which keep their current reading — branch by surface or give the agenda its own entry renderer over the same `situate`d `DayEntry` data.\n- **Tones are tokens, never hex**: alarm/accent/ink/muted/rule via the theme custom properties (color-mix where a fade is needed) so all fifteen palettes follow.\n- **The scenario contract moves**: `data-section`/`agendaSection` testids disappear or change meaning — reshape packages/tests scenarios in the same PR; `data-overdue`, `data-date`, `data-occasion` keep carrying their facts.\n- **The filter keeps working** (`keepingOwed` narrowing): removed rows leave, gap labels recompute from the days still drawn, a filtered-empty page says the filter's sentence (never \"Nothing is due.\"), and the sidebar's unnarrowed owed count (`agenda/owed.ts`) is untouched.\n- **Empty agenda** still says \"Nothing is due.\" and draws no spine — a lone today-dot on an empty page was not ruled on; default to none.\n- **Keyed rendering rules hold**: the `Key by=\"date\"` / `by=\"file\"` reasoning documented in AgendaPage.tsx and DayGroups.tsx applies to whatever replaces them.","created":"2026-08-18T10:04:21-04:00","changed":"2026-08-18T11:52:08-04:00"}
{"id":"menu-said-overlay","parent":"bugs","ord":"ah","title":"MenuSaid still paints under sticky headings — the one overlay #233 left behind #deferral","todo":true,"desc":"The findings the human waived when ruling #233 merged without further review (claude reviewer's comment 5322681125, reproduced on the branch): (1) menu/MenuSaid.tsx — the line carrying the ops layer's refusals — is still absolute…LAYER.row in the row, so a sticky section heading paints through it; the README's stacking table still names it a LAYER.row rider, contradicting overlay.ts's new doctrine. (2) The completions half of #233 fixed a second real bug with NO test (fails on master, passes there) and hand-rolls measuring where @kobalte/core/popper (already a dep, already anchoring the menu) gives flip/shift/autoUpdate free. Smaller: host.parentElement fails silently; no claims.test.ts sweep for the new layering rule.","created":"2026-08-18T10:07:11-04:00","custom":{"from":"233"}}
{"id":"filter-row-says-why","parent":"search","ord":"a6","title":"A filtered row says WHY it is drawn — highlight the hit, dim kept ancestors, excerpt a note-only match","done":"2026-08-19T10:57:19-04:00","desc":"Ruled with the human 2026-08-18, from one screenshot: a `#deferral` tag-click over roadmap.olai. Every number and every row was CORRECT, and the page was still confusing, because a filtered page never says why any given row is in front of the reader. Three cases, all present in that one screenshot:\n\n1. **A match does not show its needle.** `ir-deferral-tag`'s title merely *talks about* the tag (\"a deferral node wears #deferral\") yet matched exactly like a row that wears it as a label. The format cannot tell use from mention — a `#word` in a title IS a tag, deliberately — so the UI's job is not to distinguish them but to make the reason visible: show WHERE the query landed and the reader resolves the ambiguity themselves.\n2. **A kept ancestor looks like a match.** `instructions-reconcile` (\"Update instructions.md to match how we now work\") carries no `#deferral` anywhere; it was drawn only as the ancestor chain leading to `ir-deferral-tag`. Nothing marks it as context rather than result.\n3. **A note-only match shows no reason at all.** A row whose hit lives behind the ¶ (matched on `desc`) draws a title containing nothing the query said.\n\nTHE FIX, three parts, all view-time (nothing is stored):\n\n- **Highlight the matching substring in a matched row's title** — the classic search-needle treatment, applied to whichever title text (word, phrase, or tag pill) satisfied the query.\n- **Dim rows that are drawn but did not match** — kept ancestors. The distinction already exists in the model: membership in the `matched` id-set vs merely surviving `keeping`'s prune (`matchedIn` counts exactly this); the row just never draws it.\n- **For a row matched only in its note: draw a one-line excerpt** of the note around the hit, highlighted, under the title (or at minimum light up the ¶ itself). Explicitly NOT full auto-expansion of the note — ruled out because notes here run to ~1.5KB paragraphs (see `bun-watcher-bump`'s), the filter re-evaluates per keystroke so full notes would reflow the page violently while typing, and it would trample the reader's own open/closed state which would then need saving and restoring. A clamped excerpt is the same idea with bounded cost.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION POINTERS (as of master f35af476):\n\n- The matcher already knows which field carried a hit — `@olai/format`'s `filter.ts` scores per `SEARCH_FIELDS` (`title`/`id`/`tag`/`desc`) and `search_nodes` hits report `matched`. But the browser's filter path throws that away: `web/src/client/filter/narrowing.ts` reduces `matching(...)` to a `Set` of ids. The field (and the needle's position, or just the query's words for the row to re-find) has to ride along to the row — extend what `matching` hands back or derive per drawn row.\n- Title highlighting must hold on BOTH title paths, which are held to each other by test: the no-markdown fast path (`markdown/plain.ts` / `tags.ts`'s `taggedHtml`) and the HAST walk (`title.ts` / `styleTags`). `plain.test.ts` renders the same titles both ways; the highlight belongs under that same sweep.\n- Matching is case-folded substring over the folds in `filter.ts`'s `foldOf` — a tag is indexed bare AND as written — so highlight offsets must come from the same fold, not a fresh `indexOf` with different case rules.\n- The note excerpt can be plain text (escape, don't render markdown for one clipped line); `desc` is matched folded, so find the hit the same way.\n- The dim-vs-highlight styling wants the same narrowable-page condition tags already key on (`data-narrowable`, `styles.css`) so pages that take no filter change nothing.\n\nOut of scope here, filed separately if wanted: the count line's mixed denominators (\"8 of 57 — 17 more matches hidden as done\" reads as 8+17≠57; the 57 is page rows, the 17 is matches).","created":"2026-08-18T11:03:50-04:00","changed":"2026-08-19T10:57:28-04:00","custom":{"pr":"#240 merged 9ba1c6b5"}}
{"id":"md-live-preview-editor","parent":"editor","ord":"aB","title":"Markdown editing stops being a dumb text box — live-preview editing faithful to the bytes, autosave, vim mode in prefs","doing":true,"desc":"Ruled with the human 2026-08-18. The note editor and the document editor are plain textareas today; they become rich markdown editors — WITHOUT betraying the format's one law, \"the text on disk stays verbatim\" (docs/editing.md, `markdown/render.ts`'s header).\n\n**THE FAITHFULNESS RULING, which decides the whole architecture:** the markdown SOURCE stays the document model, and rich text is a *rendering layered onto it* — the Obsidian/Typora shape, where syntax markers (`**`, `#`, `[]()`) hide except around the caret and bold just looks bold until touched. Editors that parse markdown into their own document model and serialize back (ProseMirror, TipTap, Lexical, Milkdown) are RULED OUT by name: a round-trip serializer normalizes bytes the user never touched (`_em_`→`*em*`, escapes, list reflow), and under autosave that churn lands on every pause — phantom git diffs in the audit trail, spurious conflicts with concurrent agent edits, verbatim storage broken by construction. With source-as-model there is no serializer, so fidelity is perfect by construction and needs no tests to hold it.\n\n**THE STACK, and the human's rule about cost:** dependencies are not cost; handrolled code is. So the live-preview behavior itself comes from a package, not from decoration rules we write:\n\n- First choice: **`ink-mde`** — prebuilt CodeMirror 6 markdown editor with Obsidian-style live preview (marker-hiding, checkboxes, images), dark-appearance aware, written in Solid with a first-class `ink-mde/solid` component. Usage shape agreed:\n  ```tsx\n  <InkMde options={{ doc: node.desc, interface: { appearance: \"dark\", attribution: false }, hooks: { afterUpdate: (doc) => scheduleSave(doc) } }} />\n  ```\n- Fallback if ink-mde's rendering can't be lived with: raw `@codemirror/lang-markdown` + **`@retronav/ixora`** (à-la-carte CM6 live-preview extensions). Same document model either way, so switching later loses nothing.\n- Verify at implementation time how the chosen version accepts extra CM6 extensions (needed for the tag widget and vim below); the API detail is the implementer's, the intent is not.\n\n**Our handrolled surface is glue only:** a CM6 widget for `#tag` pills that delegates boundaries to `@olai/format`'s `titleParts` (the editor and the view must agree on what a tag is, same rule `markdown/tags.ts` follows), the autosave wiring, theming to match the panel, and the prefs toggle.\n\n**AUTOSAVE — no save button, no dirty flag:** debounced write on ~500ms idle plus a flush on blur. Every write is CONDITIONAL — `was` set to the text this editor last saved — so a concurrent agent edit refuses in the ops layer's own words and surfaces as a `SaidLine`, never a silent clobber; the drift-said-before-refusal machinery `DocEditor.tsx` already has stays. For documents this SUPERSEDES the standing ruling in `DocEditor.tsx`'s header (entered-by-verb, Save/Cancel, \"no idle commit\"): the human ruled autosave on 2026-08-18. Its half-a-sentence concern is answered by the debounce being idle-keyed, and its OVERWRITE second verb survives as the door out of a refused write. Rewrite that header to the new ruling rather than leaving the two to disagree.\n\n**Two surfaces, shipped incrementally in this order:**\n1. The NOTE editor (`edit/RowEditor.tsx`'s desc textarea — already commits on blur, so autosave is the smaller step there);\n2. The DOCUMENT editor (`document/DocEditor.tsx` — where the header rewrite above happens).\nIncremental richness is fine: markers-hidden for bold/italic/headings first, tables and nested lists can lag.\n\n**VIM MODE, enabled in prefs:** `@replit/codemirror-vim` as a CM6 extension, OFF by default, toggled by a stored preference following `layout/prefs.ts` / `preference.ts`'s `boolCodec` pattern (an `olai.editor.vim` key; surface the toggle wherever the done-preference lives in Prefs). One key conflict to settle deliberately: inside a vim-enabled editor, Esc belongs to vim (mode switch), not to the app's dismiss chord — the registry in `keys.ts` is where that gets said, not an ad-hoc guard.","created":"2026-08-18T11:16:47-04:00","changed":"2026-08-19T10:57:32-04:00","custom":{"parked":"2026-08-18 by the human's ruling (\"unhappy with markdown editor, revisit in distant future\") — draft PR #241 holds the work, branch + both reviews preserved, ten evidence images on the attachments CDN"}}
{"id":"md-second-class","ord":"a4","title":"Markdown is the second format everywhere content is reasoned about — the .olai/.md parity table #needs-brainstorm","todo":true,"desc":"Filed 2026-08-18 from the human's ask: sweep the repo and table where `.md` trails `.olai`. Three sweeps (format/store/ops/server/git; web/surface/chat/acp; docs/website) agree: markdown is first-class exactly where a file is treated AS A FILE — the kind registry (`format/src/kinds.ts:101-102`), load/watch/publish (`store`, `server/src/published.ts`), sidebar + new-file affordance, the one shared render pipeline, daily notes, `/media/` — and second-class in every layer that reasons about CONTENT.\n\n| Capability | `.olai` | `.md` | Parity? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Search | full grammar over title/id/tags/note (`format/src/filter.ts:67`) | body indexed nowhere; `/doc/` is the one page with no `?q=` (`routes.ts:302`); no `search_documents` | NO |\n| MCP tools | 25 of the 30 | 2 — `create_document` + whole-file `write_document`; no read, no list, no search; excluded from `apply` (`ops/src/batch.test.ts:745`) | NO |\n| `node.doc` | declared, validated (`checkDocs`), filterable (`has:doc`), copied by `duplicate_node` | writable by NOBODY — `CAPTURE` and `UpdateRequest` lack the field, no `set_doc`; and no read tool reports it (`Found` omits `doc`) | NO — broken in both directions |\n| Identity & edges | every node an id; `see`/`after`/mirrors/backlinks | path-only; cannot be an edge target; a `#tag` or `@mention` inside a body is indexed nowhere | NO |\n| Links | node→doc renders inline (`DocRef.tsx`) | doc→node href unclaimed by the router (`routes.ts:211` claims `/doc/` only, so it full-page-loads); a doc page shows no backlinks | PARTIAL — one direction only |\n| Editing | full outliner: drafts, undo stack, tag completion | `textarea` behind an Edit button, no autosave (`DocEditor.tsx`) — fix in flight, see-edge | NO — fix in flight |\n| ⌘K palette | node hits stream; `+` captures to Inbox | zero document rows: no open, no create, no capture (`palette/items.ts:98-173`) | NO |\n| Structure verbs | move/split/merge/duplicate + archive/inbox/trash | none — a `.md` cannot be moved, renamed, archived or captured | NO |\n| Git panel | node-level rows, verb-ful subject naming the title | path + status letter under \"Others\" (`format/src/committing.ts:179`); subject is `olai: 1 file — x.md` (`message.ts:132`) | PARTIAL — surveyed, but path-only |\n| Chat | `@` completes doc paths (parity!); node ids in agent prose are pressable; nodes armable as context | doc paths in prose and tool frames are inert; a document cannot be armed as context; a doc \"Wrote\" row has nothing to press | PARTIAL — completion yes, all else no |\n| Daily-note minting | — | `docDay` is web-only; MCP has no verb, `ops/src/tools.ts:626` asks the agent to redo the convention by hand | PARTIAL — web face only |\n| Tests | 1204 backend tests; `the_agent.feature` 80 scenarios | ~35 backend md tests (`store`: 0, `git`: 0); `an_external_agent.feature`: 2 document scenarios | NO |\n| Docs | — | README never says markdown; the MCP charter still says \"no file access\" (`server/src/mcp/face.ts`) and chat.md repeats the node-only tool list | NO |\n\nWhere `.md` is AHEAD, for fairness: table of contents (`Toc.tsx`), heading anchors in URLs, daily notes (a date-named `.md` IS the day), asset serving.\n\nREADING IT: part of this is ruled, not forgotten — \"a document is prose, and this grammar selects nodes\" is argued in three places (`routes.ts:110-114`, `markdown/tags.ts:66`, the website), and the commit panel's path-only row is defended in `commit/Others.tsx`. But the palette's silence, the missing `read_document`/`list_documents`, doc-side backlinks, the unwritable `node.doc`, inert chat references and the empty test files are argued NOWHERE — unreached, not decided. The structural root is one fact: a document has no addressable identity below the file, so everything keyed on ids excludes it by construction (the heading-anchor machinery in `routes.ts:59-74` + `markdown/outline.ts` is half a fix already). Brainstorm first (hence the tag): decide which rows are doctrine to keep and which are holes, then split PR-shaped children off this node. The two open items it overlaps ride as see-edges.","see":["search-document-bodies","md-live-preview-editor"],"created":"2026-08-18T18:51:48-04:00","changed":"2026-08-19T12:07:50-04:00","custom":{"brainstorm":"brainstorming/first-class-documents.md — the hierarchy (Document ⊃ OutlineDocument/MarkdownDocument), the [document]#[element] address grammar, derived md face now, frontmatter later (ruled with the human 2026-08-19)"}}
{"id":"filter-count-honesty","parent":"search","ord":"a7","title":"PR: the count line says what the page holds, and what is hidden as done #deferral","done":"2026-08-18T00:00:00-04:00","desc":"The deferral `filter-row-says-why` filed, taken: the count line's mixed denominators (\"8 of 57 — 17 more matches hidden as done\" read as 8+17≠57 — the 57 was page rows LEFT after the done preference, the 17 was matches it had taken away).\n\nThe line now says three truths, each only where it applies: how many rows MATCHED and are drawn, of how many the page HOLDS, and how many matched rows are NOT drawn and WHY. The denominator moved from what the preference left (`visible()`) to what the page holds (`all()`), so every part is counted inside one set and a reader who adds them up is not lied to. A part that is zero is not said; \"more\" is dropped when nothing is drawn, because `is:done` under a done-hiding reader draws nothing and \"no matches of 57 — 3 more matches hidden\" contradicts itself.\n\nThe wording is a function of three numbers (`web/src/client/filter/count.ts`) rather than a JSX binding, so it is unit-tested; the numbers stay `narrowing.ts`'s, off the same three readings the page is drawn from, never a recount that could disagree with the prune it counts.\n\nStill deferred, and named on the PR: the ⌘K palette and the header box draw eight hits of however many matched and say nothing about the rest — `SearchAnswer.total` has been on the wire, uncounted, since the matcher was split out.","created":"2026-08-18T00:00:00-04:00","changed":"2026-08-18T00:00:00-04:00"}
{"id":"count-line-honesty","parent":"search","ord":"a8","title":"The count line says what the page holds, and what is hidden as done","done":"2026-08-19T10:57:56-04:00","desc":"Filed and shipped in one overnight lane (2026-08-19, run #3). Under a filter the count line now says all three truths: matched-of-held (\"2 of 10\", the denominator measured before the done preference takes rows off) plus \"— N more matches hidden as done (Prefs)\". The wording is a pure function (web/src/client/filter/count.ts); the numbers come off the same sets the renderer draws from (filter/drawn.ts), never a recount. Findings round made all 31 count-line assertions exact reads (a substring pin would have passed with a spurious hidden clause — both reviewers caught it independently, fix sabotage-proven). Deferral filed at merge: the ⌘K palette and header search box draw at most 8 hits and say nothing about a ninth — SearchAnswer.total is on the wire and uncounted (see palette-count-cap).","created":"2026-08-19T10:57:56-04:00","custom":{"from":"overnight-3 (brief-born; captured retroactively)","pr":"#248 merged e419c67f"}}
{"id":"has-repeat-search","parent":"search","ord":"a9","title":"has:repeat joins the filter grammar","done":"2026-08-19T11:00:38-04:00","desc":"Shipped 2026-08-19 (overnight run #3). One row in HAS_FIELDS (format/src/filter.ts) — parsed, taught in the refusal, evaluated, negated with the grammar's own dash — reaching all five doors through the single matcher, costing what has:desc costs. Both reviewers do-not-object; grok's close: \"Ship it.\" docs/search.md carries the row and the has:date containment note.","created":"2026-08-19T11:00:38-04:00","custom":{"from":"overnight-3 (brief-born; captured retroactively)","pr":"#252 merged a38b0640"}}
{"id":"empty-trash","parent":"viewing","ord":"aj","title":"Empty the trash — the one write in olai that deletes","done":"2026-08-19T11:00:42-04:00","desc":"Shipped 2026-08-19 (overnight run #3). A new ops op `empty` naming EVERY archive the request empties in one op — the union holder-set holds by construction, list order means nothing, and a cross-pile cycle is not a special case (grok's objection, probe-verified, fixed at the root and withdrawn on re-verdict after he re-ran his own topology). Both faces in one change: MCP empty_trash + the web's Empty trash verb behind a confirmation counting over the SET and saying plainly that only git remembers. Optional count-shaped `was` checked per planning attempt. Refused while anything outside the Trash still names something in it.","created":"2026-08-19T11:00:42-04:00","custom":{"from":"overnight-3 (brief-born; captured retroactively)","pr":"#250 merged 557a268e"}}
{"id":"filter-highlight-cross-piece","parent":"search","ord":"aA","title":"A phrase across rendered markdown lights neither piece #deferral","todo":true,"desc":"The surviving half of #240's filter-highlight deferral. The other half — a needle inside a code span or link lit nothing — was closed by #254 (a848677d): the highlight walk now enters those subtrees while the tag SPLIT stays off (NO_TAGS_IN). What remains: a phrase spanning two rendered pieces of a title (across a **bold**, across a code-span boundary) selects the row but lights neither piece, because HAST text nodes carry no source offsets — the fix is offset-preserving rendering, a different change. #254 added a not.toContain pin so a later change cannot half-light it silently; docs/search.md names this as the one remaining corner.","created":"2026-08-19T11:01:07-04:00","custom":{"from":"#240, narrowed by #254"}}
{"id":"palette-count-cap","parent":"search","ord":"aB","title":"The palette and header search draw at most 8 hits and say nothing about a ninth #deferral","todo":true,"desc":"Filed at #248's merge (the count-line honesty PR): the filtered page's count line now tells all three truths, but the ⌘K palette and the header search box still cap at 8 drawn hits with no denominator — SearchAnswer.total is already on the wire and uncounted. Different surface, denominator and hider than the page's count line; also noted in docs/brainstorming/filter-in-place.md.","created":"2026-08-19T11:01:09-04:00","custom":{"from":"#248"}}
{"id":"parity-read-documents","parent":"md-second-class","ord":"a0","title":"MCP reads documents: list_documents + read_document","done":"2026-08-19T11:01:25-04:00","desc":"Closes the table's \"2 of 30 tools, no read, no list\" row's read half. An agent can now list the documents the directory serves and read one whole; a missing path refuses in the tools' own voice; face.ts's charter says the honest thing (\"nodes and whole documents, never bytes\") with a test pinning the retired \"no file access\" sentence. Flag argued in surface/ops.ts: read_document and the documents collection resource answer differently on purpose (request-shaped vs render-shaped).","created":"2026-08-19T11:01:25-04:00","custom":{"from":"overnight-3 (parity pick a)","pr":"#251 merged 191d7677"}}
{"id":"parity-palette-documents","parent":"md-second-class","ord":"a1","title":"A document is a row in the ⌘K box, matched by its name","done":"2026-08-19T11:01:28-04:00","desc":"Closes the table's \"zero document rows in the palette\" row. The palette and the header search box share one module: a served .md/.html matches by title/filename and opens its page; the served-path matcher moved to file/matching.ts beside the @ completion. The create row went NO — the palette has no create row for outlines either, so offering one to documents alone would invert the imbalance, not remove it (argued on the PR and in docs/search.md). Doctrine held: the node grammar reads no .md body.","created":"2026-08-19T11:01:28-04:00","custom":{"from":"overnight-3 (parity pick b)","pr":"#253 merged 4a9ae71b"}}
{"id":"pin-to-sidebar","parent":"viewing","ord":"ak","title":"Pin to sidebar: any node, document, or saved query, one click away","done":"2026-08-19T11:06:26-04:00","desc":"Approved 2026-08-18 evening; PR #246. The shelf is _olai/Pins.olai — the third by-name convention (pinsIn, same walk as inboxIn) — holding ordinary nodes whose titles are addresses: a bare address draws the live resolved face; a markdown link [label](address) draws the human's own words (ruled 2026-08-19; chip yes, pills no). No new op and no agent tool: read_subtree/add_node/move_node/archive_node ARE the agent face; the browser's one pin verb resolves to the same add. Shelf sits below the calendar, beside the tree (ruled). Grok's two objection defects (URIError on /n/% killing the sidebar — fixed at routeOf where the address bar hits it too; reorder in list-local coordinates) fixed with proven-red tests. Future: Archive/Inbox minting into _olai/ (see olai-convention-dir).","created":"2026-08-19T11:01:44-04:00","changed":"2026-08-19T11:06:26-04:00","custom":{"pr":"#246 merged e44e8ae3 (approved by the human 2026-08-19)"}}
{"id":"graph-view","parent":"viewing","ord":"al","title":"Graph view: the reference graph drawn as a navigable map","todo":true,"desc":"Approved 2026-08-18 evening; PR #247. /graph/<id> is a node's neighbourhood (1 hop, 2 on ?hops=), /graph the whole reference graph — derived live from the same indexes Referenced by reads (referencesOf beside backlinksOf, whole-corpus consistency test between them, re-verified against #249's re-keyed written-form index). Both edge kinds in written direction, files as spatial groupings, click navigates, hover names ancestry. Hand-test rounds: d3-zoom camera with +/−/Fit; labels declutter at every scale; canvas fills the viewport (the fixed ~550px box died); done nodes hidden by the app-wide done preference while the filter box still answers -is:done as a page question. Deliberate cost argued in graph.ts: the corpus page re-walks prose per store revision while open.","created":"2026-08-19T11:01:49-04:00","changed":"2026-08-19T12:07:51-04:00","custom":{"pr":"#247 parked as draft 2026-08-19 — the human ruled it unsatisfying: no .md/.olai parity, and bolting parity on would make a ball of mud; waits on the nodes/docs unification architecture (brainstorming doc)","unblocked-by":"brainstorming/first-class-documents.md — rebuilt on addresses at step 4 of its path"}}
{"id":"olai-convention-dir","parent":"infra","ord":"an","title":"_olai/ is where minted convention files land — Archive.olai and Inbox.olai follow Pins.olai","todo":true,"desc":"Ruled with #246: the shelf mints at _olai/Pins.olai (underscore, not dot — the store prunes dot-directories). The by-name resolution walks stay exactly as they are (whichever outline is CALLED the name, wherever it sits, shallowest-first), so existing files anywhere keep working; only the MINT location moves. #246 shaped the machinery as one mintedInto(name) spelling so this PR is one call each for Archive.olai and Inbox.olai, plus docs/format.md.","created":"2026-08-19T11:01:59-04:00","custom":{"from":"#246 (the human's ruling 2026-08-19)"}}
{"id":"dev-server-random-port","parent":"infra","ord":"ao","title":"A dev server never squats the production port — port 0 by default, fixed ports are a deploy's explicit word","todo":true,"desc":"Two field incidents in one night, same defect class: (1) a dev instance bound 127.0.0.1:7714 — the production port — so the orchestrator's olai MCP answered with the dev vault's outlines all evening and the ledger was wrongly believed unreachable; (2) the empty-trash lane's e2e dialed ANOTHER worktree's server through the shared /tmp/olai-dev convention and failed against a foreign bundle. Fix: dev and e2e servers bind port 0 (OS-assigned), print the port at startup and write it to a per-worktree file the harness reads back; a fixed --port is something only a deploy passes explicitly. Then a dev instance cannot squat production and two worktrees cannot dial each other.","created":"2026-08-19T11:02:04-04:00","custom":{"from":"2026-08-19 incident (the human's diagnosis)"}}
{"id":"fcd-pr1-addresses","parent":"md-second-class","ord":"a2","title":"Addresses: the [document]#[element] grammar, URL = / + address — PR 1 of first-class documents","doing":true,"desc":"Dispatched 2026-08-19 on the human's word, to Opus. Scope is the brainstorm's \"PR 1 — addresses\", self-contained: the branded primitives and the Address sum with parseAddress/printAddress and the bijection test; routes.ts rebuilt so a content URL is / + address; /o/ /doc/ /n/ deleted in the same diff; Pins.olai titles swept to the new spelling. Compat break blessed by the human.","created":"2026-08-19T12:43:48-04:00","custom":{"agent":"claude-opus","branch":"fcd-addresses","from":"brainstorming/first-class-documents.md","terminal":"6079d86f-0a99-44ae-b591-5c2d7f0a4a25"}}
{"id":"whats-new","parent":"viewing","ord":"am","title":"What's new: the app tells you what changed, every PR, linking the PR","todo":true,"desc":"Filed 2026-08-19 from the human's ask, ruled in discussion. A \"What's new\" item in the APP HEADER with an unread dot (last-seen entry held via createPreference — the receptacle is there for exactly this); clicking opens the list of entries, newest first, each linking its PR (github.com/juspay/olai/pull/N).\n\nRULED: entries are HAND-WRITTEN, ONE PER PR — a data file every PR must touch, ENFORCED BY A CI CHECK (a PR that touches no entry fails, with an explicit skip marker for the rare PR that truly has nothing user-facing). The generated-from-merge-subjects option was considered and declined: the human wants editorial control (prose beyond the title, screenshots possible), accepting the per-PR tax. Entries render through the existing markdown pipeline — olai-native, no second renderer.\n\nBrief-time details: the entry file's format and home; the CI check joins the odu pipeline; orchestrator briefs must tell every author \"write your What's new entry\" once this ships.","created":"2026-08-19T13:36:53-04:00","changed":"2026-08-19T13:36:57-04:00","custom":{"from":"human 2026-08-19"}}
